						<p>
							</p><p>SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES</p>
<p>Syllabus</p>
<p>LEARNING RESOURCES, INC., et al. <em>v</em>.
TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES, et al.</p>
<p>certiorari before judgment to the united
states court of appeals for the district of columbia circuit</p>
<p>No. 24=E2=80=931287.=E2=80=83Argued November 5, 2025=E2=80=94Decided
February 20, 2026[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/60=
7/24-1287/#F2" name=3D"T2" id=3D"T2">2</a>]*</p>
<p>The question presented is whether the
International Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA) authorizes the
President to impose tariffs. See 91Stat. 1626. Shortly after taking
office, President Trump sought to address two foreign threats: the
influx of illegal drugs from Canada, Mexico, and China,
Presidential Proclamation No. 10886, 90 Fed. Reg. 8327; Exec. Order
No. 14193, 90 Fed. Reg. 9113; Exec. Order No. 14194, 90 Fed. Reg.
9117; Exec. Order No. 14195, 90 Fed. Reg. 9121, and =E2=80=9Clarge and
persistent=E2=80=9D trade deficits, Exec. Order No. 14257, 90 Fed. Reg.
15041. The President determined that the drug influx had =E2=80=9Ccreated a
public health crisis,=E2=80=9D 90 Fed. Reg. 9113, and that the trade
deficits had =E2=80=9Cled to the hollowing out=E2=80=9D of the American
manufacturing base and =E2=80=9Cundermined critical supply chains,=E2=80=9D
<em>id</em>., at 15041. The President declared a national emergency
as to both threats, deeming them =E2=80=9Cunusual and extraordinary,=E2=80=
=9D and
invoked his authority under IEEPA to respond.</p>
<p>He imposed tariffs to deal with each threat.
As to the drug trafficking tariffs, the President imposed a 25%
duty on most Canadian and Mexican imports and a 10% duty on most
Chinese imports. <em>Id</em>., at 9114, 9118, 9122=E2=80=939123. As to the
trade deficit (=E2=80=9Creciprocal=E2=80=9D) tariffs, the President imposed=
 a duty
=E2=80=9Con all imports from all trading partners=E2=80=9D of at least 10%,=
 with
dozens of nations facing higher rates. <em>Id.</em>, at 15045, 15049.
Since imposing each set of tariffs, the President has issued
several increases, reductions, and other modifications.</p>
<p>Petitioners in <em>Learning Resources</em> and
respondents in <em>V.O.S. Selections</em> filed suit, alleging that
IEEPA does not authorize the reciprocal or drug trafficking
tariffs. The <em>Learning Resources</em> plaintiffs=E2=80=94two small
businesses=E2=80=94sued in the United States District Court for the
District of Columbia. That court denied the Government=E2=80=99s motion to
transfer the case to the United States Court of International Trade
(CIT) and granted the plaintiffs=E2=80=99 motion for a preliminary
injunction, concluding that IEEPA did not grant the President the
power to impose tariffs. The <em>V.O.S. Selections</em>
plaintiffs=E2=80=94five small businesses and 12 States=E2=80=94sued in the =
CIT.
That court granted summary judgment for the plaintiffs. And the
Federal Circuit, sitting en banc, affirmed in relevant part,
concluding that IEEPA=E2=80=99s grant of authority to =E2=80=9Cregulate . .=
 .
importation=E2=80=9D did not authorize the challenged tariffs, which =E2=80=
=9Care
unbounded in scope, amount, and duration.=E2=80=9D 149 F. 4th 1312, 1338.
The Government filed a petition for certiorari in <em>V.O.S.
Selections</em>, and the <em>Learning Resources</em> plaintiffs filed
a petition for certiorari before judgment. The Court granted the
petitions and consolidated the cases.</p>
<p><em>Held</em>:&nbsp;IEEPA does not authorize the
President to impose tariffs. The judgment in No. 24=E2=80=931287 is
vacated, and the case is remanded with instructions to dismiss for
lack of jurisdiction; the judgment in No. 25=E2=80=93250 is affirmed.</p>
<p>No. 24=E2=80=931287, 784 F.&nbsp;Supp.&nbsp;3d 209,
vacated and remanded; No. 25=E2=80=93250, 149 F.&nbsp;4th 1312,
affirmed.</p>
<p>The Chief Justice delivered the opinion of the
Court with respect to Parts I and II=E2=80=93A=E2=80=931:</p>
<p>Article I, Section 8, of the Constitution
specifies that =E2=80=9CThe Congress shall have Power To lay and collect
Taxes, Duties, Imposts and Excises.=E2=80=9D The Framers recognized the
unique importance of this taxing power=E2=80=94a power which =E2=80=9Cvery
clear[ly]=E2=80=9D includes the power to impose tariffs. <em>Gibbons</em> v=
.
<em>Ogden</em>, 9 Wheat. 1, 201. And they gave Congress =E2=80=9Calone
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. access to the pockets of the people.=E2=80=9D The
Federalist No. 48, p.&nbsp;310 (J. Madison). The Framers did not
vest any part of the taxing power in the Executive Branch. See
<em>Nicol</em> v. <em>Ames</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://=
supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/173/509/">173 U.S.
509</a>, 515.</p>
<p>The Government thus concedes that the
President enjoys no inherent authority to impose tariffs during
peacetime. It instead relies exclusively on IEEPA to defend the
challenged tariffs. It reads the words =E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D and =E2=
=80=9Cimportation=E2=80=9D
to effect a sweeping delegation of Congress=E2=80=99s power to set tariff
policy=E2=80=94authorizing the President to impose tariffs of unlimited
amount and duration, on any product from any country. 50
U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71702(a)(1)(B). Pp. 5=E2=80=937.</p>
<p>The Chief Justice, joined by Justice Gorsuch
and Justice Barrett, concluded in Part II=E2=80=93A=E2=80=932:</p>
<p>The Court has long expressed =E2=80=9Creluctan[ce] to
read into ambiguous statutory text=E2=80=9D extraordinary delegations of
Congress=E2=80=99s powers. <em>West Virginia</em> v. <em>EPA</em>, 597 U.S.
697, 723 (quoting <em>Utility Air Regulatory Group</em> v.
<em>EPA</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/=
cases/federal/us/573/302/">573 U.S.
302</a>, 324). In several cases described as involving =E2=80=9Cmajor
questions,=E2=80=9D the Court has reasoned that =E2=80=9Cboth separation of=
 powers
principles and a practical understanding of legislative intent=E2=80=9D
suggest Congress would not have delegated =E2=80=9Chighly consequential
power=E2=80=9D through ambiguous language. <em>Id</em>., at 723=E2=80=93724=
. These
considerations apply with particular force where, as here, the
purported delegation involves the core congressional power of the
purse. Congressional practice confirms as much. When Congress has
delegated its tariff powers, it has done so in explicit terms and
subject to strict limits.</p>
<p>Against that backdrop of clear and limited
delegations, the Government reads IEEPA to give the President power
to unilaterally impose unbounded tariffs and change them at will.
That view would represent a transformative expansion of the
President=E2=80=99s authority over tariff policy. It is also telling that
in IEEPA=E2=80=99s half century of existence, no President has invoked the
statute to impose <em>any</em> tariffs, let alone tariffs of this
magnitude and scope. That =E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98lack of historical preced=
ent,=E2=80=99
coupled with the breadth of authority=E2=80=9D that the President now
claims, suggests that the tariffs extend beyond the President=E2=80=99s
=E2=80=9Clegitimate reach.=E2=80=9D <em>National Federation of Independent
Business</em> v. <em>OSHA</em>, 595 U.S. 109, 119 (quoting <em>Free
Enterprise Fund</em> v. <em>Public Company Accounting Oversight
Bd.</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/case=
s/federal/us/561/477/">561 U.S.
477</a>, 505). The =E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98economic and political
significance=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D of the authority the President has ass=
erted
likewise =E2=80=9Cprovide[s] a =E2=80=98reason to hesitate before concludin=
g that
Congress=E2=80=99 meant to confer such authority.=E2=80=9D <em>West Virgini=
a</em>,
597 U.&nbsp;S., at 721 (quoting <em>FDA</em> v. <em>Brown &amp;
Williamson Tobacco Corp.</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://su=
preme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/529/120/">529 U.S.
120</a>, 159=E2=80=93160). The stakes here dwarf those of other major
questions cases. And as in those cases, =E2=80=9Ca reasonable interpreter
would [not] expect=E2=80=9D Congress to =E2=80=9Cpawn[&nbsp;]=E2=80=9D such=
 a =E2=80=9Cbig-time
policy call[&nbsp;] .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. off to another branch.=E2=80=9D
<em>Biden</em> v. <em>Nebraska</em>, 600 U.S. 477, 515 (Barrett, J.,
concurring).</p>
<p>There is no exception to the major questions
doctrine for emergency statutes. Nor does the fact that tariffs
implicate foreign affairs render the doctrine inapplicable. The
Framers gave =E2=80=9CCongress alone=E2=80=9D the power to impose tariffs d=
uring
peacetime. <em>Merritt</em> v. <em>Welsh</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" hr=
ef=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/104/694/">104
U.S. 694</a>, 700. And the foreign affairs implications of tariffs
do not make it any more likely that Congress would relinquish its
tariff power through vague language, or without careful limits.
Accordingly, the President must =E2=80=9Cpoint to clear congressional
authorization=E2=80=9D to justify his extraordinary assertion of that
power. <em>Nebraska</em>, 600 U.&nbsp;S., at 506 (internal quotation
marks omitted). He cannot. Pp.&nbsp;7=E2=80=9313.</p>
<p>The Chief Justice delivered the opinion of the
Court with respect to Part II=E2=80=93B,&nbsp;concluding:</p>
<p>(a)&nbsp;IEEPA authorizes the President to
=E2=80=9Cinvestigate, block during the pendency of an investigation,
regulate, direct and compel, nullify, void, prevent or prohibit
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation or exportation.=E2=80=9D =C2=A71702(a)(1)(B). A=
bsent
from this lengthy list of specific powers is any mention of tariffs
or duties. Had Congress intended to convey the distinct and
extraordinary power to impose tariffs, it would have done so
expressly, as it consistently has in other tariff statutes.</p>
<p>The power to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.
importation=E2=80=9D does not fill that void. The term =E2=80=9Cregulate,=
=E2=80=9D as
ordinarily used, means to =E2=80=9Cfix, establish, or control; to adjust by
rule, method, or established mode; to direct by rule or
restriction; to subject to governing principles or laws.=E2=80=9D Black=E2=
=80=99s
Law Dictionary 1156. The facial breadth of this definition places
in stark relief what =E2=80=9Dregulate=E2=80=9D is not usually thought to i=
nclude:
taxation. Many statutes grant the Executive the power to
=E2=80=9Cregulate.=E2=80=9D Yet the Government cannot identify any statute =
in which
the power to regulate includes the power to tax. The Court is
therefore skeptical that in IEEPA=E2=80=94and IEEPA alone=E2=80=94Congress =
hid a
delegation of its birth-right power to tax within the quotidian
power to =E2=80=9Cregulate.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p>While taxes may accomplish regulatory ends, it
does not follow that the power to regulate includes the power to
tax as a means of regulation. Indeed, when Congress addresses both
the power to regulate and the power to tax, it does so separately
and expressly. That it did not do so here is strong evidence that
=E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D in IEEPA does not include taxation.</p>
<p>A contrary reading would render IEEPA partly
unconstitutional. IEEPA authorizes the President to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation or exportation.=E2=80=9D =C2=A71702(a)(1)(B). B=
ut
taxing exports is expressly forbidden by the Constitution. Art. I,
=C2=A79, cl. 5.</p>
<p>The =E2=80=9Cneighboring words=E2=80=9D with which =E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=
=80=9D
=E2=80=9Cis associated=E2=80=9D also suggest that Congress did not intend f=
or
=E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D to include the revenue-raising power. <em>United
States</em> v. <em>Williams</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https:/=
/supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/553/285/">553 U.S.
285</a>, 294. Each of the nine verbs in =C2=A71702(a)(1)(B) authorizes a
distinct action a President might take in sanctioning foreign
actors or controlling domestic actors engaged in foreign commerce,
as Presidential practice confirms. And none of the listed
authorities includes the distinct and extraordinary power to raise
revenue=E2=80=94a power which no President has ever found in IEEPA. Pp.
14=E2=80=9316.</p>
<p>(b)&nbsp;Several arguments marshaled in
response are unpersuasive. First, the contention that IEEPA confers
the power to impose tariffs because early commentators and the
Court=E2=80=99s cases discuss tariffs in the context of the Commerce Clause
answers the wrong question. The question is not whether tariffs can
ever be a means of regulating commerce. It is instead whether
<em>Congress</em>, when conferring the power to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation,=E2=80=9D gave the President the power to
impose tariffs at his sole discretion. And Congress=E2=80=99s pattern of
usage is plain: When Congress grants the power to impose tariffs,
it does so clearly and with careful constraints. It did neither in
IEEPA.</p>
<p>Second, the argument that =E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D naturally
includes tariffs because the term lies between two poles in
IEEPA=E2=80=94=E2=80=9Ccompel=E2=80=9D on the affirmative end and =E2=80=9C=
prohibit=E2=80=9D on the
negative end=E2=80=94is unavailing. Although tariffs may be less extreme
than an outright compulsion or prohibition, it does not follow that
tariffs lie on the spectrum between those poles; they are different
in kind, not degree, from the other authorities in IEEPA. Tariffs
operate directly on domestic importers to raise revenue for the
Treasury and are =E2=80=9Cvery clear[ly] .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. a branch of the
taxing power.=E2=80=9D <em>Gibbons</em>, 9 Wheat., at 201. Thus, they fall
outside the spectrum entirely.</p>
<p>Third, the argument based on IEEPA=E2=80=99s
predecessor, the Trading with the Enemy Act (TWEA), and the Court
of Customs and Patent Appeals=E2=80=99 decision in <em>United States</em> v=
.
<em>Yoshida Int=E2=80=99l, Inc.</em>, 526 F.2d 560, cannot bear the weight
placed on it. A single, expressly limited opinion from a
specialized intermediate appellate court does not establish a
well-settled meaning that the Court can assume Congress
incorporated into IEEPA.</p>
<p>Fourth, the historical argument based on the
Court=E2=80=99s wartime precedents fails. Those precedents are facially
inapposite, as all agree the President lacks inherent peacetime
authority to impose tariffs. And the attenuated chain of inferences
from wartime precedents through multiple iterations of TWEA to
IEEPA cannot support=E2=80=94much less clearly support=E2=80=94a reading of=
 IEEPA
that includes the distinct power to impose tariffs.</p>
<p>Finally, arguments relying on this Court=E2=80=99s
precedents lack merit. <em>Federal Energy Administration</em> v.
<em>Algonquin SNG, Inc.</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://sup=
reme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/426/548/">426 U.S.
548</a>, bears little on the meaning of IEEPA. Section 232(b) of
the Trade Expansion Act of 1962 contains sweeping,
discretion-conferring language that IEEPA does not contain, and the
explicit reference to duties in Section 232(a) renders it natural
for Section 232(b) itself to authorize duties. Nor does <em>Dames
&amp; Moore</em> v. <em>Regan</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https=
://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/453/654/">453 U.S.
654</a>, offer support because that case was exceedingly narrow,
did not address the President=E2=80=99s power to =E2=80=9Cregulate,=E2=80=
=9D and did not
involve tariffs at all. Pp. 16=E2=80=9320.</p>
<p>Justice Kagan, joined by Justice Sotomayor and
Justice Jackson, agreed that IEEPA does not authorize the President
to impose tariffs, but concluded that the Court need not invoke the
major questions doctrine because the ordinary tools of statutory
interpretation amply support that result. Pp. 1=E2=80=937.</p>
<p>Justice Jackson would also consult legislative
history=E2=80=94in particular, the House and Senate Reports that
accompanied IEEPA and its predecessor statute, TWEA=E2=80=94to determine
that Congress did not intend for IEEPA to authorize the Executive
to impose tariffs. Pp. 1=E2=80=935.</p>
<p>Roberts, C.&nbsp;J., announced the judgment of
the Court and delivered the opinion of the Court with respect to
Parts I, II=E2=80=93A=E2=80=931, and II=E2=80=93B, in which Sotomayor, Kaga=
n, Gorsuch,
Barrett, and Jackson, JJ., joined, and an opinion with respect to
Parts II=E2=80=93A=E2=80=932 and III, in which Gorsuch and Barrett, JJ., jo=
ined.
Gorsuch, J., and Barrett, J., filed concurring opinions. Kagan, J.,
filed an opinion concurring in part and concurring in the judgment,
in which Sotomayor and Jackson, JJ., joined. Jackson, J., filed an
opinion concurring in part and concurring in the judgment. Thomas,
J., filed a dissenting opinion. Kavanaugh, J., filed a dissenting
opinion, in which Thomas and Alito, JJ., joined.</p>
