														=09

															NOTICE: This opinion is subject to
formal revision before publication in the United States Reports.
Readers are requested to notify the Reporter of Decisions, Supreme
Court of the United States, Washington, D. C. 20543,
pio@supremecourt.gov, of any typographical or other formal
errors.<p></p>

															SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES<p></p>

															_________________<p></p>

															Nos. 24=E2=80=931287 and 25=E2=80=93250<p></p>

															_________________<p></p>

															LEARNING RESOURCES, INC., et&nbsp;al.,
PETITIONERS<p></p>

															24=E2=80=931287<em>v.</em><p></p>

															DONALD J. TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE
UNITED STATES, et&nbsp;al.<p></p>

															on writ of certiorari before judgment to the
united states court of appeals for the district of columbia
circuit<p></p>

															<p></p>

															DONALD J. TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED
STATES, et&nbsp;al., PETITIONERS<p></p>

															25=E2=80=93250<em>v.</em><p></p>

															V.O.S. SELECTIONS, INC.,
et&nbsp;al.<p></p>

															on writ of certiorari to the united states
court of appeals for the federal circuit<p></p>

															[February 20, 2026]<p></p>

															<p></p>

															Chief Justice Roberts announced the judgment
of the Court and delivered the opinion of the Court, except as to
Parts II=E2=80=93A=E2=80=932 and III.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com=
/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F1" name=3D"T1" id=3D"T1">1</a>]*<p></p>

															We decide whether the International Emergency
Economic Powers Act (IEEPA) authorizes the President to impose
tariffs.<p></p>

															I<p></p>

															A<p></p>

															Shortly after taking office, President Trump
sought to address two foreign threats. The first was the influx of
illegal drugs from Canada, Mexico, and China. Presidential
Proclamation No. 10886, 90 Fed. Reg. 8327 (2025); Exec. Order No.
14193, 90 Fed. Reg. 9113 (2025); Exec. Order No. 14194, 90 Fed.
Reg. 9117 (2025); Exec. Order No. 14195, 90 Fed. Reg. 9121 (2025).
The second was =E2=80=9Clarge and persistent=E2=80=9D trade deficits. Exec.=
 Order
No. 14257, 90 Fed. Reg. 15041 (2025). The President determined that
the first threat had =E2=80=9Ccreated a public health crisis,=E2=80=9D 90 F=
ed. Reg.
9113, and that the second had =E2=80=9Cled to the hollowing out=E2=80=9D of=
 the
American manufacturing base and =E2=80=9Cundermined critical supply
chains,=E2=80=9D <em>id</em>., at 15041. He invoked his authority under IEE=
PA
to respond.<p></p>

															Enacted in 1977, IEEPA gives the President
economic tools to address significant foreign threats. 91Stat.
1626. When acting under IEEPA, the President must identify an
=E2=80=9Cunusual and extraordinary threat=E2=80=9D to American national sec=
urity,
foreign policy, or the economy, originating primarily =E2=80=9Coutside the
United States.=E2=80=9D 50 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71701(a). And he must
=E2=80=9Cdeclare[&nbsp;] a national emergency=E2=80=9D under the National
Emergencies Act. <em>Ibid</em>.; see 90Stat. 1255. He may then, =E2=80=9Cby
means of instructions, licenses, or otherwise,=E2=80=9D take the following
actions to =E2=80=9Cdeal with=E2=80=9D the threat: =E2=80=9Cinvestigate, bl=
ock during the
pendency of an investigation, regulate, direct and compel, nullify,
void, prevent or prohibit, any acquisition, holding, withholding,
use, transfer, withdrawal, transportation, importation or
exportation of, or dealing in, or exercising any right, power, or
privilege with respect to, or transactions involving, any property
in which any foreign country or a national thereof has any
interest.=E2=80=9D =C2=A7=C2=A71701(a), 1702(a)(1)(B).<p></p>

															President Trump declared a national emergency as
to both the drug trafficking and the trade deficits, which he
deemed =E2=80=9Cunusual and extraordinary=E2=80=9D threats. He then imposed=
 tariffs
to deal with each threat. As to the drug trafficking tariffs, the
President imposed a 25% duty on most Canadian and Mexican imports
and a 10% duty on most Chinese imports. 90 Fed. Reg. 9114, 9118,
9122=E2=80=939123. As to the trade deficit (or =E2=80=9Creciprocal=E2=80=9D=
) tariffs, the
President imposed a duty =E2=80=9Con all imports from all trading partners=
=E2=80=9D
of at least 10%. <em>Id</em>., at 15045. Dozens of nations faced
higher rates. <em>Id</em>., at 15049. And these tariffs applied
notwithstanding any extant trade agreements. <em>Id</em>., at
15045.<p></p>

															Since imposing each set of tariffs, the
President has issued several increases, reductions, and other
modifications. One month after imposing the 10% drug trafficking
tariffs on Chinese goods, he increased the rate to 20%. See Exec.
Order No. 14228, 90 Fed. Reg. 11463 (2025). One month later, he
removed a statutory exemption for Chinese goods under $800. Exec.
Order No. 14256, 90 Fed. Reg. 14899 (2025). Less than a week after
imposing the reciprocal tariffs, the President increased the rate
on Chinese goods from 34% to 84%. Exec. Order No. 14259, 90 Fed.
Reg. 15509 (2025). The very next day, he increased the rate further
still, to 125%. Exec. Order No. 14266, 90 Fed. Reg. 15625, 15626
(2025). This brought the total effective tariff rate on most
Chinese goods to 145%. The President has also shifted sets of goods
into and out of the reciprocal tariff framework. See,
<em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., Exec. Order No. 14360, 90 Fed. Reg. 54091
(2025) (exempting from reciprocal tariffs beef, fruits, coffee,
tea, spices, and some fertilizers); Exec. Order No. 14346, 90 Fed.
Reg. 43737 (2025). And he has issued a variety of other
adjustments. See, <em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., Exec. Order No. 14358, 90
Fed. Reg. 50729, 50730 (2025) (extending =E2=80=9Cthe suspension of
heightened reciprocal tariffs=E2=80=9D on Chinese imports).<p></p>

															B<p></p>

															Petitioners in <em>Learning Resources</em> and
respondents in <em>V.O.S. Selections</em> filed suit, alleging that
IEEPA does not authorize the reciprocal or drug trafficking
tariffs. The <em>Learning Resources</em> plaintiffs=E2=80=94two small
businesses=E2=80=94sued in the United States District Court for the
District of Columbia. The <em>V.O.S. Selections</em> plaintiffs=E2=80=94fiv=
e
small businesses and 12 States=E2=80=94sued in the United States Court of
International Trade (CIT).<p></p>

															The Government moved to transfer the <em>Learning
Resources</em> case to the CIT. It argued that the District Court
lacked jurisdiction under 28 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71581(i)(1), which
gives the CIT =E2=80=9Cexclusive jurisdiction of any civil action commenced
against=E2=80=9D the Government =E2=80=9Cthat arises out of any law of the =
United
States providing for .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. tariffs=E2=80=9D or their
=E2=80=9Cadministration and enforcement.=E2=80=9D The District Court denied=
 that
motion and granted the plaintiffs=E2=80=99 motion for a preliminary
injunction, concluding that IEEPA did not grant the President the
power to impose tariffs. 784 F.&nbsp;Supp. 3d 209 (DC 2025).<p></p>

															In the <em>V.O.S. Selections</em> case, the CIT
granted the plaintiffs=E2=80=99 motion for summary judgment. 772
F.&nbsp;Supp. 3d 1350 (2025). The Federal Circuit, sitting en banc,
affirmed in relevant part. 149 F. 4th 1312 (2025). It first
concluded that the CIT had exclusive jurisdiction because the
plaintiffs=E2=80=99 claims arose out of modifications to the Harmonized
Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS). <em>Id</em>., at 1329.
On the merits, it agreed with the CIT that IEEPA=E2=80=99s grant of
authority to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D did not
authorize the challenged tariffs, which =E2=80=9Care unbounded in scope,
amount, and duration.=E2=80=9D <em>Id</em>., at 1338. Judge Cunningham
concurred (for four judges), reasoning that IEEPA did not authorize
the President to impose <em>any</em> tariffs. <em>Id</em>., at 1340.
Judge Taranto dissented (for four judges), concluding that IEEPA
authorized the challenged tariffs. <em>Id</em>., at 1348.<p></p>

															The Government filed a motion to expedite and a
petition for certiorari in <em>V.O.S. Selections</em>, and the
<em>Learning Resources</em> plaintiffs filed a petition for
certiorari before judgment. We granted the motion and petitions and
consolidated the cases. 606 U.S. 1050 (2025).[<a href=3D"https://supreme.ju=
stia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F2" name=3D"T2" id=3D"T2">2</a>]<p><=
/p>

															II<p></p>

															Based on two words separated by 16 others in
Section 1702(a)(1)(B) of IEEPA=E2=80=94=E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D and =E2=
=80=9Cimportation=E2=80=9D=E2=80=94the
President asserts the independent power to impose tariffs on
imports from any country, of any product, at any rate, for any
amount of time. Those words cannot bear such weight.<p></p>

															A<p></p>

															1<p></p>

															Article I, Section 8, of the Constitution sets
forth the powers of the Legislative Branch. The first Clause of
that provision specifies that =E2=80=9CThe Congress shall have Power To lay
and collect Taxes, Duties, Imposts and Excises.=E2=80=9D It is no accident
that this power appears first. The power to tax was, Alexander
Hamilton explained, =E2=80=9Cthe most important of the authorities proposed
to be conferred upon the Union.=E2=80=9D The Federalist No. 33, pp. 202=E2=
=80=93203
(C. Rossiter ed. 1961). It is both a =E2=80=9Cpower to destroy,=E2=80=9D
<em>McCulloch</em> v. <em>Maryland</em>, 4 Wheat. 316, 431 (1819), and
a power =E2=80=9Cnecessary to the existence and prosperity of a
nation=E2=80=9D=E2=80=94=E2=80=9Cthe one great power upon which the whole n=
ational fabric
is based.=E2=80=9D <em>Nicol</em> v. <em>Ames</em>, <a class=3D"related-cas=
e" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/173/509/">173
U.S. 509</a>, 515 (1899).<p></p>

															The power to impose tariffs is =E2=80=9Cvery clear[ly]
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. a branch of the taxing power.=E2=80=9D <em>Gibbons</em> v.
<em>Ogden</em>, 9 Wheat. 1, 201 (1824). =E2=80=9CA tariff,=E2=80=9D after a=
ll, =E2=80=9Cis a
tax levied on imported goods and services.=E2=80=9D Congressional Research
Service (CRS), C. Casey, U.&nbsp;S. Tariff Policy: Overview 1
(2025). And tariffs =E2=80=9Craise[&nbsp;] revenue,=E2=80=9D <em>West Lynn =
Creamery,
Inc.</em> v. <em>Healy</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supr=
eme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/512/186/">512 U.S.
186</a>, 193 (1994)=E2=80=94the defining feature of a tax, <em>United
States</em> v. <em>Kahriger</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https:/=
/supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/345/22/">345 U.S.
22</a>, 28, and n. 4 (1953); <em>Sonzinsky</em> v. <em>United
States</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/c=
ases/federal/us/300/506/">300 U.S.
506</a>, 514 (1937). Indeed, the Framers expected that the
Government would for =E2=80=9Ca long time depend .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. chiefly
on=E2=80=9D tariffs for revenue. The Federalist No. 12, at 93 (A.
Hamilton). Little wonder, then, that the First Congress=E2=80=99s first
exercise of its taxing power (and its second enacted law, right
after the one providing for the new officials to take an oath) was
a tariff law. See Act of July 4, 1789, ch. 2, 1Stat. 24.<p></p>

															Recognizing the taxing power=E2=80=99s unique
importance, and having just fought a revolution motivated in large
part by =E2=80=9Ctaxation without representation,=E2=80=9D the Framers gave
Congress =E2=80=9Calone .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. access to the pockets of the
people.=E2=80=9D The Federalist No. 48, at 310 (J. Madison); see also
Declaration of Independence =C2=B619. They required =E2=80=9CAll Bills for
raising Revenue [to] originate in the House of Representatives.=E2=80=9D
U.&nbsp;S. Const., Art. I, =C2=A77, cl.&nbsp;1. And in doing so, they
ensured that only the House could =E2=80=9Cpropose the supplies requisite
for the support of government,=E2=80=9D thereby reducing =E2=80=9Call the o=
vergrown
prerogatives of the other branches.=E2=80=9D The Federalist No. 58, at 359
(J. Madison). They did not vest any part of the taxing power in the
Executive Branch. See <em>Nicol</em>, 173 U.&nbsp;S., at 515 (=E2=80=9C[T]h=
e
whole power of taxation rests with Congress=E2=80=9D).<p></p>

															The Government thus concedes, as it must, that
the President enjoys no inherent authority to impose tariffs during
peacetime. Tr. of Oral Arg. 70=E2=80=9371. And it does not defend the
challenged tariffs as an exercise of the President=E2=80=99s warmaking
powers. The United States, after all, is not at war with every
nation in the world. The Government instead relies exclusively on
IEEPA. It reads the words =E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Cimportati=
on=E2=80=9D to effect a
sweeping delegation of Congress=E2=80=99s power to set tariff
policy=E2=80=94authorizing the President to impose tariffs of unlimited
amount and duration, on any product from any country. 50
U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71702(a)(1)(B).<p></p>

															2<p></p>

															We have long expressed =E2=80=9Creluctan[ce] to read
into ambiguous statutory text=E2=80=9D extraordinary delegations of
Congress=E2=80=99s powers. <em>West Virginia</em> v. <em>EPA</em>, 597 U.S.
697, 723 (2022) (quoting <em>Utility Air Regulatory Group</em> v.
<em>EPA</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/=
cases/federal/us/573/302/">573 U.S.
302</a>, 324 (2014)). In <em>Biden</em> v. <em>Nebraska</em>, 600 U.S.
477 (2023), for example, we declined to read authorization to
=E2=80=9Cwaive or modify=E2=80=9D statutory or regulatory provisions applic=
able to
financial assistance programs as a delegation of power to cancel
$430 billion in student loan debt. <em>Id</em>., at 494 (quoting 20
U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71098bb(a)(1)). In <em>West Virginia</em> v.
<em>EPA</em>, we declined to read authorization to determine the
=E2=80=9Cbest system of emission reduction=E2=80=9D as a delegation of powe=
r to
force a nationwide transition away from the use of coal. 597
U.&nbsp;S., at 732 (quoting 42 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A77411(a)(1)). And
in <em>National Federation of Independent Business</em> v.
<em>OSHA</em>, 595 U.S. 109 (2022) (<em>per&nbsp;curiam</em>), we
declined to read authorization to ensure =E2=80=9Csafe and healthful
working conditions=E2=80=9D as a delegation of power to impose a vaccine
mandate on 84 million Americans. <em>Id</em>., at 114, 117 (quoting
29 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A7651(b)); see also, <em>e.g.</em>, <em>Alabama
Assn. of Realtors</em> v. <em>Department of Health and Human
Servs.</em>, 594 U.S. 758, 764=E2=80=93765 (2021) (<em>per curiam</em>);
<em>King</em> v. <em>Burwell</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https:=
//supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/576/473/">576 U.S.
473</a>, 485=E2=80=93486 (2015); <em>Utility Air</em>, 573 U.&nbsp;S., at
324.<p></p>

															We have described several of these cases as
=E2=80=9Cmajor questions=E2=80=9D cases. <em>Nebraska</em>, 600 U.&nbsp;S.,=
 at 505;
<em>West Virginia</em>, 597 U.&nbsp;S., at 732; see also <em>FDA</em>
v. <em>Brown &amp; Williamson Tobacco Corp.</em>, <a class=3D"related-case"=
 href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/529/120/">529 U.S.
120</a>, 159 (2000) (citing S. Breyer, Judicial Review of Questions
of Law and Policy, 38 Admin. L.&nbsp;Rev. 363, 370 (1986)). In
each, the Government claimed broad, expansive power on an uncertain
statutory basis. And in each, the statutory text might =E2=80=9C[a]s a
matter of definitional possibilities=E2=80=9D have been read to delegate
the asserted power. <em>West Virginia</em>, 597 U.&nbsp;S., at 732
(internal quotation marks omitted). But =E2=80=9Ccontext=E2=80=9D counseled
=E2=80=9Cskepticism.=E2=80=9D <em>Id</em>., at 721, 732. That context inclu=
ded not
just other language within the statute, but =E2=80=9Cconstitutional
structure=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Ccommon sense.=E2=80=9D <em>Nebraska</em>, 6=
00 U.&nbsp;S., at
512, 515 (Barrett, J., concurring). =E2=80=9C[B]oth separation of powers
principles and a practical understanding of legislative intent=E2=80=9D
suggested Congress would not have delegated =E2=80=9Chighly consequential
power=E2=80=9D through ambiguous language. <em>West Virginia</em>, 597
U.&nbsp;S., at 723=E2=80=93724.<p></p>

															These considerations apply with particular force
where, as here, the purported delegation involves the core
congressional power of the purse. =E2=80=9CCongress would likely
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. intend[&nbsp;] for itself&nbsp;=E2=80=9D the =E2=80=9Cbasic=
 and
consequential tradeoffs,=E2=80=9D <em>id</em>., at 730, inherent in uses of
this =E2=80=9Cmost complete and effectual weapon,=E2=80=9D The Federalist N=
o. 58,
at 359. And if Congress were to relinquish that weapon to another
branch, a =E2=80=9Creasonable interpreter=E2=80=9D would expect it to do so
=E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98clearly.=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D <em>Nebraska</em>,=
 600 U.&nbsp;S., at
514=E2=80=93515 (Barrett, J., concurring) (quoting <em>Utility Air</em>, 57=
3
U.&nbsp;S., at 324).<p></p>

															What common sense suggests, congressional
practice confirms. When Congress has delegated its tariff powers,
it has done so in explicit terms, and subject to strict limits.
Congress has consistently used words like =E2=80=9Cduty=E2=80=9D in statute=
s
delegating authority to impose tariffs. (A customs =E2=80=9Cduty=E2=80=9D i=
s simply
=E2=80=9Cthe federal tax levied on goods shipped into the United States.=E2=
=80=9D
Black=E2=80=99s Law Dictionary 638 (12th ed. 2024).) See,
<em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., 19 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71338(d) (=E2=80=9Crates=
 of
