steps. Insofar as the Government relies on our wartime cases
themselves, they are facially inapposite. Regardless of what they
might mean for the President=E2=80=99s inherent <em>wartime</em> authority,
all agree that the President has no inherent peacetime authority to
impose tariffs.<p></p>

															Nor are we persuaded that the dots connect from
our wartime precedents, through multiple iterations of TWEA, to
IEEPA, such that IEEPA should be interpreted to grant the President
an expansive peacetime tariff power. This argument relies
extensively on a series of inferences drawn from scant legislative
history. Such an attenuated chain cannot support=E2=80=94much less
=E2=80=9Cclearly=E2=80=9D support=E2=80=94a reading of IEEPA that includes =
the distinct
power to impose tariffs. <em>Alabama Assn.</em>, 594 U.&nbsp;S., at
764.<p></p>

															Turning to this Court=E2=80=99s precedents, the
Government first relies on <em>Federal Energy Administration</em> v.
<em>Algonquin SNG, Inc.</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://sup=
reme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/426/548/">426 U.S.
548</a> (1976). There, we held that Section 232(b) of the Trade
Expansion Act of 1962, which allows the President to =E2=80=9Cadjust the
imports=E2=80=9D of particular goods to protect national security, includes
the power to impose =E2=80=9Clicense fees.=E2=80=9D <em>Id</em>., at 561. B=
ut that
holding bears little on the meaning of IEEPA. As a textual matter,
Section 232(b) authorizes the President not only to =E2=80=9Cadjust
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. imports,=E2=80=9D but (as the Government emphasized in
<em>Algonquin</em>) to =E2=80=9C<em>take such action</em> .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. <=
em>as
he deems necessary</em>=E2=80=9D to adjust the imports of a good. Brief for
Petitioners 26 (emphasis in original) and Tr. of Oral Arg. 6=E2=80=937, in
<em>Federal Energy Administration</em> v. <em>Algonquin SNG, Inc.</em>,
O. T. 1975, No. 75=E2=80=93382. IEEPA does not contain such sweeping,
discretion-conferring language. As for context, Section 232(a)
states that =E2=80=9C[n]o action shall be taken=E2=80=9D to =E2=80=9Cdecrea=
se or eliminate=E2=80=9D
an existing =E2=80=9Cduty or other import restriction=E2=80=9D if doing so =
would
threaten national security. 19 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71862(a) (1970
ed.). This explicit reference to duties preceding Section 232(b)
renders it natural for Section 232(b) itself to authorize duties.
Thus, we decline to extend <em>Algonquin</em>=E2=80=99s expressly =E2=80=9C=
limited=E2=80=9D
holding any further. 426 U.&nbsp;S., at 571.<p></p>

															Finally, the Government invokes <em>Dames &amp;
Moore</em> v. <em>Regan</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://sup=
reme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/453/654/">453 U.S.
654</a> (1981), but that case offers no support. <em>Dames &amp;
Moore</em> was exceedingly narrow,[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/ca=
ses/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F7" name=3D"T7" id=3D"T7">7</a>] did not addres=
s the President=E2=80=99s power to =E2=80=9Cregulate,=E2=80=9D
and did not involve tariffs at all. If anything, that case
highlights the importance of close attention to IEEPA=E2=80=99s text. =E2=
=80=9CThe
terms of .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. IEEPA,=E2=80=9D we held, =E2=80=9Cdo not authorize=
=E2=80=9D the
suspension of claims. <em>Id</em>., at 675. So too here; the terms of
IEEPA do not authorize tariffs.<p></p>

															III<p></p>

															The President asserts the extraordinary power
to unilaterally impose tariffs of unlimited amount, duration, and
scope. In light of the breadth, history, and constitutional context
of that asserted authority, he must identify clear congressional
authorization to exercise it.<p></p>

															IEEPA=E2=80=99s grant of authority to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D falls short. IEEPA contains no
reference to tariffs or duties. The Government points to no statute
in which Congress used the word =E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D to authorize tax=
ation.
And until now no President has read IEEPA to confer such power.<p></p>

															We claim no special competence in matters of
economics or foreign affairs. We claim only, as we must, the
limited role assigned to us by Article III of the Constitution.
Fulfilling that role, we hold that IEEPA does not authorize the
President to impose tariffs.<p></p>

															The judgment of the United States Court of
Appeals for the Federal Circuit in case No. 25=E2=80=93250 is affirmed. The
judgment of the United States District Court for the District of
Columbia in case No. 24=E2=80=931287 is vacated, and the case is remanded
with instructions to dismiss for lack of jurisdiction.<p></p>

															It is so ordered.<p></p>
