														=09

															SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES<p></p>

															_________________<p></p>

															Nos. 24=E2=80=931287 and 25=E2=80=93250<p></p>

															_________________<p></p>

															LEARNING RESOURCES, INC., et&nbsp;al.,
PETITIONERS<p></p>

															24=E2=80=931287<em>v.</em><p></p>

															DONALD J. TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE
UNITED STATES, et&nbsp;al.<p></p>

															on writ of certiorari to the united states
court of appeals for the district of columbia circuit<p></p>

															<p></p>

															DONALD J. TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED
STATES, et&nbsp;al., PETITIONERS<p></p>

															25=E2=80=93250<em>v.</em><p></p>

															V.O.S. SELECTIONS, INC.,
et&nbsp;al.<p></p>

															on writ of certiorari to the united states
court of appeals for the federal circuit<p></p>

															[February 20, 2026]<p></p>

															<p></p>

															Justice Kagan, with whom Justice Sotomayor and
Justice Jackson join, concurring in part and concurring in the
judgment.<p></p>

															The Court holds today that the International
Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA) does not authorize the
President to impose tariffs. I agree with that conclusion, as I do
with the bulk of the principal opinion=E2=80=99s reasoning. But because I
think the ordinary tools of statutory interpretation amply support
today=E2=80=99s result, I do not join the part of that opinion invoking the
so-called major-questions doctrine.<p></p>

															The question that part asks, similar to the one
posed in other =E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98major questions=E2=80=99 cases,=E2=
=80=9D is whether the
President can identify =E2=80=9Cclear congressional authorization=E2=80=9D =
for his
action=E2=80=94here, to impose tariffs under IEEPA. <em>Ante,</em> at 7, 13=
,
20. The demand is for a clear statement=E2=80=94something more explicit or
specific than the statutory basis that would ordinarily suffice to
support executive action. See, <em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., <em>West
Virginia</em> v. <em>EPA</em>, 597 U.S. 697, 721=E2=80=93724, 732 (2022);
<em>Biden</em> v. <em>Nebraska</em>, 600 U.S. 477, 505=E2=80=93506 (2023). =
The
reason for that requirement, according to today=E2=80=99s opinion, is that
the Executive has claimed an =E2=80=9Cextraordinary=E2=80=9D power=E2=80=94=
one never
asserted before and having large-scale =E2=80=9Ceconomic and political
significance.=E2=80=9D <em>Ante,</em> at 7, 11; see <em>ante</em>, at 7=E2=
=80=9311.<p></p>

															I objected, in the principal cases cited, to the
demand for a special brand of legislative clarity. See <em>West
Virginia</em>, 597 U.&nbsp;S., at 764=E2=80=93784 (Kagan, J., dissenting);
<em>Nebraska</em>, 600 U.&nbsp;S., at 542=E2=80=93550 (Kagan, J.,
dissenting). In my view, the Court used its clear-authorization
rule in those cases to negate expansive delegations Congress had
approved. I explained there that the proper way to interpret a
delegation provision is through the standard rules of statutory
construction. See <em>West Virginia</em>, 597 U.&nbsp;S., at 765=E2=80=9376=
6
(Kagan, J., dissenting). That means, most concisely stated, reading
text in context. More expansively put, it means examining a
delegation provision=E2=80=99s language, assessing that provision=E2=80=99s=
 place
in the broader statutory scheme, and applying a =E2=80=9Cmodicum of common
sense=E2=80=9D about how Congress typically delegates. <em>Id</em>., at 764
(Kagan, J., dissenting); see <em>FDA</em> v. <em>Brown &amp;
Williamson Tobacco Corp.</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://su=
preme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/529/120/">529 U.S.
120</a>, 133 (2000). The last of those inquiries includes
consideration of whether Congress ever has before, or likely would,
delegate the power the Executive asserts=E2=80=94a matter also of import in
applying the major-questions doctrine. See <em>ante,</em> at 8=E2=80=9310;
<em>Nebraska</em>, 600 U.&nbsp;S., at 512=E2=80=93514, 517=E2=80=93519 (Bar=
rett, J.,
concurring); <em>id</em>., at 546, n.&nbsp;3 (Kagan, J., dissenting).
In the past, though, I have thought that the Court used that
doctrine to override=E2=80=94rather than help discover=E2=80=94the best rea=
ding of
delegation statutes. See <em>West Virginia</em>, 597 U.&nbsp;S., at
756 (Kagan, J., dissenting); <em>Nebraska</em>, 600 U.&nbsp;S., at
543 (Kagan, J., dissenting).<p></p>

															This case presents more nearly the opposite
situation: The use of a clear-statement rule here is unnecessary
because ordinary principles of statutory interpretation lead to the
same result.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-=
1287/#F1" name=3D"T1" id=3D"T1">1</a>] It is not just
that the Government=E2=80=99s arguments fail to satisfy an especially
strict test; it is that they fail to satisfy the normal one. Even
without a clear-statement rule in the picture, the conclusion
follows: IEEPA does not authorize the President to impose tariffs.
And indeed, the principal opinion=E2=80=99s reasoning well explains why.
The rest of this opinion draws on that analysis (I hope without too
much rehashing) to demonstrate what I view as the fundamental
point: Usual text-in-context interpretation dooms the tariffs the
President has imposed. The crucial provision of IEEPA, when viewed
in light of the broader statutory scheme and with a practical
awareness of how Congress delegates tariff authority, does not give
the President the power he wants.<p></p>

															Most important, IEEPA=E2=80=99s key phrase=E2=80=94the one t=
he
Government relies on=E2=80=94says nothing about imposing tariffs or taxes.
That text authorizes the President, upon finding a foreign threat
and declaring an emergency, to =E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D the =E2=80=9Cimpo=
rtation=E2=80=9D of
foreign goods. 50 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71702(a)(1)(B). And the
meaning of =E2=80=9Cregulate,=E2=80=9D both in common parlance and as Congr=
ess uses
the word, does not encompass taxing. See <em>ante,</em> at 14=E2=80=9315. T=
o
=E2=80=9Cregulate,=E2=80=9D according to the Government=E2=80=99s preferred=
 definition,
means to =E2=80=9Cfix, establish or control; to adjust by rule, method, or
established mode; to direct by rule or restriction; to subject to
governing principles or laws.=E2=80=9D Brief for Federal Parties 24
(quoting Black=E2=80=99s Law Dictionary 1156 (5th ed. 1979)). Nothing in
that definition naturally refers to levying taxes. Nor does
Congress ever use the word =E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D in that way. Hundreds=
 of
provisions in the U.&nbsp;S. Code give agencies the authority to
=E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D one thing or another. Yet the Government cannot =
identify
a single one that is understood to grant taxing power. See Tr. of
Oral Arg. 30. When Congress wants to delegate that power, it uses a
whole different vocabulary=E2=80=94terms like =E2=80=9Cduty,=E2=80=9D =E2=
=80=9Ctariff,=E2=80=9D or
=E2=80=9Csurcharge,=E2=80=9D which do not appear in IEEPA. See <em>ante,</e=
m> at 8
(citing representative statutes); see also <em>ante,</em> at 19
(discussing, in particular, 19 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71862 (1970
ed.)). And likewise, when Congress means to cover <em>both</em>
regulatory and taxing powers, it refers to each separately. See
<em>ante</em>, at 15 (also citing statutes). Of course, Congress
knows that taxes can be used for regulatory ends: They can be a
means of controlling or adjusting behavior. But Congress still
follows the path this Court long ago marked out, and the one most
consonant with ordinary meaning, of treating the power to
=E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D trade as =E2=80=9Centirely distinct=E2=80=9D fro=
m the power to =E2=80=9Clevy
taxes.=E2=80=9D <em>Gibbons</em> v. <em>Ogden</em>, 9 Wheat. 1, 201=E2=80=
=93202 (1824);
see <em>ante,</em> at 15. So in granting only the former, IEEPA
excludes the latter: The President has the ability to regulate, but
not to impose taxes on, imports.<p></p>

															The surrounding statutory language confirms the
point. As the principal opinion explains, =E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D is one=
 of 9
verbs listed in IEEPA=E2=80=99s delegation provision. See <em>ante,</em> at
15. (The others are =E2=80=9Cinvestigate,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cblock,=E2=80=9D=
 =E2=80=9Cdirect,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Ccompel,=E2=80=9D
=E2=80=9Cnullify,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cvoid,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cprevent,=E2=80=
=9D and =E2=80=9Cprohibit.=E2=80=9D =C2=A71702(a)(1)(B).)
Those verbs are followed by 11 objects, each describing a distinct
sort of transaction involving foreign property=E2=80=94not just
=E2=80=9Cimportation,=E2=80=9D but also =E2=80=9Cacquisition,=E2=80=9D =E2=
=80=9Cuse,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Ctransfer,=E2=80=9D and so
forth. <em>Ibid</em>. Combine the verbs and objects in all possible
ways, and the statute authorizes 99 actions a President can take to
address a foreign threat. And exactly none of the other 98 involves
raising revenues. Rather, each enables the President to impose
penalties, restrictions, or controls on foreign commerce. See
<em>ante,</em> at 15. So when the phrase =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.
importation=E2=80=9D is invoked to impose quantity or quality limits on
bringing foreign goods into the country=E2=80=94for example, by setting
quotas or requiring quarantines=E2=80=94the phrase fits well with its 98
neighbors. Just like the rest, it provides a way to constrain or
alter various foreign transactions. But when that phrase is invoked
to impose tariffs? Then it becomes the odd man out=E2=80=94the only one of
99 permission slips to involve =E2=80=9Cthe core congressional power of the
purse.=E2=80=9D <em>Ante,</em> at 8; see <em>ante,</em> at 5=E2=80=936. So =
even if
(contra both conventional and congressional usage) the word
=E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D might refer to taxation in some other (hitherto
undiscovered) statutory context, it would not do so in
IEEPA.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#=
F2" name=3D"T2" id=3D"T2">2</a>]<p></p>

															Likewise, Congress=E2=80=99s consistent practice in
delegating tariff power refutes the Government=E2=80=99s position. As the
principal opinion details, Title 19 of the U.&nbsp;S. Code includes
multiple provisions granting the President authority to levy
tariffs. See <em>ante,</em> at 8=E2=80=939. But in each and every instance,
Congress has not only used specific language (<em>e</em>.<em>g</em>.,
=E2=80=9Cduty=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Csurcharge=E2=80=9D), see <em>supra</em>,=
 at 4, but also imposed
tight restraints on the power given. It has capped the
tariff&nbsp;=E2=80=99s rate (<em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., 15%); or limited the
tariff&nbsp;=E2=80=99s duration (<em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., 150 days); or
established strict procedural conditions before the tariff can take
effect (<em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., investigations, public hearings, and
reports); or all of the above. See <em>ante,</em> at 8=E2=80=939. What
Congress has never done in a tariff provision is what the
Government claims it did here=E2=80=94conferred power on the President to
impose a tariff of any amount, for any time, on only his own
say-so. And construing IEEPA to give that unparalleled authority
would effectively erase all the carefully confined tariff
provisions in Title 19. For any President could then escape the
rigors of those laws=E2=80=94could put in place, say, a non-time-limited
100% tariff on all foreign products=E2=80=94by the simple expedient of
identifying a foreign threat. That gutting of Title 19=E2=80=99s tariff
scheme is not what Congress, when delegating power to =E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=
=80=9D
imports, could have meant to accomplish.<p></p>

															Nor has any President until now understood IEEPA
to authorize imposing tariffs. Between 1977 (when IEEPA was
enacted) and 2024, eight Presidents had the chance to make use of
IEEPA=E2=80=99s delegation of power. And all chose the same course. They
invoked the statute=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9Cregulate importation=E2=80=9D provis=
ion for a
variety of non-tariff purposes. See <em>ante,</em> at 10. But they
looked elsewhere=E2=80=94to Title 19=E2=80=99s provisions=E2=80=94for tarif=
f authority. See
<em>ante,</em> at 10=E2=80=9311. In other words, each President read the
statutes as Congress wrote them, with IEEPA enabling him to
regulate imports and Title 19 enabling him=E2=80=94in confined
situations=E2=80=94to tax those foreign goods. None, as far as anyone has
suggested, even considered doing otherwise.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.just=
ia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F3" name=3D"T3" id=3D"T3">3</a>]<p></p=
>

															For all those reasons, straight-up statutory
construction resolves this case for me; I need no major-questions
thumb on the interpretive scales. IEEPA gives the President
significant authority over transactions involving foreign property,
including the importation of goods. But in that generous
delegation, one power is conspicuously missing. Nothing in IEEPA=E2=80=99s
text, nor anything in its context, enables the President to
unilaterally impose tariffs. And needless to say, without statutory
authority, the President=E2=80=99s tariffs cannot stand. See <em>ante,</em>
at 5=E2=80=936.<p></p>
