														=09

															SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES<p></p>

															_________________<p></p>

															Nos. 24=E2=80=931287 and 25=E2=80=93250<p></p>

															_________________<p></p>

															LEARNING RESOURCES, INC., et&nbsp;al.,
PETITIONERS<p></p>

															24=E2=80=931287<em>v.</em><p></p>

															DONALD J. TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE
UNITED STATES, et&nbsp;al.<p></p>

															on writ of certiorari before judgment to the
united states court of appeals for the district of columbia
circuit<p></p>

															<p></p>

															DONALD J. TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED
STATES, et&nbsp;al., PETITIONERS<p></p>

															25=E2=80=93250<em>v.</em><p></p>

															V.O.S. SELECTIONS, INC.,
et&nbsp;al.<p></p>

															on writ of certiorari to the united states
court of appeals for the federal circuit<p></p>

															[February 20, 2026]<p></p>

															<p></p>

															Justice Jackson, concurring in part and
concurring in the judgment.<p></p>

															I agree with the Court=E2=80=99s conclusion that the
International Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA) does not
provide the President with the power to tariff. Three of my
colleagues have reached this result via the major questions
doctrine, see <em>ante,</em> at 7=E2=80=9313 (opinion of Roberts,
C.&nbsp;J.)=E2=80=94a framing that asks, in essence, whether Congress
=E2=80=9Cwould <em>likely</em> have intended=E2=80=9D to delegate the autho=
rity to
tariff to the President through IEEPA. <em>West Virginia</em> v.
<em>EPA</em>, 597 U.S. 697, 730 (2022) (emphasis added); see also
<em>id</em>., at 722=E2=80=93723. While probing Congress=E2=80=99s intent i=
s the
right inquiry, my colleagues speculate needlessly. In my view, the
Court can, and should, consult a statute=E2=80=99s legislative history to
determine what Congress actually intended the statute to do.<p></p>

															As Congress undertakes the legislative process,
congressional committees in the Senate and House often generate
official reports that describe Congress=E2=80=99s aims for the legislation.
See R. Katzmann, Judging Statutes 19=E2=80=9320 (2014) (Katzmann). Indeed,
there is evidence that lawmakers themselves pay more attention to
these reports than a statute=E2=80=99s text to understand the statute=E2=80=
=99s
purpose and meaning. A. Gluck &amp; L. Bressman, Statutory
Interpretation From the Inside=E2=80=94An Empirical Study of Congressional
Drafting, Delegation, and the Canons: Part I, 65 Stan. L. Rev. 901,
965=E2=80=93966, 968=E2=80=93969 (2013); see also Katzmann 37=E2=80=9338. T=
hus, in contrast
to the principal dissent=E2=80=99s rejection of Committee Reports as a
means of ascertaining a statute=E2=80=99s meaning, <em>post</em>, at 16,
n.&nbsp;11 (opinion of Kavanaugh, J.), I think these Senate and
House Reports are among the best evidence of what Congress sought
to accomplish with its enactments. See Gluck, 65 Stan. L. Rev., at
965, 977=E2=80=93978, 989.<p></p>

															In the cases now before us, that evidence shows
that Congress did not intend for IEEPA to authorize the Executive
to impose tariffs. Accord, <em>ante,</em> at 5, n.&nbsp;2 (Kagan, J.,
concurring in part and concurring in judgment). Instead, Congress
intended to delegate to the President the power to freeze and
control foreign property transactions.<p></p>

															Four pieces of the relevant legislative record
support this conclusion. The first two are the House and Senate
Reports that accompanied the 1941 amendment to IEEPA=E2=80=99s predecessor
statute, the Trading with the Enemy Act (TWEA). First enacted in
1917, TWEA authorized the President to control foreign property
during wartime. But some of TWEA=E2=80=99s sections delegating this
authority had lapsed, and =E2=80=9Cthere [was] doubt as to the
effectiveness of other sections.=E2=80=9D H.&nbsp;R. Rep. No. 1507, 77th
Cong., 1st Sess., 2 (1941). Accordingly, Congress amended TWEA in
1941, adding the subsection that includes the =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D language on which the President relies
today. First War Powers Act, 55Stat. 839=E2=80=93840. The Reports explained
Congress=E2=80=99s primary purpose for the 1941 amendment: shoring up the
President=E2=80=99s ability to control foreign-owned property by
maintaining and strengthening the =E2=80=9Cexisting system of foreign
property control (commonly known as freezing control).=E2=80=9D H.&nbsp;R.
Rep. No. 1507, at 2=E2=80=933; see also S.&nbsp;Rep. No. 911, 77th Cong.,
1st Sess., 2 (1941).[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us=
/607/24-1287/#F1" name=3D"T1" id=3D"T1">1</a>]<p></p>

															When Congress enacted IEEPA in 1977, limiting
the circumstances under which the President could exercise his
emergency authorities, it kept the =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.
importation=E2=80=9D language from TWEA. =C2=A7203(a)(1)(B), 91Stat. 1626. =
The
other two relevant pieces of legislative history=E2=80=94the Senate and
House Reports that accompanied IEEPA=E2=80=94demonstrate that Congress=E2=
=80=99s
intent regarding the scope of this statutory language remained the
same. As the Senate Report explained, Congress=E2=80=99s sole objective for
the =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D subsection was t=
o grant
the President the emergency authority =E2=80=9Cto control or freeze
property transactions where a foreign interest is involved.=E2=80=9D S.
Rep. No. 95=E2=80=93466, p. 5 (1977). The House Report likewise described
IEEPA as empowering the President to =E2=80=9Cregulate or freeze any
property in which any foreign country or a national thereof has any
interest.=E2=80=9D H.&nbsp;R. Rep. No. 95=E2=80=93459, p. 15 (1977).<p></p>

															With this evidence of Congress=E2=80=99s objective,
interpreting the text of IEEPA becomes an easy task. Each of the
listed verbs=E2=80=94=E2=80=9Cinvestigate, block during the pendency of an
investigation, regulate, direct and compel, nullify, void, prevent
or prohibit,=E2=80=9D 50 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71702(a)(1)(B)=E2=80=94pro=
vides a means
by which the President can freeze or control foreign property
transactions. See <em>ante,</em> at 4=E2=80=935, and n.&nbsp;2 (opinion of
Kagan, J.). Tariffs are different in kind. They are a tax on
imports; a means of generating revenue from transactions between
private parties. See <em>ante,</em> at 6 (majority opinion). Because
tariffs are not a means by which the President can freeze or
control foreign assets, interpreting IEEPA to authorize tariffs
would require the Court to override Congress=E2=80=99s expressed purpose
for including the =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D la=
nguage
in the statute.<p></p>

															*=E2=80=83=E2=80=83*=E2=80=83=E2=80=83*<p></p>

															Like The Chief Justice=E2=80=99s opinion, the
principal dissent declines the help of legislative history. See
<em>post,</em> at 16, n.&nbsp;11 (opinion of Kavanaugh, J.). The
dissent concludes that IEEPA and TWEA are =E2=80=9Cbest understood=E2=80=9D=
 as
authorizing tariffs, and that any other interpretation would =E2=80=9Cnot
make much sense.=E2=80=9D <em>Post</em>, at 24=E2=80=9325, 29.[<a href=3D"h=
ttps://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F2" name=3D"T2" id=
=3D"T2">2</a>] But why would it matter which interpretation
<em>we</em> think is =E2=80=9Cbest=E2=80=9D when Congress has already told =
us? The
legislative history here plainly establishes that Congress
understood and intended IEEPA and TWEA to authorize a wholly
different type of power: the power to freeze foreign-owned
property. And the proper role of the Court is to give effect to
Congress=E2=80=99s intent, not our own instincts. See <em>United States</em=
>
v. <em>American Trucking Assns., Inc.</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=
=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/310/534/">310
U.S. 534</a>, 542 (1940).<p></p>

															In short, in these cases, the legislative
history provides helpful evidence of =E2=80=9Cwhat Congress was trying to
do=E2=80=9D in IEEPA. Katzmann 38. Given that evidence, we need not
speculate or, worse, step into Congress=E2=80=99s shoes and formulate our
own views about what powers would be best to delegate to the
President for use during an emergency. See <em>ibid.</em>; J. Hurst,
Dealing With Statutes 33 (1982). When Congress tells us why it has
included certain language in a statute, the limited role of the
courts in our democratic system of government=E2=80=94as interpreters, not
lawmakers=E2=80=94demands that we give effect to the will of the
people.<p></p>
<div class=3D"opinion-footnotes">
<strong class=3D"heading-5 font-w-bold">Notes</strong>
<div class=3D"opinion-footnote"><span class=3D"opinion-footnote-ref"><a hre=
f=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#T1" name=3D"F=
1" id=3D"F1">1</a></span>
<span class=3D"opinion-footnote-text">&nbsp;In addition to
maintaining the President=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9Cfreezing control=E2=80=9D auth=
ority, Congress
also sought to authorize the President to <em>seize</em> foreign
property and use it to serve the interests of the United States. H.
R. Rep. No. 1507, at 3. To this end, the 1941 amendment provided
that foreign-owned property =E2=80=9Cshall vest .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. in such
agency or person as may be designated .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. by the
President.=E2=80=9D 55Stat. 840. Congress did not include this vesting
language in IEEPA.</span></div>
<div class=3D"opinion-footnote"><span class=3D"opinion-footnote-ref"><a hre=
f=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#T2" name=3D"F=
2" id=3D"F2">2</a></span>
<span class=3D"opinion-footnote-text">&nbsp;This reasoning appears to
follow the Court=E2=80=99s relatively recent practice of picking what it
deems the best reading of a statute without consideration of
Congress=E2=80=99s intent. See, <em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., <em>Stanley</em> v.
<em>City of Sanford</em>, 606 U.S. 46, 51=E2=80=9354 (2025); accord,
<em>id</em>., at 96=E2=80=9397, and n. 12 (Jackson, J.,
dissenting).</span></div>
