effectively banned the sale of liquor, the court held that Cahaba
did not enjoy such extraordinary =E2=80=9Cprohibitory=E2=80=9D power becaus=
e it was
=E2=80=9Cnot authorized by any express grant of power=E2=80=9D in the town=
=E2=80=99s
charter. <em>Id</em>., at 469; see also <em>id.,</em> at 466.<p></p>

															These cases are not outliers. Treatises confirm
that the extraordinary power principle was fundamental to municipal
corporations. A statute could =E2=80=9Cnot by implication invest [a] body
with any extraordinary authority.=E2=80=9D J. Willcock, The Law of
Municipal Corporations =C2=B6226, p. 99 (1827). Extraordinary powers
required =E2=80=9Cexpress words to that effect.=E2=80=9D <em>Ibid.</em> And=
 =E2=80=9C[a]ny
fair, reasonable doubt concerning the existence of power [was]
resolved by the courts against the corporation, and the power [was]
denied.=E2=80=9D 1 J. Dillon, Commentaries on the Law of Municipal
Corporations 145 (4th ed. 1890).<p></p>

															The takeaway is simple enough. Early
corporations often functioned much like today=E2=80=99s executive branch,
exercising delegated regulatory authority. And, when interpreting
the scope of that authority, the common law had a clear-statement
rule that looked strikingly like the major questions doctrine.<p></p>

															Historically, a similar precept applied in
