															C<p></p>

															If its effort to secure a broad foreign
affairs exception to the major questions doctrine won=E2=80=99t work, the
dissent hints at a more limited one specific to tariffs. Such an
exception makes sense, the dissent says, because =E2=80=9CPresidents have
long been granted substantial discretion over tariffs.=E2=80=9D
<em>Post</em>, at 52 (internal quotation marks omitted). Indeed, the
dissent contends, this tradition traces =E2=80=9Cback to near the
Founding.=E2=80=9D <em>Post</em>, at 59. If the dissent were right about
that, one might hesitate before accepting the President=E2=80=99s
concession that this case does not implicate any inherent Article
II authority. But, at least as I read it, history offers the
dissent little to work with.<p></p>

															Americans fought the Revolution in no small part
because they believed that only their elected representatives (not
the King, not even Parliament) possessed authority to tax them.
Declaration of Independence =C2=B619. And, they believed, that held true
not just for direct taxes like those in the Stamp Act, but also for
many duties on imports, like those found in the Sugar Act. E.
Morgan &amp; H. Morgan, The Stamp Act Crisis: Prologue to
Revolution 72=E2=80=9374 (1995 ed.); see 1 E. Stanwood, American Tariff
Controversies in the Nineteenth Century 60 (1903) (Stanwood); C.
Van Tyne, The Causes of the War of Independence 126=E2=80=93136 (1922); J.
Otis, The Rights of the British Colonies Asserted and Proved
(1764), in The Collected Political Writings of James Otis 119,
161=E2=80=93162 (2015); see also <em>id</em>., at xii (Introduction).<p></p=
>

															Americans later codified these beliefs in the
Constitution. Under the Articles of Confederation, the national
government was laden with debt and enjoyed few ways to repay it. To
address that problem, the framers afforded the federal government
new taxing powers in the Constitution. Art. I, =C2=A78, cl. 1. Many
thought these powers among =E2=80=9Cthe most important=E2=80=9D features of=
 the new
federal charter. See, <em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., The Federalist No. 33, at
202=E2=80=93203 (A. Hamilton). But, consistent with their view that only
the people=E2=80=99s elected representatives could constitutionally tax
them, the framers gave Congress alone =E2=80=9Caccess to the pockets of the
people.=E2=80=9D <em>Id</em>., No. 48, at 310 (J. Madison). And to cement
that role, the Constitution required that =E2=80=9CAll Bills for raising
Revenue shall originate in the House of Representatives,=E2=80=9D the body
most responsive to the people. Art. I, =C2=A77, cl. 1.<p></p>

															For much of the Nation=E2=80=99s history, this taxing
power was essentially a tariff power. The framers even considered
(and eventually rejected) the possibility of giving the federal
government the power to tax only through tariffs. The Federalist
No. 35, at 211 (A. Hamilton). No surprise, then, that Congress=E2=80=99s
first exercise of its taxing power was a tariff law. P. Ashley,
Modern Tariff History 170=E2=80=93171 (2d ed. 1910). And until the 20th
century, tariffs =E2=80=9Caccounted for between 50 and 90 percent=E2=80=9D =
of the
federal government=E2=80=99s revenue. J. Dobson, Two Centuries of Tariffs:
The Background and Emergence of the United States International
Trade Commission 1 (1976).<p></p>

															How did Congress exercise its all-important
tariff power? It debated every detail of the first tariff Act.
Stanwood 39=E2=80=9371. Ultimately, Congress said, imported malt would
incur a charge of 10 cents a bushel. Brown sugar one cent. Loaf
sugar three cents. And so on. <em>Id.</em>, at 59. The first tariff
Act was set to last for seven years. <em>Id.</em>, at 72. It lasted
barely one. <em>Ibid.</em> Soon, Congress was at it again, laying out
another exacting schedule of duties. <em>Id.</em>, at 75=E2=80=9376.
Throughout much of the 19th century, Congress proceeded similarly,
enacting highly detailed tariff schedules one after another. See F.
Taussig, The Tariff History of the United States 68=E2=80=93170 (8th ed.
1931).<p></p>

															An early debate over executive involvement in
setting tariffs demonstrates just how strongly Congress felt that
tariffs were a legislative business. In December 1791, President
Washington told Congress that General St. Clair had been defeated
in the Northwest Indian War, and the country would have to increase
the size of the army. Stanwood 104. That meant the government
needed more money. In response, a resolution was offered in the
House of Representatives to solicit advice from the Secretary of
the Treasury, Alexander Hamilton, on the best way to raise the
additional revenue=E2=80=94including through new tariffs. 3 Annals of
Congress 437 (1792); Stanwood 105=E2=80=93106. Ultimately, Hamilton=E2=80=
=99s
advice was sought, but only after a debate over the
constitutionality of even asking a member of the executive branch
for advice on raising revenue. <em>Ibid</em>.; 3 Annals of Congress
447.<p></p>

															To be sure, on later occasions Congress turned
to the executive branch for more help still. But it usually did so
to address changing trade practices in foreign countries. And in
doing so, Congress set the important policies, with the executive
branch responsible for finding facts=E2=80=94like what other countries=E2=
=80=99
trade policies were at any given moment=E2=80=94or filling in the details.
So, for example, Congress passed a statute in 1815 to repeal any
=E2=80=9Cdiscriminating duty of tonnage .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. whenever the
President=E2=80=9D was =E2=80=9Csatisfied=E2=80=9D that other countries=E2=
=80=99 =E2=80=9Cdiscriminating or
countervailing duties=E2=80=9D had =E2=80=9Cbeen abolished.=E2=80=9D Act of=
 Mar. 3, 1815,
ch. 77, 3Stat. 224; see also, <em>e.g.</em>, Act of Jan. 7, 1824,
4Stat. 2=E2=80=933.<p></p>

															Given this history, it=E2=80=99s no surprise that the
dissent relies mostly on statutes and cases after 1890.
<em>Post</em>, at 59. But even they do little to support its claim.
<em>J. W. Hampton, Jr., &amp; Co.</em> v. <em>United States</em>,
<a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/=
us/276/394/">276 U.S.
394</a> (1928), for example, involved a law instructing the
President to =E2=80=9Cinvestigat[e]=E2=80=9D the costs of production for Am=
erican
firms and their foreign counterparts and issue tariffs to
=E2=80=9Cequalize=E2=80=9D those costs. <em>Id</em>., at 401, 409 (internal=
 quotation
marks omitted). The statute the Court faced in <em>Marshall Field
&amp; Co.</em> v. <em>Clark</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https:/=
/supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/143/649/">143 U.S.
649</a>, 681 (1892), spoke similarly. Even when <em>Federal Energy
Administration</em> v. <em>Algonquin SNG, Inc.</em>, <a class=3D"related-ca=
se" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/426/548/">426 U.S.
548</a>, came along in 1976, the Court upheld President Ford=E2=80=99s
imposition of monetary exactions on a single class of products
under a statute that provided at least some guidance about how he
should implement the law. <em>Id</em>., at 559. And whether correctly
decided or not, that case lies a far step from this one.<p></p>

															Before us, the President insists he may use
IEEPA to equalize foreign and domestic duties=E2=80=94or not. He may use it
to negotiate with foreign countries=E2=80=94or not. He may set tariffs at 1
percent or 1,000,000 percent. He may target one nation and one
product or every nation and nearly every product. And he may change
his mind at any time for nearly any reason. At least as I see it,
history dating =E2=80=9Cback to near the Founding,=E2=80=9D <em>post</em>, =
at 59,
does not support the notion that Presidents have traditionally
enjoyed so much power. More nearly, history refutes it.[<a href=3D"https://=
supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F6" name=3D"T6" id=3D"T6">=
6</a>]<p></p>

															IV<p></p>

															That leaves one final camp to consider.
Justice Thomas suggests that Congress may hand over most of its
constitutionally vested powers to the President completely and
forever. <em>Post</em>, at 2=E2=80=933 (dissenting opinion). On his view, t=
he
only powers Congress may not delegate are those that involve =E2=80=9Crules
setting the conditions for deprivations of life, liberty, or
property.=E2=80=9D <em>Ibid.</em> From this rule, it follows that Congress
may give all its tariff powers to the President because
=E2=80=9C[i]mporting is a matter of privilege.=E2=80=9D <em>Post</em>, at 1=
0=E2=80=9311. And,
as a result, this case does not implicate any
=E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98&nbsp;=E2=80=9Cseparation of powers=E2=80=9D&nbsp;=
=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D concerns at all.
<em>Post</em>, at 3 (quoting <em>ante,</em> at 8).<p></p>

															It=E2=80=99s a sweeping theory. One that would require
us to reimagine much of our case law addressing Article I=E2=80=99s Vesting
Clause. And one that presents difficulties of its own.<p></p>

															First, I do not see how Justice Thomas=E2=80=99s theory
resolves all =E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98&nbsp;=E2=80=9Cseparation of powers=E2=
=80=9D&nbsp;=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D
concerns in this case. <em>Post</em>, at 3 (quoting <em>ante,</em> at
8). Suppose for argument=E2=80=99s sake that Congress <em>can</em> delegate
its tariff powers to the President as completely as Justice Thomas
suggests. Even then, the question remains whether Congress
<em>has</em> given the President the tariff authority he claims in
this case=E2=80=94or whether the President is seeking to exploit
questionable statutory language to aggrandize his own power. See
Part I=E2=80=93C, <em>supra</em>. Put another way, Justice Thomas=E2=80=99s
nondelegation solution does not automatically solve the major
questions problem. As we have seen, when an executive official
claims Congress has delegated to him some extraordinary power, the
major questions doctrine requires him to identify clear statutory
authority for its exercise=E2=80=94a standard he must satisfy even if
Congress is free to pass to him the power he seeks. <em>Post</em>, at
2=E2=80=933. In fact, this Court has previously applied, with our
colleague=E2=80=99s assent, the major questions doctrine in a case that
appears, under his present view, to involve a power that Congress
could delegate wholesale to the President. See <em>Nebraska</em>, 600
U.&nbsp;S., at 486=E2=80=93488 (involving the power to cancel federal
student loan debts, which on Justice Thomas=E2=80=99s account presumably
qualifies as a benefit or privilege, not a right to life, liberty,
or property). And, just as the major questions doctrine precluded
the executive branch=E2=80=99s assertion of power in that case, it does so
here.<p></p>

															Second, even when it comes to the nondelegation
doctrine, Justice Thomas=E2=80=99s theory raises many questions. I
appreciate that the doctrine may apply with less force in certain
areas, such as when Congress legislates in a way that implicates
one of the President=E2=80=99s inherent powers. See Part III=E2=80=93B,
<em>supra</em>; <em>Gundy</em>, 588 U.&nbsp;S., at 159 (Gorsuch, J.,
dissenting). But Justice Thomas would go much further. On his
telling, the doctrine applies only to Congress=E2=80=99s true legislative
powers, which he says include only those powers addressing the
deprivation of life, liberty, or property. As it turns out, only a
small subset of Congress=E2=80=99s enumerated powers in Article I, =C2=A78,=
 fit
that bill. See <em>post</em>, at 5=E2=80=936 (listing the powers to punish
counterfeiters, tax =E2=80=9Cinternal[ly],=E2=80=9D and regulate interstate
commerce). Only those few powers are exclusively vested in Congress
and subject to review of any kind under the nondelegation doctrine.
All =E2=80=9Cother kinds of power[s]=E2=80=9D enumerated in Article I, =C2=
=A78=E2=80=94including
the powers to borrow and spend money, declare war, and regulate
foreign trade=E2=80=94are not truly legislative and may be delegated at
will. <em>Post</em>, at 2. So Congress may hand them off to the
President completely and he has no need to worry about legal
challenges under even this Court=E2=80=99s (relatively lax) nondelegation
doctrine. No matter, too, that Congress might find itself
permanently unable to retrieve these powers. See Part I=E2=80=93C,
<em>supra</em>.<p></p>

															But if all that=E2=80=99s true, what do we make of the
Constitution=E2=80=99s text? Section 1 of Article I vests =E2=80=9C[a]ll
legislative Powers herein granted=E2=80=9D in Congress and no one else.
Section 8 proceeds to list those powers in detail and without
differentiation. Neither provision speaks of some divide between
true legislative powers touching on =E2=80=9Clife, liberty, or property=E2=
=80=9D
that are permanently vested in Congress alone and =E2=80=9Cother kinds of
power[s]=E2=80=9D that may be given away and possibly lost forever to the
President. <em>Post</em>, at 2.<p></p>

															What do we make, too, of what the founders said
about Article I both before and after the Constitution=E2=80=99s
ratification? They regularly referred to powers in Article I,
=C2=A78=E2=80=94even those that do not touch on life, liberty, or property=
=E2=80=94as
legislative in nature. At the Constitutional Convention, early
drafts described the powers to regulate =E2=80=9Cforeign=E2=80=9D commerce,=
 =E2=80=9Craise
armies,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cequip Fleets,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Ccoi[n] .&nbsp;.&n=
bsp;. money,=E2=80=9D and
=E2=80=9Cestablish post-offices=E2=80=9D as =E2=80=9Clegislative powers.=E2=
=80=9D 2 The Records of
the Federal Convention of 1787, pp. 142=E2=80=93144 (M. Farrand ed. 1966)
(Farrand). James Madison wrote to Congress in 1817 that =E2=80=9C[t]he
legislative powers vested in Congress are specified and enumerated
in the eighth section of the first article of the Constitution.=E2=80=9D 8
The Writings of James Madison 386 (G. Hunt ed. 1908); see also 1
<em>id</em>., at 112, 133, 381 (noting, before the Constitutional
Convention, the =E2=80=9Clegislative power over captures,=E2=80=9D and argu=
ing
borrowing money is an =E2=80=9Cexclusive power of Legislation=E2=80=9D).<p>=
</p>

															Alexander Hamilton spoke similarly. 3 The Works
of Alexander Hamilton 479 (H. Lodge ed. 1904) (Lodge) (discussing
=E2=80=9C[t]he legislative power of borrowing money=E2=80=9D); 6 <em>id</em=
>., at 182
(describing =E2=80=9Cthe legislative power of regulating trade with foreign
nations=E2=80=9D); 2 <em>id</em>., at 197, 198 (calling of =E2=80=9Cthe leg=
islative
kind=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Cof a legislative nature=E2=80=9D the powers to r=
aise money and
troops, =E2=80=9Cestablish rules in all cases of capture by sea or land,=E2=
=80=9D
=E2=80=9Cregulate the alloy and value of coin,=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Cmake a=
ll laws for the
government of the army and navy=E2=80=9D). So did James Wilson. 1 Collected
Works of James Wilson 268 (K. Hall &amp; D. Hall eds. 2007)
(describing all the Senate=E2=80=99s powers as =E2=80=9Clegislative powers,=
=E2=80=9D with
the exception of the powers to try impeachments, concur in
treaties, and consent to the appointment of officers, matters
addressed outside Art. I, =C2=A78).<p></p>

															What do we make as well of early congressional
debates? In the Second Congress, for example, the House of
Representatives rejected on nondelegation grounds a proposal to
give the President a largely unfettered power to establish postal
routes, even though doing so hardly would have touched on life,
