Congress, four Representatives likewise objected on nondelegation
grounds to a bill that authorized the President to raise an army of
up to 10,000 men. 8 <em>id</em>., at 1525=E2=80=931527, 1532, 1535 (remarks
of Reps. Nicholas, Gallatin, Baldwin, and McDowell). Though the
bill ultimately passed, see Act of May 28, 1798, 1Stat. 558, it did
so apparently because it was deemed not to violate Article I=E2=80=99s
nondelegation principle=E2=80=94no Member of Congress responded that the
principle was wholly inapplicable because the delegated power was
not one that involved setting conditions for deprivations of life,
liberty, or property. See 8 Annals of Congress 1525=E2=80=931542.<p></p>

															What are we to do, too, with this Court=E2=80=99s
nondelegation precedents, which have never turned on Justice
Thomas=E2=80=99s view of life, liberty, or property? See <em>J. W. Hampton,
Jr., &amp; Co.</em>, 276 U.&nbsp;S., at 403, 409 (scrutinizing a
delegation to executive officials to set customs duties); <em>Panama
Refining Co.</em> v. <em>Ryan</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https=
://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/293/388/">293 U.S.
388</a>, 405=E2=80=93406, 422, 433 (1935) (holding unconstitutional a
delegation to executive officials to prohibit the transportation of
petroleum products in interstate and <em>foreign</em> commerce);
<em>National Broadcasting Co.</em> v. <em>United States</em>, <a class=3D"r=
elated-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/319/190/">=
319 U.S.
190</a>, 196, 214=E2=80=93215, 225=E2=80=93226 (1943) (scrutinizing the del=
egation
of authority to regulate the granting of broadcasting licenses);
see also <em>Sessions</em> v. <em>Dimaya</em>, 584 U.S. 148, 217 (2018)
(Thomas, J., dissenting) (=E2=80=9C[I]mpermissible delegations of
legislative power violate [the nondelegation] principle, not just
delegations that deprive individuals of =E2=80=98life, liberty, or
property=E2=80=99=E2=80=9D).<p></p>

															Third, even if a distinction between true
legislative powers and =E2=80=9Cother kinds of power[s]=E2=80=9D were prope=
r,
<em>post</em>, at 2, I do not see why the tariff power would fall in
the latter category and thus be something Congress could delegate
away wholesale, without scrutiny, and forever. Justice Thomas
suggests all that is possible because, at the founding, the tariff
power was considered a =E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98prerogative right=E2=80=99&n=
bsp;=E2=80=9D of the
British King. <em>Post</em>, at 11 (quoting N. Gras, Early English
Customs System 21 (1918)).<p></p>

															That seems doubtful. Tariffs may have been among
the King=E2=80=99s prerogative powers during the reign of Edward I. See
<em>id</em>., at 20=E2=80=9321; see also <em>post</em>, at 11, n. 3 (citing=
 P.
Einzig, The Control of the Purse: Progress and Decline of
Parliament=E2=80=99s Financial Control 65 (1959) (discussing the practices
=E2=80=9Cduring the Middle Ages=E2=80=9D)). But even before the year 1400,
Parliament had achieved some =E2=80=9Cvictory over the King in the matter
of imposing import duties.=E2=80=9D <em>Id</em>., at 108=E2=80=93109. And a=
fter the
Glorious Revolution of 1688, as this Court has put it, Parliament
=E2=80=9Csecured supremacy in fiscal matters.=E2=80=9D <em>Consumer Financi=
al
Protection Bureau</em> v. <em>Community Financial Services Assn. of
America, Ltd.</em>, 601 U.S. 416, 428 (2024) (citing 1 W.
Blackstone, Commentaries on the Laws of England 306, 333 (1771)).
=E2=80=9CBy the time of the American Revolution, trade regulation was thus
a prime topic of <em>legislative</em> concern=E2=80=9D in Britain. M.
McConnell, The President Who Would Not Be King 217 (2020) (emphasis
added); see also J. Chitty, Law of the Prerogatives of the Crown
163 (1820) (=E2=80=9C[T]he King does not possess any general common law
prerogative with respect to foreign commerce=E2=80=9D).<p></p>

															More importantly still, whatever the views in
Britain may have been, American revolutionaries hardly shared some
universal conviction that all manner of tariffs were a matter of
the King=E2=80=99s prerogative, or even something Parliament, lacking
colonial representatives, could freely impose on them. Though in
the mid-1760s some colonists distinguished between
=E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98internal=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9C&nbsp=
;=E2=80=98external=E2=80=99 taxation=E2=80=9D and
=E2=80=9Cconceded [Parliament=E2=80=99s] right to raise revenue through dut=
ies on
trade,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cthe inadequacy of [that] much overstrained distinc=
tion=E2=80=9D
soon =E2=80=9Cbecame obvious.=E2=80=9D B. Bailyn, The Ideological Origins o=
f the
American Revolution 212=E2=80=93213, 215 (1967). Illustrative of the point,
John Dickinson came to =E2=80=9Crepudiat[e]=E2=80=9D the distinction =E2=80=
=9Cflatly and
formally=E2=80=9D in his Letters from a Farmer in Pennsylvania, <em>id</em>=
.,
at 215, contending instead that laws aimed at raising revenue, but
enacted without representation, were objectionable without
=E2=80=9Cdistinction .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. between internal and external taxes,=
=E2=80=9D
Letters From a Farmer in Pennsylvania 39 (1774). See also
<em>supra</em>, at 36=E2=80=9337 (recounting colonial objections to the Sug=
ar
Act); H. Unger, American Tempest 101 (2011) (observing that the
=E2=80=9Cimport duties=E2=80=9D in the Townshend Acts helped =E2=80=9Cincit=
e Americans to
rebel=E2=80=9D). And, of course, it was duties on foreign tea that
triggered the Boston Tea Party. J. Ellis, The Cause 17=E2=80=9318 (2021).
Are we really to believe that the patriots that night in Boston
Harbor considered the whole of the tariff power some kingly
prerogative?<p></p>

															As we have already seen, too, the growing
American conviction that the peacetime tariff power is legislative
and belongs only to the people=E2=80=99s elected representatives was later
reflected in both the Constitution and early congressional
practice. See Part III=E2=80=93C, <em>supra</em>. To that discussion, I wou=
ld
add just this. The Articles of Confederation granted the
Confederation Congress authority to make commercial treaties, but
no authority to restrain =E2=80=9Cthe <em>legislative power</em> of the
respective states=E2=80=9D to impose =E2=80=9Cimposts and duties <em>on
foreigners</em>.=E2=80=9D Art. IX (emphasis added). At the Constitutional
Convention that followed, where the tariff power was transferred to
the federal government, delegates likewise referred to it as a
=E2=80=9Clegislative power.=E2=80=9D See, <em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., 3 Farrand=
 615; 2
<em>id</em>., at 142=E2=80=93143. And, during debates over the Jay Treaty,
Hamilton explained that he held no doubt that regulating foreign
trade and raising money from it was a =E2=80=9Clegislative power,=E2=80=9D =
if one
that could be constrained by treaty. 6 Lodge 182, 189=E2=80=93190, 196.
Reflecting the same sentiment that helped fuel the Revolution, he
asked: =E2=80=9C[W]hat legislative power can be more sacred?=E2=80=9D <em>I=
d</em>.,
at 196.<p></p>

															*<p></p>

															For those who think it important for the
Nation to impose more tariffs, I understand that today=E2=80=99s decision
will be disappointing. All I can offer them is that most major
decisions affecting the rights and responsibilities of the American
people (including the duty to pay taxes and tariffs) are funneled
through the legislative process for a reason. Yes, legislating can
be hard and take time. And, yes, it can be tempting to bypass
Congress when some pressing problem arises. But the deliberative
nature of the legislative process was the whole point of its
design. Through that process, the Nation can tap the combined
wisdom of the people=E2=80=99s elected representatives, not just that of
one faction or man. There, deliberation tempers impulse, and
compromise hammers disagreements into workable solutions. And
because laws must earn such broad support to survive the
legislative process, they tend to endure, allowing ordinary people
to plan their lives in ways they cannot when the rules shift from
day to day. In all, the legislative process helps ensure each of us
has a stake in the laws that govern us and in the Nation=E2=80=99s future.
For some today, the weight of those virtues is apparent. For
others, it may not seem so obvious. But if history is any guide,
the tables will turn and the day will come when those disappointed
by today=E2=80=99s result will appreciate the legislative process for the
bulwark of liberty it is.<p></p>
