														=09

															SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES<p></p>

															_________________<p></p>

															Nos. 24=E2=80=931287 and 25=E2=80=93250<p></p>

															_________________<p></p>

															LEARNING RESOURCES, INC., et&nbsp;al.,
PETITIONERS<p></p>

															24=E2=80=931287<em>v.</em><p></p>

															DONALD J. TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE
UNITED STATES, et&nbsp;al.<p></p>

															on writ of certiorari to the united states
court of appeals for the district of columbia circuit<p></p>

															<p></p>

															DONALD J. TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED
STATES, et&nbsp;al., PETITIONERS<p></p>

															25=E2=80=93250<em>v.</em><p></p>

															V.O.S. SELECTIONS, INC.,
et&nbsp;al.<p></p>

															on writ of certiorari to the united states
court of appeals for the federal circuit<p></p>

															[February 20, 2026]<p></p>

															<p></p>

															Justice Thomas, dissenting.<p></p>

															I join Justice Kavanaugh=E2=80=99s principal dissent in
full. As he explains, the Court=E2=80=99s decision today cannot be
justified as a matter of statutory interpretation. Congress
authorized the President to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation.=
=E2=80=9D
50 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71702(a)(1)(B). Throughout American history,
the authority to =E2=80=9Cregulate importation=E2=80=9D has been understood=
 to
include the authority to impose duties on imports. <em>Post</em>, at
9=E2=80=9313, 22=E2=80=9329 (Kavanaugh, J., dissenting). The meaning of tha=
t phrase
was beyond doubt by the time that Congress enacted this statute,
shortly after President Nixon=E2=80=99s highly publicized duties on imports
were upheld based on identical language. <em>Post</em>, at 14=E2=80=9322. T=
he
statute that the President relied on therefore authorized him to
impose the duties on imports at issue in these cases. Justice
Kavanaugh makes clear that the Court errs in concluding
otherwise.<p></p>

															I write separately to explain why the statute at
issue here is consistent with the separation of powers as an
original matter. The Constitution=E2=80=99s separation of powers forbids
Congress from delegating core legislative power to the President.
This principle, known as the nondelegation doctrine, is rooted in
the Constitution=E2=80=99s Legislative Vesting Clause and Due Process
Clause. Art. I, =C2=A71; Amdt. 5. Both Clauses forbid Congress from
delegating core legislative power, which is the power to make
substantive rules setting the conditions for deprivations of life,
liberty, or property. Neither Clause prohibits Congress from
delegating other kinds of power. Because the Constitution assigns
Congress many powers that do not implicate the nondelegation
doctrine, Congress may delegate the exercise of many powers to the
President. Congress has done so repeatedly since the founding, with
this Court=E2=80=99s blessing.<p></p>

															The power to impose duties on imports can be
delegated.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-12=
87/#F1" name=3D"T1" id=3D"T1">1</a>] At the founding,
that power was regarded as one of many powers over foreign commerce
that could be delegated to the President. Power over foreign
commerce was not within the core legislative power, and engaging in
foreign commerce was regarded as a privilege rather than a right.
Early Congresses often delegated to the President power to regulate
foreign commerce, including through duties on imports. As I
suggested over a decade ago, the nondelegation doctrine does not
apply to =E2=80=9Ca delegation of power to make rules governing private
conduct in the area of foreign trade,=E2=80=9D including rules imposing
duties on imports. <em>Department of Transportation</em> v.
<em>Association of American Railroads</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=
=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/575/43/">575 U.S.
43</a>, 80=E2=80=9381, n. 5 (2015) (opinion concurring in judgment).
Therefore, to the extent that the Court relies on
=E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98separation of powers principles=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=
=80=9D to rule against the
President, <em>ante,</em> at 8 (opinion of Roberts, C.&nbsp;J.), it
is mistaken.<p></p>

															I<p></p>

															The nondelegation doctrine is rooted in both
the Legislative Vesting Clause and the Due Process Clause. The
doctrine ensures that =E2=80=9C[t]he Legislative [Branch] cannot transfer
the Power of Making Laws to any other hands.=E2=80=9D J. Locke, Two
Treatises of Government =C2=A7141, p. 380 (P. Laslett ed. 1964) (Locke)
(emphasis deleted). Importantly, however, the nondelegation
doctrine applies only to Congress=E2=80=99s core legislative power, not to
all of its powers.<p></p>

															A<p></p>

															The Legislative Vesting Clause grants Congress
alone the federal legislative power. It requires that =E2=80=9C[a]ll
legislative Powers=E2=80=9D granted to the Federal Government =E2=80=9Cshal=
l be
vested in a Congress of the United States.=E2=80=9D Art. I, =C2=A71. It fol=
lows
that those federal legislative powers cannot be exercised by anyone
else, including the President. See <em>Association of American
Railroads</em>, 575 U.&nbsp;S., at 74 (opinion of Thomas, J.).<p></p>

															=E2=80=9CLegislative power=E2=80=9D for purposes of the Vest=
ing
Clause means the power to make substantive rules setting the
conditions for deprivations of life, liberty, or property. I have
described this power as the =E2=80=9Ccore legislative power=E2=80=9D to dis=
tinguish
it from other powers that the Constitution grants Congress.
<em>Id</em>., at 80. Core legislative power includes only the power
to make =E2=80=9Claw=E2=80=9D in the =E2=80=9CBlackstonian sense of general=
ly applicable
rules of private conduct,=E2=80=9D the violation of which results in the
deprivation of =E2=80=9Ccore private rights.=E2=80=9D <em>Id</em>., at 73, =
76. These
core private rights are the natural rights to life, liberty, and
property. See 1 W. Blackstone, Commentaries on the Laws of England
123=E2=80=93136 (1765) (Blackstone); C. Nelson, Adjudication in the
Political Branches, 107 Colum. L.&nbsp;Rev. 559, 566=E2=80=93567
(2007).<p></p>

															The nondelegation doctrine is also rooted in the
Due Process Clause. That Clause prohibits the Federal Government
from depriving any person of =E2=80=9Clife, liberty, or property, without
due process of law.=E2=80=9D Amdt. 5. The Founders modeled it on chapter 39
of the Magna Carta, which prohibited the deprivation of a free
man=E2=80=99s private rights =E2=80=9Cexcept by the lawful judgment of his =
peers
and by the law of the land.=E2=80=9D A. Howard, Magna Carta: Text and
Commentary 45 (rev. ed. 1998); see <em>Obergefell</em> v.
<em>Hodges</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.c=
om/cases/federal/us/576/644/">576 U.S.
644</a>, 723 (2015) (Thomas, J., dissenting). By the founding, the
Magna Carta was understood to mean that =E2=80=9Cno subject would be
deprived of a private right=E2=80=94that is, a right of life, liberty, or
property=E2=80=94except in accordance with =E2=80=98the law of the land,=E2=
=80=99 which
consisted only of statutory and common law.=E2=80=9D <em>Association of
American Railroads</em>, 575 U.&nbsp;S., at 72 (opinion of Thomas,
J.) (citing N. Chapman &amp; M. McConnell, Due Process as
Separation of Powers, 121 Yale L. J. 1672, 1688 (2012)).<p></p>

															A rule made by someone other than the
legislature, such as the King, was not =E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98the law of t=
he
land.=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D <em>Association of American Railroads</em>, 5=
75
U.&nbsp;S., at 72 (opinion of Thomas, J.). Chief Justice Coke
famously held invalid the King=E2=80=99s proclamation prohibiting new
buildings in London because the King could not =E2=80=9Ccreate any offence=
=E2=80=9D
=E2=80=9Cwithout Parliament.=E2=80=9D <em>Case of Proclamations</em>, 12 Co=
. Rep. 74,
74=E2=80=9375, 77 Eng. Rep. 1352, 1353 (K. B. 1611); see <em>Association of
American Railroads</em>, 575 U.&nbsp;S., at 72 (opinion of Thomas,
J.) (explaining that this principle was associated with chapter 39
of the Magna Carta). When the Founders transplanted the same
principle into the Due Process Clause, they ensured that when the
government wanted to deprive people of the familiar core private
rights of =E2=80=9Clife, liberty, and property,=E2=80=9D it could not do so=
 =E2=80=9Con the
basis of a rule (or a will) not enacted by the legislature.=E2=80=9D
<em>Id.</em>, at 75=E2=80=9376.<p></p>

															B<p></p>

															Neither the Legislative Vesting Clause nor the
Due Process Clause forbids Congress from delegating its other
powers. As this Court put it two centuries ago, although Congress
cannot delegate powers that are =E2=80=9Cstrictly and exclusively
legislative,=E2=80=9D it can =E2=80=9Ccertainly delegate=E2=80=9D others. <=
em>Wayman</em> v.
<em>Southard</em>, 10 Wheat. 1, 42=E2=80=9343 (1825) (opinion for the Court
by Marshall, C.&nbsp;J.).<p></p>

															Many of Congress=E2=80=99s powers fall within the core
legislative power subject to the nondelegation doctrine. For
example, the Constitution gives Congress the power to regulate
commerce among the States. Art. I, =C2=A78, cl.&nbsp;3. Congress can
thus make substantive rules for interstate trade=E2=80=94such as by
restricting drug shipments across state lines=E2=80=94punishable with fines
or imprisonment. Cf. <em>Gonzales</em> v. <em>Raich</em>, <a class=3D"relat=
ed-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/545/1/">545 U.=
S.
1</a>, 58 (2005) (Thomas, J., dissenting). Likewise, the
Constitution gives Congress many other powers that implicate life,
liberty, and property, including the power to provide for the
punishment of counterfeiting, Art. I, =C2=A78, cl.&nbsp;6; the power to
provide for the punishment of treason, Art.&nbsp;III, =C2=A73,
cl.&nbsp;2; and the power to impose internal taxes, Art. I, =C2=A78,
cl.&nbsp;1; Amdt. 16. These powers cannot be delegated, as I have
repeatedly explained. See, <em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., <em>Association of
American Railroads</em>, 575 U.&nbsp;S., at 77 (opinion of Thomas,
J.); <em>Whitman</em> v. <em>American Trucking Assns., Inc.</em>,
<a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/=
us/531/457/">531 U.S.
457</a>, 487 (2001) (Thomas, J., concurring). They cannot be
delegated even if Congress delegates them unambiguously. Cf.
<em>ante</em>, at 8 (opinion of Roberts, C.&nbsp;J.).<p></p>

															Congress also has many powers that are not
subject to the nondelegation doctrine. =E2=80=9CWe now think of the powers
listed in Article I, Section 8 as quintessentially legislative
powers, but many of them were actual, former, or asserted powers of
the Crown, which the drafters decided to allocate to the
legislative branch.=E2=80=9D M. McConnell, The President Who Would Not Be
King 274 (2020) (McConnell); accord, <em>Zivotofsky</em> v.
<em>Kerry</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.co=
m/cases/federal/us/576/1/">576 U.S.
1</a>, 36 (2015) (Thomas, J., concurring in judgment in part and
dissenting in part). These include the powers to raise and support
armies, to fix the standards of weights and measures, to grant
copyrights, to dispose of federal property, and, as discussed
below, to regulate foreign commerce. Art. I, =C2=A78; Art.&nbsp;IV, =C2=A73=
.
None of these powers involves setting the rules for the deprivation
of core private rights. Blackstone called them =E2=80=9Cprerogative=E2=80=
=9D
powers, and sometimes =E2=80=9Cexecutive.=E2=80=9D See 1 Blackstone 242, 24=
5,
255=E2=80=93262, 264=E2=80=93265, 276, 279; 2 <em>id</em>., at 407, 410 (17=
66); 1 W.
Crosskey, Politics and the Constitution in the History of the
United States 416, 421=E2=80=93425 (1953); McConnell 274=E2=80=93275. By on=
e count,
13 of the 29 powers given to Congress in Article I were powers that
=E2=80=9CBlackstone described as =E2=80=98executive=E2=80=99 powers.=E2=80=
=9D 1 Crosskey, Politics
and the Constitution, at 428.<p></p>

															For most of American history, the nondelegation
doctrine was understood not to apply to these powers. Contra,
<em>ante,</em> at 42=E2=80=9346 (Gorsuch, J., concurring). =E2=80=9CThe ear=
ly
congresses felt free to delegate certain powers to President
Washington in broad terms.=E2=80=9D McConnell 333. Thus, the Constitution
gives Congress the power to support armies, Art. I, =C2=A78, cl. 12, but
Congress in 1789 delegated to the President the power to establish
regulations for benefits to veterans wounded in the Revolutionary
War. See Act of Sept. 29, 1789, ch. 24, 1Stat. 95. The Constitution
gives Congress the power to grant patents, Art. I, =C2=A78, cl.&nbsp;8,
but Congress in 1790 delegated to executive officials the power to
grant patents in their discretion. See Act of Apr. 10, 1790, ch. 7,
=C2=A71, 1Stat. 109=E2=80=93110. The Constitution gives Congress the power =
to
borrow money, Art. I, =C2=A78, cl.&nbsp;2, but Congress in 1790
delegated to the President the power to borrow up to $12 million on
behalf of the United States in his discretion. See Act of Aug. 4,
1790, =C2=A72, 1Stat. 139. The Constitution gives Congress the power to
raise armies, Art. I, =C2=A78, cl. 12, but Congress in 1791 delegated to
the President the power to raise an army of 2,000 troops in his
discretion. See Act of Mar. 3, 1791, =C2=A78, 1Stat. 223. And, as I
explain further below, see <em>infra</em>, at 13=E2=80=9315, the Constituti=
on
gives Congress the power to regulate foreign commerce, Art. I, =C2=A78,
cl. 3, but early Congresses often delegated to the President the
power to regulate foreign commerce. See, <em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., Act of
July 22, 1790, ch. 33, 1Stat. 137; Act of June 4, 1794, ch. 41,
1Stat. 372.<p></p>

															These early delegations had one thing in common:
They did not implicate the Legislative Vesting Clause or the Due
Process Clause. =E2=80=9CNone of these statutes disturbed natural rights or
intruded into the core of the legislative power.=E2=80=9D McConnell 333;
cf. A. Bamzai, Comment, Delegation and Interpretive Discretion:
<em>Gundy</em>, <em>Kisor</em>, and the Formation and Future of
Administrative Law, 133 Harv. L. Rev. 164, 178 (2019). They
therefore did not violate the nondelegation doctrine.<p></p>

															The Constitutional Convention seemed to agree
with this understanding of delegation. Contra, <em>ante</em>, at 42
(Gorsuch, J., concurring). James Madison proposed an amendment
clarifying that the President had the power =E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98to exec=
ute such
other powers=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D as were =E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98delega=
ted by the national
Legislature,=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D so long as the delegated powers were
=E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98&nbsp;=E2=80=9Cnot Legislative nor Judiciary in the=
ir
nature.=E2=80=9D&nbsp;=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D 1 Records of the Federal Con=
vention of 1787,
p. 67 (M. Farrand ed. 1966). Thus, in Madison=E2=80=99s view, some of
Congress=E2=80=99s powers were =E2=80=9Cnot Legislative=E2=80=9D and could =
be =E2=80=9Cdelegated=E2=80=9D
to the President. <em>Ibid.</em> Madison=E2=80=99s proposal was rejected
after others argued that it was unnecessary. <em>Ibid.</em> Madison
agreed that the purpose of the proposed amendment was only to
=E2=80=9Cprevent doubts and misconstructions.=E2=80=9D <em>Ibid.</em> Nobod=
y disputed
that Madison stated the correct scope of the nondelegation
doctrine. <em>Ibid</em>.; see also McConnell 332 (=E2=80=9C[W]e can infer
[from Madison=E2=80=99s motion] that the framers understood that Congress
would be able to delegate its royal prerogative powers back to the
President=E2=80=9D).[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us=
/607/24-1287/#F2" name=3D"T2" id=3D"T2">2</a>]<p></p>

															II<p></p>

															As a matter of original understanding,
historical practice, and judicial precedent, the power to impose
duties on imports is not within the core legislative power.
Congress can therefore delegate the exercise of this power to the
President.<p></p>

															A<p></p>

															Neither of the two constitutional foundations
for the nondelegation doctrine forbids Congress from delegating to
the President the power to impose duties on imports.<p></p>

															1<p></p>

															The Legislative Vesting Clause provides no
basis for applying the nondelegation doctrine to the power to
impose duties on imports.<p></p>

															=E2=80=9CThe =E2=80=98power over external affairs [is] in or=
igin
and essential character different from that over internal
affairs.=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D <em>Haaland</em> v. <em>Brackeen</em>, 599=
 U.S. 255,
356 (2023) (Thomas, J., dissenting) (quoting <em>United States</em>
v. <em>Curtiss-Wright Export Corp.</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"=
https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/299/304/">299
U.S. 304</a>, 319 (1936)). Although internal affairs are governed
by the domestic law of one sovereign, external affairs implicate
the relationship between sovereigns, which is subject to the law of
nations. See Locke =C2=A7=C2=A7145=E2=80=93148, at 383=E2=80=93384; 1 Black=
stone 264; 4
<em>id</em>., at 66=E2=80=9368 (1769); E. de Vattel, The Law of Nations
161=E2=80=93163, 281=E2=80=93289 (J. Chitty ed. 1852) (Vattel). External af=
fairs,
then, are not susceptible to being =E2=80=9Cdirected by antecedent,
standing, positive Laws=E2=80=9D made by one nation. Locke =C2=A7147, at 38=
4.
When a person goes abroad, he must resort to the political branches
(and ultimately the military)=E2=80=94rather than the judiciary=E2=80=94for
protection, can indebt the executive to foreign nations for his
personal misconduct, and can trigger a foreign conflict. See Vattel
161=E2=80=93163, 281=E2=80=93289; 2 F. Wharton, Digest of International Law=
 =C2=A7222,
pp. 575=E2=80=93576 (2d ed. 1887); see also <em>id</em>., =C2=A7=C2=A7189, =
213=E2=80=93221, at
432=E2=80=93445, 539=E2=80=93575.<p></p>

															The power to regulate external affairs was
accordingly not viewed as within the core legislative power at the
founding. See <em>Zivotofsky</em>, 576 U.&nbsp;S., at 35=E2=80=9337 (opinio=
n
of Thomas, J.). Blackstone described powers over =E2=80=9Cintercourse with
foreign nations=E2=80=9D as =E2=80=9Cprerogative=E2=80=9D powers naturally =
belonging to the
King. 1 Blackstone 245; see <em>id</em>., at 232. Locke agreed that
this power =E2=80=9Cmust be lodged=E2=80=9D with the =E2=80=9Cexecutive.=E2=
=80=9D
<em>Zivotofsky</em>, 576 U.&nbsp;S., at 35 (opinion of Thomas, J.)
(citing Locke =C2=A7148). Baron de Montesquieu classified all powers =E2=80=
=9Cin
respect to things dependent on the law of nations=E2=80=9D as part of =E2=
=80=9Cthe
executive power.=E2=80=9D 1 The Spirit of Laws 151 (T. Nugent transl., rev.
ed. 1899). The =E2=80=9Clegislative=E2=80=9D power, by contrast, =E2=80=9Ca=
pplied only
within the realm.=E2=80=9D McConnell 214.<p></p>

															The power to regulate external affairs included
power over foreign commerce. At the founding, the =E2=80=9Cexternal
executive power=E2=80=9D included =E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98the transactions =
of the state
with any other independent state.=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D <em>Zivotofsky</e=
m>, 576
U.&nbsp;S., at 36 (opinion of Thomas, J.). In Great Britain, the
King had no unilateral legislative power, McConnell 107, but he had
much unilateral power over foreign commerce. His power over foreign
commerce included the power to =E2=80=9Cgovern foreign trade,=E2=80=9D <em>=
id</em>.,
at 216, and to =E2=80=9Cprohibit any of his subjects from leaving the
realm,=E2=80=9D 1 Blackstone 261; accord, <em>East India Co.</em> v.
<em>Sandys</em>, Skin. 223, 223=E2=80=93224, 90 Eng. Rep. 103 (K.&nbsp;B.
1684) (describing the =E2=80=9Cinherent prerogative in the Crown, that none
should trade with foreigners without the King=E2=80=99s licence=E2=80=9D). =
Thomas
Rutherforth=E2=80=99s Institutes of Natural Law=E2=80=94=E2=80=9Ca treatise=
 routinely cited
by the Founders,=E2=80=9D <em>Zivotofsky</em>, 576 U.&nbsp;S., at 36 (opini=
on
of Thomas, J.)=E2=80=94explained that the =E2=80=9Cexternal executive power=
=E2=80=9D
included =E2=80=9Cthe power of adjusting the rights of a nation in respect
of .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. trade.=E2=80=9D 2 Institutes of Natural Law 55=E2=80=935=
6
(1756); accord, Locke =C2=A7146, at 383. The power to impose duties on
imports was a conventional method for governing foreign trade. It
originated as a =E2=80=9Cprerogative right=E2=80=9D of the King, N. Gras, E=
arly
English Customs System 21 (1918).[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cas=
es/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F3" name=3D"T3" id=3D"T3">3</a>]<p></p>

															2<p></p>

															The Due Process Clause likewise provides no
basis for applying the nondelegation doctrine to the power to
impose duties on imports. The Due Process Clause protects =E2=80=9Crights,=
=E2=80=9D
not =E2=80=9Cprivileges.=E2=80=9D <em>Gutierrez</em> v. <em>Saenz</em>, 606=
 U.S. 305,
331 (2025) (Thomas, J., dissenting). Importing is a matter of
privilege.<p></p>

															The government can charge money for privileges
without depriving a person of property for due-process purposes.
The government charges people money every day for a wide range of
activities, such as to enter a government park, mail an envelope,
apply for a copyright, or file a lawsuit. Because a person has no
core private right to engage in these activities, the government is
not subject to due-process restraints in setting such charges. The
due-process question is not whether a government action
=E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98raise[s] revenue,=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D <em>ante,=
</em> at 6 (majority
opinion), but whether it implicates core private rights.
<em>Supra</em>, at 3=E2=80=934. Thus, when Congress delegates power to make
=E2=80=9Cregulations=E2=80=9D on federal land, the Secretary of Agriculture=
 can set
a =E2=80=9Ccharge=E2=80=9D for the =E2=80=9Cprivilege of grazing sheep=E2=
=80=9D on that land
without thereby =E2=80=9Cexercis[ing] the legislative power.=E2=80=9D <em>U=
nited
States</em> v. <em>Grimaud</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://=
supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/220/506/">220 U.S.
506</a>, 522=E2=80=93523 (1911); see also Bamzai, 133 Harv. L.&nbsp;Rev.,
at 180=E2=80=93182; contra, <em>ante,</em> at 8 (opinion of Roberts,
C.&nbsp;J.). Congress has, consistent with due process, delegated
the power to set charges for a wide range of privileges. See 16
U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A76802 (delegating the power to set fees for
entrance to and use of federal recreation areas); 17
U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71316 (delegating the power to =E2=80=9Cby regulati=
on
set reasonable fees=E2=80=9D for applications); 39 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=
=A73622
(delegating the power to set postal rates); 28 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C.
=C2=A71911 (=E2=80=9CThe Supreme Court may fix the fees to be charged by it=
s
clerk=E2=80=9D).<p></p>

															A person had no core private right to import
goods at the founding. On the Founders=E2=80=99 understanding, statutes
allowing =E2=80=9Cimportation of goods from abroad were thought to create
mere privileges rather than core private rights.=E2=80=9D Nelson, 107
Colum. L. Rev., at 580. Foreign commerce was governed by the law of
nations, which is a law of =E2=80=9Csovereigns,=E2=80=9D not of =E2=80=9Cpr=
ivate
individuals.=E2=80=9D Vattel 285. =E2=80=9C[A]ny attempt to introduce forei=
gn
goods=E2=80=9D without the =E2=80=9Cexpressed allowances=E2=80=9D of the so=
vereign was =E2=80=9Ca
violation of its sovereignty.=E2=80=9D <em>Cross</em> v. <em>Harrison</em>,=
 16
How. 164, 196 (1854). =E2=80=9CEvery state=E2=80=9D had =E2=80=9Ca right to=
 prohibit the
entrance of <em>foreign merchandises</em>,=E2=80=9D including through the
imposition of duties on imports. Vattel =C2=A7=C2=A790, 99, at 38, 43.
Because =E2=80=9Cno one had a vested right to import=E2=80=9D any =E2=80=9C=
goods from
abroad,=E2=80=9D the imposition of =E2=80=9Ctariffs=E2=80=9D as a condition=
 for importing
those goods did not implicate the Due Process Clause any more than
when the government charges money for other privileges. Nelson, 107
Colum. L. Rev., at 580.<p></p>

															*=E2=80=83=E2=80=83*=E2=80=83=E2=80=83*<p></p>

															The power to impose duties on imports thus does
not implicate either of the constitutional foundations for the
nondelegation doctrine. Hence, even the strongest critics of
delegation, myself included, have recognized that regulations of
foreign commerce might not be subject to ordinary nondelegation
limitations. See <em>FCC</em> v. <em>Consumers=E2=80=99 Research</em>, 606 =
U.S.
656, 742, n. 19 (2025) (Gorsuch, J., dissenting) (=E2=80=9C[I]t may be
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. that tariffs and domestic taxes present different
contexts when it comes to the problem of delegation=E2=80=9D); accord,
<em>Association of American Railroads</em>, 575 U.&nbsp;S., at 80,
and n. 5 (opinion of Thomas, J.). So long as Congress complies with
other constitutional limitations, it can delegate this power.<p></p>

															B<p></p>

															Historical practice and precedent confirm that
Congress can delegate the power to impose duties on imports.<p></p>

															1<p></p>

															Since the 1790s, Congress has consistently
delegated to the President power over foreign commerce, including
the power to impose duties on imports. =E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98Practically =
every
volume of the United States Statutes=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D contains broad
delegations to the President in the area of foreign commerce.
<em>Id</em>., at 80, n. 5 (quoting <em>Curtiss-Wright Export
Corp.</em>, 299 U.&nbsp;S., at 324).<p></p>

															The First Congress gave the President the power
to =E2=80=9Cprescribe=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Crules and regulations=E2=80=9D that=
 would =E2=80=9Cgover[n]=E2=80=9D any
person licensed to trade with Indians. 1Stat. 137. Trade with
Indians was regarded as =E2=80=9Ca matter of external relations.=E2=80=9D M=
cConnell
333. In delegating this power, Congress did not specify or limit
what kinds of regulations the President could impose. Act of July
22, 1790, 1Stat. 137=E2=80=93138. Pursuant to that broad delegation, the
President restricted trading =E2=80=9C[d]istilled [s]pirits,=E2=80=9D requi=
red each
trader to =E2=80=9Cgive intelligence=E2=80=9D to the Government, and subdel=
egated
to his superintendents the power to =E2=80=9Cassign the limits within which
each trader shall trade.=E2=80=9D 61 Timothy Pickering Papers,
Massachusetts Historical Society 4 (Aug. 28, 1790); see also Letter
from G. Washington to H. Knox (Aug. 13, 1790), in 6 Papers of
George Washington 244=E2=80=93245 (D. Twohig ed. 1996). Any person who
violated the President=E2=80=99s regulations would owe $1,000 =E2=80=9Cpaya=
ble to
the President.=E2=80=9D 1Stat. 137.<p></p>

															Succeeding early Congresses delegated many more
powers over foreign commerce to the President. In 1794, Congress
delegated to the President the power to =E2=80=9Clay an embargo on all
ships and vessels in the ports of the United States,=E2=80=9D including
ships belonging to Americans, unless Congress was in session. Act
of June 4, 1794, 1Stat. 372. It authorized the President to make
=E2=80=9Csuch regulations as the circumstances of the case may require=E2=
=80=9D in
exercising that delegated power. <em>Ibid</em>. Congress allowed the
President to impose the embargo as =E2=80=9Cin his opinion, the public
safety shall so require.=E2=80=9D <em>Ibid</em>. In 1795, Congress delegate=
d
to the President the power to =E2=80=9Cpermit the exportation of arms,
cannon and military stores, the law prohibiting the exportation of
the same to the contrary notwithstanding.=E2=80=9D Act of Mar. 3, 1795, ch.
53, 1Stat. 444. In 1798, Congress delegated to the President the
power to discontinue =E2=80=9Cprohibitions and restraints=E2=80=9D on comme=
rce with
France. Act of June 13, 1798, 1Stat. 565=E2=80=93566; see also,
<em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., Act of Mar. 3, 1817, ch. 39, 3Stat. 361=E2=80=93362
(delegating to the President the power to discontinue a ban on
importation of plaster of Paris). In 1799, Congress delegated to
the President the authority to discontinue and to reimpose
=E2=80=9Crestraints and prohibitions=E2=80=9D on commerce with France when =
he
=E2=80=9Cdeem[ed] it expedient and consistent with the interest of the
United States.=E2=80=9D Act of Feb. 9, 1799, 1Stat. 615. And, in 1800,
Congress delegated to the President the power to remove a ban on
trade with France, and to =E2=80=9Cre-establish=E2=80=9D certain =E2=80=9Cr=
estraints and
prohibitions=E2=80=9D when he =E2=80=9Cdeem[ed] it expedient.=E2=80=9D Act =
of Feb. 27,
1800, 2Stat. 9=E2=80=9310.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/fede=
ral/us/607/24-1287/#F4" name=3D"T4" id=3D"T4">4</a>]<p></p>

															Congress likewise delegated to the President the
power to set duties on imports. In 1815, Congress delegated to the
President the power to lower reciprocal duties when he was
=E2=80=9Csatisfied=E2=80=9D that other nations=E2=80=99 trade practices no =
longer operated
=E2=80=9Cto the disadvantage of the United States.=E2=80=9D Act of Mar. 3, =
1815,
ch. 77, 3Stat. 224. In 1824, Congress delegated to the President
the power to lower and to reimpose duties in response to foreign
nations=E2=80=99 trade practices. See Act of Jan. 7, 1824, 4Stat. 2=E2=80=
=933.
Throughout the early decades of the Republic, Congress continued to
delegate to the President similar powers over duties on imports on
a regular basis. See, <em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., Act of May 24, 1828, ch.
111, 4Stat. 308; Act of May 31, 1830, ch. 219, 4Stat. 425; Act of
July 13, 1832, ch. 207, 4Stat. 578=E2=80=93579. Presidents frequently
changed the rates of duties on imports as to various foreign
nations pursuant to these delegations.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.co=
m/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F5" name=3D"T5" id=3D"T5">5</a>]<p></p>

															2<p></p>

															This Court has consistently upheld Congress=E2=80=99s
delegation of power over foreign commerce, including the power to
impose duties on imports.<p></p>

															The Court has long conveyed to Congress that it
may =E2=80=9Cinvest the President with large discretion in matters arising
out of the execution of statutes relating to trade and commerce
with other nations.=E2=80=9D <em>Marshall Field &amp; Co.</em> v.
<em>Clark</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.co=
m/cases/federal/us/143/649/"></a><a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://=
supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/143/649/">143
U.S. 649</a>, 691 (1892). Since shortly after the founding, the
Court has rejected challenges to delegations of power over foreign
commerce. See <em>Cargo of Brig Aurora</em> v. <em>United States</em>,
7 Cranch 382, 386, 387=E2=80=93389 (1813). Even when a =E2=80=9Cchallenged
delegation, if it were confined to internal affairs, would be
invalid,=E2=80=9D the Court has upheld the delegation. <em>Curtiss-Wright
Export Corp</em>., 299 U.&nbsp;S., at 315, 322. There is a
=E2=80=9Cfundamental=E2=80=9D difference, the Court has explained, between =
=E2=80=9Cforeign
or external affairs=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Cdomestic or internal affairs.=E2=
=80=9D
<em>Id</em>., at 315. Thus, =E2=80=9CCongress may of course delegate very
large grants of its power over foreign commerce to the President,=E2=80=9D
<em>Chicago &amp; Southern Air Lines, Inc.</em> v. <em>Waterman S. S.
Corp</em>., <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/ca=
ses/federal/us/333/103/">333 U.S.
103</a>, 109 (1948), including when it comes to imposing =E2=80=9Cduties=E2=
=80=9D
on imports, <em>Curtiss-Wright Export Corp</em>., 299 U.&nbsp;S., at
325, n.&nbsp;2.<p></p>

															When Congress has delegated to the President the
power to impose duties on imports, this Court has upheld those
delegations. In <em>Clark</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://s=
upreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/143/649/">143 U.S.
649</a>, the Court upheld Congress=E2=80=99s delegation to the President of
the power to impose duties on nations whose importation policies
=E2=80=9Che may deem to be reciprocally unequal and unreasonable.=E2=80=9D
<em>Id</em>., at 680. It explained that Congress had =E2=80=9Cfrequently,
from the organization of the government to the present time,=E2=80=9D
conferred powers over =E2=80=9Ctrade and commerce=E2=80=9D to =E2=80=9Cthe =
President.=E2=80=9D
<em>Id.</em>, at 683. In <em>J. W. Hampton, Jr., &amp; Co.</em> v.
<em>United States</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.j=
ustia.com/cases/federal/us/276/394/">276 U.S.
394</a> (1928), the Court upheld a delegation to the President to
impose duties as necessary up to statutorily limited rates to make
them reciprocal. <em>Id</em>., at 401, 409. And, in <em>Federal Energy
Administration</em> v. <em>Algonquin SNG, Inc.</em>, <a class=3D"related-ca=
se" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/426/548/">426 U.S.
548</a> (1976), the Court upheld a delegation of the power to
impose a universal duty on imported oil. <em>Id</em>., at 555,
558=E2=80=93560.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607=
/24-1287/#F6" name=3D"T6" id=3D"T6">6</a>]<p></p>

															Although these cases involved duties on imports,
the Court nowhere suggested that a different nondelegation rule
applied because the duty was a =E2=80=9Ctax=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Craise[d] r=
evenue.=E2=80=9D
<em>Ante</em>, at 6 (majority opinion) (internal quotation marks
omitted).[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-128=
7/#F7" name=3D"T7" id=3D"T7">7</a>]<p></p>

															III<p></p>

															Congress=E2=80=99s delegation here was constitutional.
The statute at issue in these cases, the International Emergency
Economic Powers Act, delegates to the President a wide range of
powers over foreign commerce. IEEPA gives the President, on
conditions satisfied here, the power to =E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D foreign
commerce, including =E2=80=9Cimportation=E2=80=9D of foreign property. 50
U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71702(a)(1)(B).<p></p>

															IEEPA=E2=80=99s delegation of power to impose duties on
imports complies with the nondelegation doctrine. Congress
delegated to the President a version of the same power that it has
delegated to him in many statutes since the early days of the
Republic. See <em>supra</em>, at 13=E2=80=9317. Congress limited that
delegation to foreign commerce. See =C2=A71702(a)(1)(B); see also =C2=A7170=
1.
In delegating the power to impose duties on imports, it gave the
President no core legislative power to make substantive rules
setting the conditions for deprivations of life, liberty, or
property. Its delegation therefore complied with the constitutional
separation of powers and is consistent with centuries of practice
and precedent. It did not need to exercise that power itself and
did not need to delegate it =E2=80=9Cunambiguously=E2=80=9D=E2=80=94even th=
ough, as Justice
Kavanaugh explains, it did. See <em>post</em>, at 38=E2=80=9345 (dissenting
opinion).<p></p>

															The principal opinion bases its decision on the
major questions doctrine. <em>Ante</em>, at 7=E2=80=9313 (opinion of Robert=
s,
C.&nbsp;J.). In some cases, the Court has used the major questions
doctrine as a canon of statutory interpretation because delegations
of major powers are unlikely to be subtle. See, <em>e</em>.<em>g</em>.,
<em>Whitman,</em> 531 U.&nbsp;S., at 468; see <em>ante,</em> at 8
(opinion of Roberts, C.&nbsp;J.); see also <em>Biden</em> v.
<em>Nebraska</em>, 600 U.S. 477, 501=E2=80=93503 (2023). In other cases, th=
e
Court has used it to avoid what would have been originally
understood as an unconstitutional delegation of legislative power.
See, <em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., <em>West Virginia</em> v. <em>EPA</em>, 597
U.S. 697, 723 (2022); <em>ante,</em> at 8 (opinion of Roberts,
C.&nbsp;J.). In today=E2=80=99s cases, neither the statutory text nor the
Constitution provide a basis for ruling against the President. I
respectfully dissent.<p></p>
