																<div id=3D"tab-opinion-5177102" class=3D"hidden-content">
						<div class=3D"-display-inline-block text-left">
														=09

															SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES<p></p>

															_________________<p></p>

															Nos. 24=E2=80=931287 and 25=E2=80=93250<p></p>

															_________________<p></p>

															LEARNING RESOURCES, INC., et&nbsp;al.,
PETITIONERS<p></p>

															24=E2=80=931287<em>v.</em><p></p>

															DONALD J. TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE
UNITED STATES, et&nbsp;al.<p></p>

															on writ of certiorari to the united states
court of appeals for the district of columbia circuit<p></p>

															<p></p>

															DONALD J. TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED
STATES, et&nbsp;al., PETITIONERS<p></p>

															25=E2=80=93250<em>v.</em><p></p>

															V.O.S. SELECTIONS, INC.,
et&nbsp;al.<p></p>

															on writ of certiorari to the united states
court of appeals for the federal circuit<p></p>

															[February 20, 2026]<p></p>

															<p></p>

															Justice Kavanaugh, with whom Justice Thomas
and Justice Alito join, dissenting.<p></p>

															Acting pursuant to his statutory authority to
=E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D under the 1977 Inter=
national
Emergency Economic Powers Act, or IEEPA, the President has imposed
tariffs on imports of foreign goods from various countries. The
tariffs have generated vigorous policy debates. Those policy
debates are not for the Federal Judiciary to resolve. Rather, the
Judiciary=E2=80=99s more limited role is to neutrally interpret and apply
the law. The sole legal question here is whether, under IEEPA,
tariffs are a means to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation.=E2=80=
=9D
Statutory text, history, and precedent demonstrate that the answer
is clearly yes: Like quotas and embargoes, tariffs are a
traditional and common tool to regulate importation.<p></p>

															Since early in U.&nbsp;S. history, Congress has
regularly authorized the President to impose tariffs on imports of
foreign goods. Presidents have often used that authority to obtain
leverage with foreign nations, help American manufacturers and
workers compete on a more level playing field, and generate revenue
for the United States. Numerous laws such as the Trade Expansion
Act of 1962 and the Trade Act of 1974 continue to authorize the
President to place tariffs on foreign imports in a variety of
circumstances, and Presidents have often done so. In recent years,
Presidents George W. Bush, Obama, and Biden have all imposed
tariffs on foreign imports under those statutory authorities.<p></p>

															President Trump has similarly imposed tariffs,
and has done so here under IEEPA. During declared national
emergencies, IEEPA broadly authorizes the President to regulate
international economic transactions. Most relevant for this case,
during those national emergencies, IEEPA grants the President the
power to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D of foreign
goods.<p></p>

															In early 2025, President Trump declared two
national emergencies pursuant to the National Emergencies Act. See
50 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71621(a). One emergency concerned drug
trafficking into the United States. The other emergency involved
trade imbalances with foreign nations that have harmed American
manufacturers and workers.<p></p>

															To help address those emergencies, the President
drew upon his authority in IEEPA to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.
importation,=E2=80=9D and he imposed tariffs on imports from various
countries.<p></p>

															The plaintiffs argue and the Court concludes
that the President lacks authority under IEEPA to impose tariffs. I
disagree. In accord with Judge Taranto=E2=80=99s careful and persuasive
opinion in the Federal Circuit, I would conclude that the
President=E2=80=99s power under IEEPA to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.
importation=E2=80=9D encompasses tariffs. As a matter of ordinary meaning,
including dictionary definitions and historical usage, the broad
power to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D includes th=
e
traditional and common means to do so=E2=80=94in particular, quotas,
embargoes, and tariffs.<p></p>

															History and precedent confirm that conclusion.
In 1971, President Nixon imposed 10 percent tariffs on almost all
foreign imports. He levied the tariffs under IEEPA=E2=80=99s predecessor
statute, the Trading with the Enemy Act, which similarly authorized
the President to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.&nbsp;importation.=E2=80=
=9D The
Nixon tariffs were upheld in court.<p></p>

															Moreover, in 1976, a year before IEEPA was
enacted, this Court unanimously ruled that a similarly worded
statute authorizing the President to =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D p=
ermitted
President Ford to impose monetary exactions on foreign oil imports.
See <em>Federal Energy Administration</em> v. <em>Algonquin SNG,
Inc.</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cas=
es/federal/us/426/548/">426 U.S.
548</a> (1976) (<em>Algonquin</em>).<p></p>

															For both the Nixon tariffs and the Ford tariffs
upheld by this Court in <em>Algonquin</em>, the relevant statutory
provisions did not specifically refer to =E2=80=9Ctariffs=E2=80=9D or =E2=
=80=9Cduties,=E2=80=9D but
instead more broadly authorized the President to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D or to =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports.=E2=
=80=9D Therefore,
when IEEPA was enacted in 1977 in the wake of the Nixon and Ford
tariffs and the <em>Algonquin</em> decision, Congress and the public
plainly would have understood that the power to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D included tariffs. If Congress wanted
to exclude tariffs from IEEPA, it surely would not have enacted the
same broad =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D language =
that had
just been used to justify major American tariffs on foreign
imports.<p></p>

															Importantly, IEEPA=E2=80=99s authorization for the
President to impose tariffs did not grant the President any new
substantive power. Since the Founding, numerous statutes have
authorized=E2=80=94and still do authorize=E2=80=94the President to impose t=
ariffs
and other foreign import restrictions. IEEPA merely allows the
President to impose tariffs somewhat more efficiently to deal with
foreign threats during national emergencies.<p></p>

															Context and common sense buttress that
interpretation of IEEPA. The plaintiffs and the Court acknowledge
that IEEPA authorizes the President to impose quotas or embargoes
on foreign imports=E2=80=94meaning that a President could completely block
some or all imports. But they say that IEEPA does not authorize the
President to employ the lesser power of tariffs, which simply
condition imports on a payment. As they interpret the statute, the
President could, for example, block all imports from China but
cannot order even a $1 tariff on goods imported from China.<p></p>

															That approach does not make much sense. Properly
read, IEEPA does not draw such an odd distinction between quotas
and embargoes on the one hand and tariffs on the other. Rather, it
empowers the President to regulate imports during national
emergencies with the tools Presidents have traditionally and
commonly used, including quotas, embargoes, and tariffs.<p></p>

															The Court today nonetheless concludes otherwise
and holds that IEEPA does not authorize the President to impose
tariffs to deal with the declared drug trafficking and trade
deficit emergencies. But the Court=E2=80=99s decision is splintered. In
today=E2=80=99s six-Justice majority, three Justices (Justice Sotomayor,
Justice Kagan, and Justice Jackson) interpret IEEPA not to
authorize tariffs as a matter of ordinary statutory interpretation.
I disagree for the reasons noted above and elaborated on at length
in this opinion.<p></p>

															Three other Justices (The Chief Justice, Justice
Gorsuch, and Justice Barrett) lean on the major questions canon of
statutory interpretation to resolve this case. That important canon
requires =E2=80=9Cclear congressional authorization=E2=80=9D for an executi=
ve
action of major economic and political significance, particularly
when the Executive exercises an =E2=80=9Cunheralded=E2=80=9D power. <em>Wes=
t
Virginia</em> v. <em>EPA</em>, 597 U.S. 697, 722=E2=80=93723 (2022) (quotat=
ion
marks omitted).<p></p>

															In my view, as I will explain, the major
questions canon does not control here for two alternative and
independent reasons.<p></p>

															First, the statutory text, history, and
precedent constitute =E2=80=9Cclear congressional authorization=E2=80=9D fo=
r the
President to impose tariffs under IEEPA. In particular, throughout
American history, Presidents have commonly imposed tariffs as a
means to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation.=E2=80=9D So tariffs=
 were
not an =E2=80=9Cunheralded=E2=80=9D power when Congress enacted IEEPA in 19=
77 and
authorized the President to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=
=E2=80=9D
of foreign goods. Therefore, the major questions doctrine is
satisfied here. Cf. <em>Biden</em> v. <em>Missouri</em>, 595 U.S. 87
(2022) (<em>per curiam</em>).<p></p>

															Second, in any event, the Court has <em>never</em>
before applied the major questions doctrine in the foreign affairs
context, including foreign trade. Rather, as Justice Robert Jackson
summarized and remains true, this Court has always recognized the
=E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98unwisdom of requiring Congress in this field of
governmental power to lay down narrowly definite standards by which
the President is to be governed.=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D <em>Youngstown She=
et &amp;
Tube Co.</em> v. <em>Sawyer</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https:/=
/supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/343/579/">343 U.S.
579</a>, 636, n.&nbsp;2 (1952) (concurring opinion) (quoting
<em>United States</em> v. <em>Curtiss-Wright Export Corp.</em>,
<a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/=
us/299/304/">299 U.S.
304</a>, 321=E2=80=93322 (1936)). In foreign affairs cases, courts read the
statute as written and do not employ the major questions doctrine
as a thumb on the scale against the President.<p></p>

															Although I firmly disagree with the Court=E2=80=99s
holding today, the decision might not substantially constrain a
President=E2=80=99s ability to order tariffs going forward. That is because
numerous other federal statutes authorize the President to impose
tariffs and might justify most (if not all) of the tariffs at issue
in this case=E2=80=94albeit perhaps with a few additional procedural steps
that IEEPA, as an emergency statute, does not require. Those
statutes include, for example, the Trade Expansion Act of 1962
(Section 232); the Trade Act of 1974 (Sections 122, 201, and 301);
and the Tariff Act of 1930 (Section 338). In essence, the Court
today concludes that the President checked the wrong statutory box
by relying on IEEPA rather than another statute to impose these
tariffs.<p></p>

															In the meantime, however, the interim effects of
the Court=E2=80=99s decision could be substantial. The United States may be
required to refund billions of dollars to importers who paid the
IEEPA tariffs, even though some importers may have already passed
on costs to consumers or others. As was acknowledged at oral
argument, the refund process is likely to be a =E2=80=9Cmess.=E2=80=9D Tr. =
of Oral
Arg. 153=E2=80=93155. In addition, according to the Government, the IEEPA
tariffs have helped facilitate trade deals worth trillions of
dollars=E2=80=94including with foreign nations from China to the United
Kingdom to Japan, and more. The Court=E2=80=99s decision could generate
uncertainty regarding those trade arrangements.<p></p>

															In any event, the only issue before the Court
today is one of law. In light of the statutory text, longstanding
historical practice, and relevant Supreme Court precedents, I would
conclude that IEEPA authorizes the President to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D by imposing tariffs on foreign imports
during declared national emergencies. I therefore respectfully
dissent.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287=
/#F2" name=3D"T2" id=3D"T2">2</a>]<p></p>

															I<p></p>

															Before turning to the specifics of IEEPA=E2=80=99s
text, history, and precedent, I briefly review several fundamental
constitutional principles about the roles of the three branches of
the U.&nbsp;S. Government with respect to this case.<p></p>

															<em>First</em>, the plaintiffs and their
<em>amici</em>, echoed by the Court, rhetorically emphasize that
Article I, Section 8, of the Constitution assigns Congress, not the
President, authority over tariffs. <em>Ante,</em> at 5. That rhetoric
is a red herring in this case because no one disputes the point.
Everyone, including the President, agrees that Congress possesses
constitutional authority over tariffs.<p></p>

															The important principle here, as everyone also
acknowledges, is that Congress may in turn authorize the President
to impose tariffs. Cf. <em>FCC</em> v. <em>Consumers=E2=80=99 Research</em>=
,
606 U.S. 656, 673=E2=80=93675 (2025); <em>J. W. Hampton, Jr., &amp; Co.</em=
>
v. <em>United States</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://suprem=
e.justia.com/cases/federal/us/276/394/">276 U.S.
394</a>, 409=E2=80=93410 (1928). Indeed, since the beginning of the
Republic, Congress has regularly empowered the President to order
tariffs and other foreign import restrictions under various
circumstances. As noted above, many current federal laws continue
to grant the President expansive tariff authority, including the
Trade Expansion Act of 1962 (Section 232); the Trade Act of 1974
(Sections 122, 201, and 301); and the Tariff Act of 1930 (Section
338). Neither the plaintiffs nor the Court has suggested that the
numerous laws granting tariff power to the President violate the
Constitution=E2=80=99s separation of powers.<p></p>

															<em>Second</em>, and relatedly, the President does
not claim unilateral authority to impose IEEPA tariffs without
congressional authorization or over a congressional prohibition. On
the contrary, the President=E2=80=99s argument recognizes that, in
exercising his statutory tariff power under IEEPA, he must act
within the scope of Congress=E2=80=99s authorizations and abide by
Congress=E2=80=99s limitations. And the Executive has further acknowledged
that the Judiciary maintains the final word in justiciable cases on
whether Congress has authorized the President to impose those
tariffs under IEEPA. See <em>Trump</em> v. <em>CASA, Inc.</em>, 606
U.S. 831, 859=E2=80=93860, n. 18 (2025); cf. <em>Marbury</em> v.
<em>Madison</em>, 1 Cranch 137, 177=E2=80=93178 (1803).<p></p>

															The President here contends only that Congress,
by enacting IEEPA in 1977, authorized the President to impose
tariffs on foreign imports in declared national emergencies. To use
the familiar vernacular of Justice Robert Jackson in
<em>Youngstown</em>, the President argues that this case falls into
category one, where the President is acting =E2=80=9Cpursuant to an express
or implied authorization of Congress.=E2=80=9D <em>Youngstown Sheet &amp;
Tube Co.</em> v. <em>Sawyer</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https:/=
/supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/343/579/">343 U.S.
579</a>, 635 (1952) (concurring opinion). The President has not
here asserted authority to impose IEEPA tariffs in a peacetime
emergency in a <em>Youngstown</em> category two or three scenario.
<em>Id.</em>, at 637=E2=80=93638.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cas=
es/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F3" name=3D"T3" id=3D"T3">3</a>]<p></p>

															<em>Third</em>, Congress possesses a variety of
tools to limit the President=E2=80=99s tariffs=E2=80=94directly via new leg=
islation
or, perhaps more readily, by not approving annual appropriations
necessary for the Executive Branch to continue to implement the
tariffs. See <em>Biden</em> v. <em>Nebraska</em>, 600 U.S. 477, 505
(2023) (=E2=80=9CAmong Congress=E2=80=99s most important authorities is its=
 control
of the purse=E2=80=9D).<p></p>

															Importantly, the House, the Senate, and the
President annually approve most appropriations. As a result, each
House of Congress and the President independently possesses <em>de
facto</em> veto power over particular appropriations.[<a href=3D"https://su=
preme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F4" name=3D"T4" id=3D"T4">4<=
/a>]<p></p>

															Of course, many different appropriations items
are usually considered and packaged together, so the negotiations
can be complex. But the point stands: Congress is not a helpless
bystander when it comes to the President=E2=80=99s exercise of tariff
authority under IEEPA. Cf. Ike Skelton National Defense
Authorization Act for Fiscal Year 2011, 124Stat. 4351=E2=80=934352 (barring
Executive from using funds to transfer detainees from Guantanamo
into United States); Boland Amendment, 98Stat. 1935=E2=80=931936 (1984)
(barring certain Executive Branch agencies from providing aid to
Contras in Nicaragua).<p></p>

															In Congress, moreover, everything is related to
everything else, as the saying goes. Members and Committees of
Congress possess substantial tools of leverage over the Executive
Branch. Cf. The Federalist No. 51, p. 322 (C. Rossiter ed. 1961)
(J. Madison). Congress could, for example, wield its authority over
oversight, legislation, confirmations, or appropriations to
pressure the President to reduce or eliminate some or all of the
IEEPA tariffs.<p></p>

															In light of Congress=E2=80=99s appropriations authority
and its other robust powers, it is not correct to suggest=E2=80=94as The
Chief Justice=E2=80=99s opinion today elliptically does, <em>ante</em>, at
9=E2=80=94that two-thirds majorities of both Houses of Congress would need
to pass new legislation over a Presidential veto in order to limit
these IEEPA tariffs or, more generally, to restrict the President=E2=80=99s
use of IEEPA to impose tariffs.<p></p>

															II<p></p>

															This case presents one straightforward
question of statutory interpretation: Does Congress=E2=80=99s explicit
grant of authority in IEEPA for the President to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D of foreign goods in declared national
emergencies authorize the President to impose tariffs? The answer
is a clear yes.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/=
24-1287/#F5" name=3D"T5" id=3D"T5">5</a>]<p></p>

															A<p></p>

															I begin as always with the statutory text.<p></p>

															In 1941, a few days after Pearl Harbor, Congress
first enacted the relevant language, =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.
importation,=E2=80=9D in an amendment to the 1917 Trading with the Enemy
Act, known as TWEA. 55Stat. 839; 40Stat. 411. After that 1941
amendment, TWEA authorized the President to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D both during wartime and during
peacetime national emergencies.<p></p>

															Then, in 1977, Congress split TWEA into two
separate statutes. As relevant here, Congress amended TWEA to
authorize the President to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=
=80=9D
during wartime only. 91Stat. 1625. And Congress enacted a separate
statute, IEEPA, that granted the President the power to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D during peacetime national emergencies.
<em>Id</em>., at 1626.<p></p>

															The relevant IEEPA text authorized the President
to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cby means=
 of
instructions, licenses, <em>or otherwise</em>.=E2=80=9D <em>Ibid</em>.; 50
U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71702(a)(1) (emphasis added). As the term
=E2=80=9Cotherwise=E2=80=9D indicates, the broadly worded statute did not e=
xclude
tariffs or dictate any specific means of regulating
importation.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-=
1287/#F6" name=3D"T6" id=3D"T6">6</a>]<p></p>

															At the time of TWEA=E2=80=99s amendment in 1941 and
IEEPA=E2=80=99s enactment in 1977, the ordinary dictionary meaning of
=E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D was to =E2=80=9Ccontrol,=E2=80=9D to =E2=80=9Cad=
just by rule,=E2=80=9D or to =E2=80=9Csubject to
governing principles or laws.=E2=80=9D Black=E2=80=99s Law Dictionary 1156 =
(5th ed.
1979); see also Black=E2=80=99s Law Dictionary 1519 (3d ed. 1933) (same);
Webster=E2=80=99s Third New International Dictionary 1913 (1976) (defining
=E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D as =E2=80=9Cto govern or direct according to rul=
e=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Cto bring
under the control of law or constituted authority=E2=80=9D); American
Heritage Dictionary 1096 (1969) (=E2=80=9C[t]o control or direct according
to a rule=E2=80=9D; =E2=80=9C[t]o adjust in conformity to a specification o=
r
requirement=E2=80=9D).<p></p>

															Imposing tariffs on imports is clearly a way of
controlling imports (Black=E2=80=99s); governing or directing imports
according to rule (Webster=E2=80=99s, American Heritage); adjusting imports
by rule, method, or established mode (Black=E2=80=99s, American Heritage);
or more generally subjecting imports to governing principles or
laws (Black=E2=80=99s). So the dictionary definitions amply demonstrate
that tariffs are a means to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;.&nbsp;importation=E2=80=9D of foreign imports.[<a href=3D"ht=
tps://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F7" name=3D"T7" id=
=3D"T7">7</a>]<p></p>

															Consistent with those dictionary definitions and
statutory references, tariffs historically have been=E2=80=94and still
are=E2=80=94a common means for the United States to regulate importation of
foreign goods. See, <em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., Section 338 of the Tariff
Act of 1930, 46Stat. 704=E2=80=93706 ( 19 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71338);
Section 232 of the Trade Expansion Act of 1962, 76Stat. 877 ( 19
U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71862); Title II of the Trade Act of 1974,
88Stat. 2011 ( 19 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A72251 <em>et&nbsp;seq</em>.);
Title III of the Trade Act of 1974, 88Stat. 2041 ( 19
U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A72411 <em>et&nbsp;seq</em>.).[<a href=3D"https://su=
preme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F8" name=3D"T8" id=3D"T8">8<=
/a>]<p></p>

															In determining the ordinary meaning of =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;.&nbsp;importation,=E2=80=9D the meaning of the related
phrase =E2=80=9Cregulate commerce=E2=80=9D is also instructive. That phrase=
 has
long been interpreted to encompass tariffs. Since the Founding, the
Constitution=E2=80=99s assignment to Congress of the broad power to
=E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D foreign commerce has been understood to include =
tariffs
on foreign imports. See Art. I, =C2=A78. As Chief Justice Marshall
explained, the =E2=80=9Cright to <em>regulate</em> commerce, <em>even by th=
e
imposition of duties</em>, was not controverted.=E2=80=9D <em>Gibbons</em> =
v.
<em>Ogden</em>, 9 Wheat. 1, 202 (1824) (emphasis added). So too
Justice Story: The =E2=80=9Cpower to <em>regulate</em> commerce <em>include=
s
the power of laying duties</em> to countervail the regulations and
restrictions of foreign nations.=E2=80=9D 2 J. Story, Commentaries on the
Constitution of the United States 530 (1833) (emphasis added). And
still more Story: To =E2=80=9Clay duties=E2=80=9D is a =E2=80=9Ccommon mean=
s of executing
the power=E2=80=9D to =E2=80=9C<em>regulate</em> commerce.=E2=80=9D <em>Id<=
/em>., at 531
(emphasis added). James Madison likewise stated that it cannot =E2=80=9Cbe
inferred=E2=80=9D that the =E2=80=9Cpower to <em>regulate</em> trade does n=
ot involve
a power to tax it.=E2=80=9D Letter from J. Madison to J. Cabell, Sept. 18,
1828, in 9 Writings of James Madison 326 (G. Hunt ed. 1910)
(emphasis added).<p></p>

															Marshall, Story, and Madison make for a
formidable trio. And this Court has long echoed the
Marshall-Story-Madison understanding that tariffs =E2=80=9C<em>regulate</em=
>=E2=80=9D
foreign commerce. The =E2=80=9Claying of a duty on imports, although an
exercise of the taxing power, is also an exercise of the power to
<em>regulate foreign commerce</em>.=E2=80=9D <em>McGoldrick</em> v. <em>Gul=
f Oil
Corp.</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/ca=
ses/federal/us/309/414/">309 U.S.
414</a>, 428 (1940) (emphasis added). And again: Even though =E2=80=9Cthe
taxing power is a distinct power and embraces the power to lay
duties, it does not follow that duties may not be imposed in the
exercise of the power to <em>regulate commerce</em>. The contrary is
well established.=E2=80=9D <em>Board of Trustees of Univ. of Ill.</em> v.
<em>United States</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.j=
ustia.com/cases/federal/us/289/48/">289 U.S.
48</a>, 58 (1933) (emphasis added).[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/c=
ases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F9" name=3D"T9" id=3D"T9">9</a>]<p></p>

															The plaintiffs and the Court today seize on the
word =E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D in isolation, and say that it does not enco=
mpass
the power to tariff. <em>Ante</em>, at 14=E2=80=9316. But the relevant
statutory phrase is =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation.=E2=80=9D=
 And we
must look to the meaning of the phrase as a whole, as our
precedents dictate. See <em>FCC</em> v. <em>AT&amp;T Inc.</em>,
<a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/=
us/562/397/">562 U.S.
397</a>, 406 (2011) (=E2=80=9C[T]wo words together may assume a more
particular meaning than those words in isolation=E2=80=9D). As I have
explained, since the Founding, tariffs on foreign imports have been
a common means of regulating foreign commerce, including imports.
Notably, under the Court=E2=80=99s reading of the word =E2=80=9Cregulate,=
=E2=80=9D
Marshall, Story, and Madison all erred by concluding that the power
to =E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D foreign commerce includes the power to impose=
 tariffs
on foreign imports. That seems dubious.<p></p>

															If the Federal Government=E2=80=99s constitutional power
to =E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D foreign commerce includes tariffs (as this Co=
urt has
repeatedly said), and if the power to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.
importation=E2=80=9D is the power to regulate foreign commerce with respect
to imports (as it plainly is), then IEEPA=E2=80=99s authorization for the
President to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D clearly
encompasses tariffs. Historical usage and that textual syllogism
further buttress the dictionary definitions and help establish that
tariffs are a means to regulate importation.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.jus=
tia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F10" name=3D"T10" id=3D"T10">10</a>]<=
p></p>

															B<p></p>

															Perhaps even more significantly, when IEEPA
was enacted in 1977, Congress and the public clearly would have
understood that the phrase =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=
=80=9D
encompassed tariffs. We know as much not only because of the
dictionary definitions and the traditional understanding of tariffs
as a tool to regulate foreign imports. We also know as much because
of tariffs imposed by two Presidents and approved by federal
courts, including the Supreme Court, in the years shortly before
IEEPA=E2=80=99s 1977 enactment.<p></p>

															<em>First</em>, in 1971, President Nixon imposed
10 percent tariffs across the board on virtually all imports from
every country in the world. Presidential Proclamation No. 4074, 3
CFR 60=E2=80=9361 (1971=E2=80=931975 Comp.). Those tariffs were justified u=
nder
IEEPA=E2=80=99s predecessor statute, the Trading with the Enemy Act, or
TWEA.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F=
11" name=3D"T11" id=3D"T11">11</a>] Like IEEPA now,
TWEA at that time authorized the President to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D during national emergencies, as well
as wartime. And like IEEPA now, TWEA did not specifically use the
words =E2=80=9Ctariff&nbsp;=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cduty.=E2=80=9D<p></p>

															The Nixon tariffs did not fly below the radar.
On the contrary, President Nixon announced the worldwide 10 percent
tariffs in a primetime address to the Nation on August 15, 1971. He
imposed the tariffs as a tool =E2=80=9Cto make certain that American
products will not be at a disadvantage=E2=80=9D and that =E2=80=9Cthe produ=
ct of
American labor will be more competitive.=E2=80=9D Public Papers of the
Presidents, Richard Nixon, Aug. 15, 1971, p. 889. President Nixon
sought to remove =E2=80=9Cthe unfair edge that some of our foreign
competition has,=E2=80=9D and he declared that when =E2=80=9Cthe unfair tre=
atment
is ended, the import tax will end.=E2=80=9D <em>Ibid</em>.<p></p>

															The Nixon tariffs applied to almost all imports
of foreign goods into the United States. And the tariffs had no
time limit. To be sure, they did not end up lasting forever. But
President Nixon terminated them only because the tariffs (as
intended) induced major American trading partners to negotiate new
agreements. Presidential Proclamation No. 4098, 3 CFR 94 (1971=E2=80=931975
Comp.).<p></p>

															The Nixon tariffs garnered substantial national
and international attention, and were generally popular in
Congress. Predictably, however, the tariffs sparked litigation
challenges. In 1975, the Court of Customs and Patent Appeals, the
predecessor to the Federal Circuit, upheld the Nixon tariffs as a
lawful exercise of the President=E2=80=99s authority to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D under TWEA. <em>United States</em> v.
<em>Yoshida Int=E2=80=99l, Inc.</em>, 526 F.2d 560, 576, 583=E2=80=93584. T=
he losing
plaintiffs did not seek further review in this Court.<p></p>

															Two years later in 1977, when Congress divided
TWEA into two, Congress retained that same =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D language in both laws=E2=80=94in TWEA =
for
wartime and in IEEPA for peacetime national emergencies. In doing
so, Members of Congress were plainly aware=E2=80=94after all, how could
they not be=E2=80=94that the =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=
=E2=80=9D
language had recently been invoked by the President and interpreted
by the courts to encompass tariffs. Indeed, the House Committee
Report noted that the relevant =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.
importation=E2=80=9D provision in TWEA =E2=80=9Ccame into play when, on Aug=
ust 15,
1971, President Nixon declared a national emergency with respect to
the balance-of-payments crisis and under that emergency imposed a
surcharge on imports.=E2=80=9D H.&nbsp;R. Rep. No. 95=E2=80=93459, p. 5 (19=
77). The
Report further referenced the appeals court=E2=80=99s holding in
<em>Yoshida</em> that TWEA =E2=80=9Cauthorized imposition of duties=E2=80=
=9D because
of =E2=80=9Cthe existence of the national emergency.=E2=80=9D H.&nbsp;R. Re=
p. No.
95=E2=80=93459, at 5.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/u=
s/607/24-1287/#F12" name=3D"T12" id=3D"T12">12</a>]<p></p>

															The Nixon tariffs persuasively demonstrate that
Members of Congress and the public would have understood the phrase
=E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D to include tariffs w=
hen
IEEPA was enacted in 1977. If Congress wanted to exclude tariffs
from IEEPA=E2=80=99s scope, why would it enact the exact statutory language
from TWEA that had just been invoked by the President and
interpreted by the courts to cover tariffs? Neither the plaintiffs
nor the Court today offers a good answer to that question.
Understandably so, because there is no good answer.<p></p>

															The Court tries to dodge the force of the Nixon
tariffs by observing that one appeals court=E2=80=99s interpretation of
=E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D to uphold President =
Nixon=E2=80=99s
tariffs does not suffice to describe that interpretation as
=E2=80=9Cwell-settled=E2=80=9D when IEEPA was enacted in 1977. <em>Ante</em=
>, at
17=E2=80=9318. Fair enough. But that is not the right question. The
question is what Members of Congress and the public would have
understood =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D to mean w=
hen
Congress enacted IEEPA in 1977. See <em>New Prime Inc.</em> v.
<em>Oliveira</em>, 586 U.S. 105, 113 (2019). Given the significant
and well-known Nixon tariffs, it is entirely implausible to think
that Congress=E2=80=99s 1977 re-enactment of the phrase =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D in IEEPA was somehow meant or
understood to exclude tariffs.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/=
federal/us/607/24-1287/#F13" name=3D"T13" id=3D"T13">13</a>]<p></p>

															<em>Second</em>, if one holds any lingering doubts
about Congress=E2=80=99s and the public=E2=80=99s understanding of the powe=
r to
=E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D as of 1977, a second=
 episode
shortly before IEEPA=E2=80=99s enactment should answer them.<p></p>

															In 1975, President Ford imposed significant
monetary exactions on foreign imports of oil. Presidential
Proclamation No. 4341, 3 CFR 433 (1971=E2=80=931975 Comp.). He acted under
Section 232 of the Trade Expansion Act of 1962. Like TWEA and
IEEPA, the relevant provision of Section 232 did not use the word
=E2=80=9Ctariff&nbsp;=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cduty.=E2=80=9D Rather, Section 2=
32 broadly authorized
the President to =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D of a product, 19
U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71862(b) (1970 ed.)=E2=80=94language akin to the
=E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D language in IEEPA an=
d
TWEA.<p></p>

															In contrast to the Nixon tariffs, the Ford
tariffs on oil imports generated some pushback in Congress. And a
group of utility companies and States quickly sued, arguing that
the relevant statutory phrase =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D did not
authorize monetary exactions such as tariffs.<p></p>

															Over a dissent, the D.&nbsp;C. Circuit agreed
with the plaintiffs challenging the Ford tariffs. Much like the
Court=E2=80=99s decision today, the D.&nbsp;C. Circuit in the Ford matter
concluded that Congress must explicitly authorize monetary
exactions and that the applicable statutory phrase, =E2=80=9Cadjust the
imports,=E2=80=9D did not do so. <em>Algonquin SNG, Inc.</em> v. <em>Federa=
l
Energy Admin.</em>, 518 F.2d 1051, 1055 (CADC 1975).<p></p>

															In 1976, the Ford tariffs case came to the
Supreme Court. In this Court, the plaintiffs pressed nearly
identical arguments (and rhetorical flourishes) as those advanced
by the plaintiffs and repeated by the Court in today=E2=80=99s case.<p></p>

															The plaintiffs argued that the Ford-imposed
monetary exactions involved =E2=80=9Cthe broadest exercise of the tariff
power in the history of the American Republic,=E2=80=9D reminiscent of
=E2=80=9CGeorge III=E2=80=99s stamp tax.=E2=80=9D Tr. of Oral Arg. in <em>F=
ederal Energy
Administration</em> v. <em>Algonquin SNG, Inc.</em>, O. T. 1975, No.
75=E2=80=93382, p. 26. They contended that the statute=E2=80=99s authorizat=
ion for
the President to =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D did not allow for suc=
h
monetary exactions because the statute did =E2=80=9Cnot mention the tariff
on its face.=E2=80=9D <em>Ibid</em>. They asserted that this Court had =E2=
=80=9Cnever
implied a tax, never in the history of this Court from language
which does not explicitly provide for tax, and here there is no
such language, there is no language that mentions a measure of tax
nor a method of calculation of tax. There is no such thing.=E2=80=9D
<em>Id</em>., at 33. They echoed the D.&nbsp;C. Circuit=E2=80=99s holding
that reading the phrase =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D to encompass t=
ariffs
would be =E2=80=9Can anomalous departure=E2=80=9D from =E2=80=9Cthe consist=
ently explicit,
well-defined manner in which Congress has delegated control over
foreign trade and tariffs.=E2=80=9D <em>Algonquin</em>, 518 F.&nbsp;2d, at
1055. And they claimed that interpreting the statute to include
fees =E2=80=9Cundermines the whole tariff structure of the United States.=
=E2=80=9D
Tr. of Oral Arg. in <em>Algonquin</em>, at 26.<p></p>

															Importantly, the <em>Algonquin</em> plaintiffs
acknowledged (as do the plaintiffs and the Court in today=E2=80=99s case)
that the statutory language =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D would allo=
w the
President to impose quotas and embargoes on foreign imports. See
Brief for Respondents in <em>Algonquin,</em> No. 75=E2=80=93382, pp. 26=E2=
=80=9327,
and n. 30. So a President could completely block all imports or
limit their quantity. But according to the plaintiffs, Congress=E2=80=99s
=E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D language precluded the President from
exercising the lesser power of imposing monetary exactions such as
tariffs.<p></p>

															The Supreme Court decided the Ford tariffs case
in 1976. The Court unanimously reversed the D.&nbsp;C. Circuit and
flatly rejected the plaintiffs=E2=80=99 arguments. The Court held that the
statutory phrase =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D=E2=80=94even though i=
t did not
include terms such as =E2=80=9Ctariff,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Ctax,=E2=80=9D =E2=
=80=9Cduty,=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cfee=E2=80=9D=E2=80=94granted
President Ford the authority to impose not only quotas and
embargoes, but also monetary exactions on foreign imports.
<em>Federal Energy Administration</em> v. <em>Algonquin SNG, Inc.</em>,
<a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/=
us/426/548/">426 U.S.
548</a>, 561 (1976).<p></p>

															The Court analyzed the statutory text and found
=E2=80=9Cno support in the language of the statute=E2=80=9D for the plainti=
ffs=E2=80=99
argument that =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D should =E2=80=9Cbe read =
to encompass
only quantitative methods=E2=80=94<em>i.e.</em>, quotas=E2=80=94as opposed =
to
monetary methods=E2=80=94<em>i.e.</em>, license fees=E2=80=94of effecting s=
uch
adjustments.=E2=80=9D <em>Ibid</em>. The Court further explained: =E2=80=9C=
Unless one
assumes, and we do not, that quotas will always be a feasible
method of dealing directly with national security threats posed by
the circumstances under which imports are entering the country,
limiting the President to the use of quotas would effectively and
artificially prohibit him from directly dealing with some of the
very problems against which =C2=A7232(b) is directed.=E2=80=9D <em>Id</em>.=
, at
561=E2=80=93562 (quotation marks omitted).<p></p>

															In short, according to the unanimous
<em>Algonquin</em> Court, the statutory text, structure, and logic of
Section 232 definitively established that the President=E2=80=99s authority
to =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D encompassed not only quotas and emb=
argoes,
but also monetary exactions such as tariffs and fees.<p></p>

															Today=E2=80=99s case should follow
<em>a&nbsp;fortiori</em> from <em>Algonquin</em>. No meaningful
daylight exists between the statutory phrase =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=
=80=9D
in Section 232 at issue in <em>Algonquin</em> and the phrase
=E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D in IEEPA at issue he=
re. The
plaintiffs and the Court in this case do not even try to
distinguish =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D from =E2=80=9Cregulate .&n=
bsp;.&nbsp;.
importation.=E2=80=9D Nor could they. Recall that the dictionary definition
of =E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D includes =E2=80=9C<em>adjust</em> by rule.=E2=
=80=9D Black=E2=80=99s Law
Dictionary, at 1156 (5th ed. 1979) (emphasis added). To adjust
imports is to regulate imports. Indeed, if anything, the phrase
=E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D is broader in scope =
than the
phrase =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports.=E2=80=9D<p></p>

															So if Section 232=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=
=80=9D
includes tariffs=E2=80=94as this Court unanimously concluded in
<em>Algonquin</em> in 1976 just a year before IEEPA=E2=80=94how can IEEPA=
=E2=80=99s
=E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D not include tariffs?=
<p></p>

															<em>Algonquin</em>=E2=80=99s importance for today=E2=80=99s =
case
rests not merely on its status as a unanimous on-point Supreme
Court statutory precedent=E2=80=94although it is surely significant for
that reason as well. The case is especially consequential for
present purposes because it helps show the ordinary public and
congressional understanding of =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.
importation=E2=80=9D in 1977 when Congress enacted IEEPA.<p></p>

															To be clear, the question here is not what
individual Members of Congress might have subjectively intended in
1977. The question is the ordinary meaning and understanding of the
words that Congress used. Given that the phrase =E2=80=9Cadjust the
imports=E2=80=9D=E2=80=94again, in a statutory provision that did not use s=
pecific
words such as =E2=80=9Ctariff&nbsp;=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cduty=E2=80=9D=E2=
=80=94was unanimously held by this
Court in 1976 to include tariffs, and given that President Nixon
had similarly relied on his statutory authority to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D to impose 10 percent tariffs on
virtually all imports from all countries, could a rational citizen
or Member of Congress in 1977 have understood =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D in IEEPA not to encompass tariffs? I
think not. Any citizens or Members of Congress in 1977 who somehow
thought that the =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D lan=
guage in
IEEPA excluded tariffs would have had their heads in the sand.<p></p>

															The Court today tries its best to distinguish
<em>Algonquin</em> on the ground that Section 232 included =E2=80=9Csweepin=
g=E2=80=9D
language authorizing the President to take =E2=80=9Csuch action=E2=80=9D th=
at =E2=80=9Che
deems necessary,=E2=80=9D whereas IEEPA does not. <em>Ante</em>, at 19. But
the <em>Algonquin</em> Court did not rely on that language and
instead focused on whether the phrase =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D =
included
monetary exactions. See 426 U.&nbsp;S., at 561. Moreover, IEEPA
itself broadly authorizes the President to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cby means of instructions, lic=
enses,
<em>or otherwise</em>=E2=80=9D in order to =E2=80=9Cdeal with=E2=80=9D an =
=E2=80=9Cunusual and
extraordinary=E2=80=9D foreign =E2=80=9Cthreat=E2=80=9D to the =E2=80=9Cnat=
ional security, foreign
policy, or economy of the United States.=E2=80=9D 50 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C.
=C2=A7=C2=A71701(a), 1702(a)(1)(B) (emphasis added). That language is
similarly expansive, authorizing the President to employ various
tools to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation.=E2=80=9D In short, =
just as
the phrase =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D includes tariffs, as
<em>Algonquin</em> held, so too the phrase =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;=
.
importation=E2=80=9D includes tariffs.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.co=
m/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F14" name=3D"T14" id=3D"T14">14</a>]<p></p>

															The Court also attempts to brush aside
<em>Algonquin</em> by citing an entirely different provision of the
Trade Expansion Act=E2=80=94one that was not at issue in
<em>Algonquin</em>=E2=80=94that expressly refers to a =E2=80=9Cduty.=E2=80=
=9D <em>Ante</em>, at
19. But the <em>Algonquin</em> Court did not rely on=E2=80=94or even
mention=E2=80=94that provision when concluding that the statutory phrase
=E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D includes tariffs. For good reason. Tha=
t
provision, which states that =E2=80=9C[n]o action shall be taken=E2=80=9D t=
o
=E2=80=9Cdecrease or eliminate=E2=80=9D an existing =E2=80=9Cduty or other =
import
restriction,=E2=80=9D 19 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71862(a) (1970 ed.), conce=
rns
only the power to reduce existing tariffs and plainly does not bear
on a President=E2=80=99s power to <em>impose</em> tariffs under Section
232.<p></p>

															To sum up on the Nixon and Ford tariffs: When
enacting IEEPA in 1977, Congress employed the exact language
recently invoked by President Nixon to justify 10 percent worldwide
tariffs. And IEEPA came fast on the heels of this Court=E2=80=99s unanimous
1976 decision in <em>Algonquin</em>, which held that substantially
similar =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D language authorized President =
Ford=E2=80=99s
tariffs on oil imports. Importantly, moreover, the statutory
provisions authorizing the Nixon and Ford tariffs did not use
specific words such as =E2=80=9Ctariff&nbsp;=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cduty.=E2=
=80=9D<p></p>

															The Nixon and Ford tariffs, this Court=E2=80=99s
decision in <em>Algonquin</em>, and the ordinary and historical
understanding of tariffs as a means of regulating imports together
render it all but impossible to conclude that Congress in 1977
implicitly excluded tariffs when retaining TWEA=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D language in IEEPA. If Congress in 1977
wanted to exclude tariffs from the President=E2=80=99s IEEPA toolkit,
either it would have not retained the phrase =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation,=E2=80=9D or it would otherwise have made clear
in IEEPA that the power to impose tariffs was excluded. Congress
did neither.<p></p>

															C<p></p>

															Two additional historical points strongly
reinforce that analysis of text and precedent and further
demonstrate that =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D in =
IEEPA
encompasses tariffs.<p></p>

															<em>First</em>, U.&nbsp;S. history from the 1800s
through IEEPA=E2=80=99s 1977 enactment illustrates how the statute came to
incorporate the President=E2=80=99s long-recognized authority to impose
tariffs during wartime and then also during peacetime national
emergencies.<p></p>

															Long before the initial 1917 enactment of the
Trading with the Enemy Act, which was IEEPA=E2=80=99s predecessor, the
President possessed inherent wartime authority to prohibit
commercial relations with enemy nations. That inherent authority
included the power to impose tariffs on foreign imports.<p></p>

															For example, during the Mexican-American War in
the 1840s, President Polk permitted only limited trade with Mexico,
subject to tariffs. Some Members of Congress publicly questioned
whether the President possessed that tariff authority. In response,
President Polk justified the tariffs on the ground that =E2=80=9Cthe
military right to exclude commerce altogether from the ports of the
enemy in our military occupation included the minor right of
admitting it under prescribed conditions.=E2=80=9D J. Polk, To the House of
Representatives of the United States (Jan. 2, 1849), in 6
Compilation of the Messages and Papers of the Presidents 2522, 2523
(J. Richardson ed. 1897).<p></p>

															In 1854, the Supreme Court agreed with President
Polk=E2=80=99s view, stating: =E2=80=9CNo one can doubt=E2=80=9D that the P=
resident, as
=E2=80=9Ccommander-in-chief of our naval force,=E2=80=9D possessed the auth=
ority to
=E2=80=9Cregulate import duties.=E2=80=9D <em>Cross</em> v. <em>Harrison</e=
m>, 16 How.
164, 189=E2=80=93190.<p></p>

															In 1862, President Lincoln partially lifted an
existing blockade against the Confederate States during the Civil
War. Like President Polk, he then permitted limited trade, subject
to a monetary fee. A group of cotton sellers later sued, arguing
that the fee =E2=80=9Cwas essentially a tax and not authorized by any act
of Congress, which alone had the power to impose taxes.=E2=80=9D
<em>Hamilton</em> v. <em>Dillin</em>, 21 Wall. 73, 81 (1875). The
Supreme Court rejected that argument, holding that there was =E2=80=9Cno
question=E2=80=9D that requiring a monetary fee to trade with the
Confederate States was part of =E2=80=9Cthe war power of the United States
government.=E2=80=9D <em>Id</em>., at 86=E2=80=9387. The existence of war m=
eant =E2=80=9Ca
suspension of commercial intercourse between the opposing sections
of the country,=E2=80=9D so if =E2=80=9Csuch a course of dealing were to be
permitted at all, it would necessarily be upon such conditions as
the government chose to prescribe.=E2=80=9D <em>Id</em>., at 87.<p></p>

															And in 1898, during the Spanish-American War,
President McKinley imposed duties =E2=80=9Cupon the occupation of any forts
and places in the Philippine Islands.=E2=80=9D <em>Lincoln</em> v. <em>Unit=
ed
States</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/c=
ases/federal/us/197/419/">197 U.S.
419</a>, 428 (1905) (quotation marks omitted). This Court
subsequently recognized those McKinley duties as a lawful wartime
measure. <em>Id</em>., at 427=E2=80=93428.<p></p>

															Why does that wartime history matter? Because
when Congress first enacted the Trading with the Enemy Act in 1917
during World War I, it statutorily codified some of the President=E2=80=99s
longstanding inherent wartime powers over foreign trade, which
included the power to tariff. See Trading with the Enemy Act, ch.
106, 40Stat. 411; see also Brief for Professor Aditya Bamzai as
<em>Amicus Curiae</em> 16=E2=80=9319, 26=E2=80=9327. For the duration of Wo=
rld War I,
TWEA authorized the President, when he found =E2=80=9Cthe public safety so
requires,=E2=80=9D to make it unlawful =E2=80=9Cto import into the United S=
tates=E2=80=9D
from any =E2=80=9Cnamed=E2=80=9D country certain goods =E2=80=9Cexcept at s=
uch time or
times, and under such regulations or orders, and subject to such
limitations and exceptions as the President shall prescribe.=E2=80=9D =C2=
=A711,
40Stat. 422=E2=80=93423.<p></p>

															In 1933, during the Great Depression and five
days after President Franklin Roosevelt took office, Congress
expanded TWEA to apply not only in wartime, but also during a
=E2=80=9Cnational emergency=E2=80=9D declared by the President. 48Stat. 1.<=
p></p>

															Eight years later, in 1941, a few days after
Pearl Harbor, Congress again amended TWEA=E2=80=99s language by more
succinctly providing that the President may =E2=80=9Cregulate=E2=80=9D cert=
ain
transactions, including =E2=80=9Cimportation,=E2=80=9D under TWEA during wa=
r or
=E2=80=9Cany other period of national emergency declared by the President.=
=E2=80=9D
55Stat. 839.<p></p>

															So as of 1941=E2=80=94and from then to 1977=E2=80=94TWEA
expressly authorized the President to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.
importation=E2=80=9D both during wartime and during peacetime national
emergencies. Historically, Presidents had regulated importation by
imposing tariffs, as the Polk, Lincoln, and McKinley tariffs
illustrated. So TWEA from 1941 to 1977 was best understood to
authorize tariffs. See Brief for Professor Aditya Bamzai as
<em>Amicus Curiae</em> 27=E2=80=9328.<p></p>

															During that period, as I have discussed at
length above, President Nixon in 1971 imposed 10 percent tariffs on
almost all imports of foreign goods and relied on TWEA=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9Cr=
egulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D language to justify them. Those
tariffs were upheld in court.<p></p>

															Then, in 1977, Congress amended TWEA and divided
it into two statutes. TWEA retained the President=E2=80=99s power to
=E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation,=E2=80=9D but only during war=
time.
The newly enacted second law, IEEPA, also retained the power to
=E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation,=E2=80=9D and it would apply =
during
periods of declared national emergencies. As this Court has
previously recognized, IEEPA was =E2=80=9Cdirectly drawn=E2=80=9D from TWEA=
, and
the relevant authorities are essentially the same. <em>Dames &amp;
Moore</em> v. <em>Regan</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://sup=
reme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/453/654/"></a><a class=3D"related-case" hr=
ef=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/453/654/">453
U.S. 654</a>, 671, 672=E2=80=93673 (1981).<p></p>

															Therefore, IEEPA=E2=80=99s specific language=E2=80=94=E2=80=
=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D=E2=80=94was not new statutory text whe=
n
Congress enacted IEEPA in 1977. Far from it. Beginning in 1941,
TWEA had already authorized the President to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D of foreign goods in wartime and
national emergencies. And the earlier Polk, Lincoln, and McKinley
examples, as well as the later Nixon example, demonstrated that the
power to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D historicall=
y
encompassed tariffs as well as quotas and embargoes.<p></p>

															The plaintiffs and the Court today assert that
wartime precedents do not govern peacetime. But Congress modeled
IEEPA on TWEA precisely so that the President could continue to
exercise certain wartime authorities such as quotas, embargoes, and
tariffs during peacetime national emergencies as well. Congress
first explicitly extended that wartime power to national
emergencies in 1933, during the Franklin Roosevelt Administration.
Cf. <em>New State Ice Co.</em> v. <em>Liebmann</em>, <a class=3D"related-ca=
se" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/285/262/">285 U.S.
262</a>, 306 (1932) (Brandeis, J., dissenting) (The Great
Depression was =E2=80=9Can emergency more serious than war=E2=80=9D). And C=
ongress
has continued to authorize the President to exercise that power in
both wartime and peacetime emergencies.<p></p>

															In short, Congress in 1977 enacted the same
=E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D language that had lo=
ng been
understood to encompass tariffs.<p></p>

															<em>Second</em>, contrary to the tenor of the
plaintiffs=E2=80=99 and the Court=E2=80=99s arguments here, it would not ha=
ve been
at all unusual or surprising for Congress, when enacting IEEPA in
1977, to authorize the President to impose tariffs. Since the early
days of the Republic, Congress has regularly granted the President
the power to regulate foreign trade, including via tariffs.<p></p>

															A few examples: In 1810, Congress authorized the
President to prohibit imports from Great Britain or France if
either nation violated the neutral commerce of the United States.
<em>Cargo of Brig Aurora</em> v. <em>United States</em>, 7 Cranch 382,
382=E2=80=93384, 388 (1813); 2Stat. 606.<p></p>

															In 1890, Congress granted the President the
power to impose import duties in response to duties imposed by
other countries on American exports. <em>Marshall Field &amp;
Co.</em> v. <em>Clark</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supre=
me.justia.com/cases/federal/us/143/649/">143 U.S.
649</a>, 680=E2=80=93681 (1892); 26Stat. 612.<p></p>

															In 1922, Congress empowered the President to
levy import duties under certain conditions. <em>J. W. Hampton, Jr.,
&amp; Co.</em> v. <em>United States</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D=
"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/276/394/">276
U.S. 394</a>, 400=E2=80=93402 (1928); 42Stat. 941.<p></p>

															In 1930, Congress enacted Section 338 of the
Tariff Act, which authorizes the President to impose tariffs when
he finds that =E2=80=9Cany foreign country places any burden or
disadvantage upon the commerce of the United States.=E2=80=9D 19
U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71338(d); 46Stat. 705.<p></p>

															In 1962, Congress authorized the President in
Section 232 of the Trade Expansion Act to =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=
=9D of a
foreign good that threatens to impair national security.
=C2=A71862(c)(1)(A); 76Stat. 877.<p></p>

															In 1974, under Section 201 of the Trade Act,
Congress granted the President the power to =E2=80=9Ctake all appropriate
and feasible action within his power,=E2=80=9D including imposing a =E2=80=
=9Cduty=E2=80=9D
on imports that, according to the U. S. International Trade
Commission, have caused or threatened =E2=80=9Cserious injury=E2=80=9D to a
domestic industry. =C2=A7=C2=A72251(a), 2253(a)(1)(A), (3)(A); 88Stat.
2014=E2=80=932015.<p></p>

															So too, Section 301 authorizes the President to
direct the U.&nbsp;S. Trade Representative to =E2=80=9Cimpose duties=E2=80=
=9D on
countries engaging in unfair trade practices. =C2=A7=C2=A72411(a), (c)(1)(B=
);
88Stat. 2041=E2=80=932042.<p></p>

															And Section 122 of the Act grants the President
the power to impose a =E2=80=9Ctemporary import surcharge=E2=80=9D to =E2=
=80=9Cdeal with
large and serious United States balance-of-payment deficits.=E2=80=9D
=C2=A72132(a)(1)(A); 88Stat. 1987=E2=80=931988.<p></p>

															Those many statutes definitively establish that
Congress, since near the Founding, has delegated to the President
broad power to impose tariffs on foreign imports. See also
<em>ante,</em> at 13=E2=80=9315 (Thomas, J., dissenting). So it would hardl=
y
have been unusual or surprising for Congress to have granted tariff
power to the President during wartime and peacetime national
emergencies, as it did in TWEA and IEEPA.<p></p>

															To be sure, given those other statutes that
authorize the President to impose tariffs on foreign imports, one
might reasonably ask: Why did the President need distinct tariff
authority under IEEPA during peacetime emergencies=E2=80=94or, for that
matter, under TWEA during wartime?<p></p>

															The basic answer is that IEEPA is an emergency
statute that allows the President to impose tariffs somewhat more
quickly, as would be expected in a declared national emergency.
Similarly, in wartime, TWEA allows the President to impose tariffs
more rapidly.<p></p>

															But critically, TWEA and IEEPA do not authorize
the President to exercise some new substantive power. Rather, they
authorize the President to exercise a commonly granted
power=E2=80=94tariffs=E2=80=94more efficiently than under the many ordinary=
 tariff
statutes.<p></p>

															The plaintiffs and the Court assert that
interpreting IEEPA to authorize tariffs would in effect evade
specific limits on tariffs in certain other tariff statutes. But as
Judge Taranto explained in the Federal Circuit, Congress in IEEPA
understandably afforded the President more flexibility to act
during declared emergencies, just as Congress had done in TWEA for
wartime since 1917. See 149 F. 4th 1312, 1363=E2=80=931366 (2025)
(dissenting opinion).<p></p>

															Moreover, IEEPA is not a blank check. IEEPA
contains its own limits, including the requirement that the tariffs
deal with an unusual and extraordinary foreign threat, 50
U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71701(b); a default 1-year limit on emergencies,
=C2=A71622(d); an enumerated list of exceptions, =C2=A71702(b); and
comprehensive congressional reporting requirements, =C2=A71703. And as
noted above, each House of Congress possesses a variety of tools to
revoke, limit, or influence a President=E2=80=99s IEEPA or TWEA
tariffs.<p></p>

															Relatedly, it is also not surprising that the
many ordinary tariff statutes expressly refer to =E2=80=9Ctariffs,=E2=80=9D
=E2=80=9Cduties,=E2=80=9D and the like, while IEEPA and TWEA do not. As Jud=
ge
Taranto astutely explained, =E2=80=9CCongress in those statutes was
overwhelmingly focused on tariff issues,=E2=80=9D whereas =E2=80=9CCongress=
 in
IEEPA (as in TWEA) was focused on the subject of emergencies and
giving plainly broad emergency authority regarding foreign
property.=E2=80=9D 149 F.&nbsp;4th, at 1364 (dissenting opinion).<p></p>

															In sum, in authorizing the President to
=E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation,=E2=80=9D IEEPA embodies an
=E2=80=9Ceyes-open congressional grant of broad emergency authority in this
foreign-affairs realm, which unsurprisingly extends beyond
authorities available under non-emergency laws, and Congress
confirmed the understood breadth by tying IEEPA=E2=80=99s authority to
particularly demanding procedural requirements for keeping Congress
informed.=E2=80=9D <em>Id</em>., at 1348.<p></p>

															D<p></p>

															Finally, all of that text, history, and
precedent is further reinforced by two compelling pieces of
context.<p></p>

															<em>First</em>, interpreting IEEPA to exclude
tariffs creates nonsensical textual and practical anomalies. The
plaintiffs and the Court do not dispute that the President can act
in declared emergencies under IEEPA to impose quotas or even total
embargoes on all imports from a given country. But the President
supposedly cannot take the far more modest step of conditioning
those imports on payment of a tariff or duty.<p></p>

															Textually, however, if quotas and embargoes are
a means to regulate importation, how are tariffs not a means to
regulate importation? Nothing in the text supports such an
illogical distinction.<p></p>

															And it does not make much sense to think that
IEEPA allows the President in a declared national emergency to, for
example, shut off all or most imports from China, but not to impose
even a $1 tariff on imports from China. As Judge Taranto forcefully
pointed out in the Federal Circuit, tariffs are =E2=80=9Cjust a less
extreme, more flexible tool for pursuing the same objective of
controlling the amount or price of imports that, after all, could
be barred altogether.=E2=80=9D 149 F.&nbsp;4th, at 1363 (dissenting
opinion). All of that explains why this Court in <em>Algonquin</em>
definitively rejected such a strange slice-and-dice approach to the
President=E2=80=99s statutory power to =E2=80=9Cadjust=E2=80=9D imports. If=
 quotas and
embargoes are authorized, so are tariffs.<p></p>

															In short, whether through prohibiting imports
via embargoes or regulating the quantity of imports through quotas
or regulating the price of imports with tariffs, Congress granted
the President flexibility in declared national emergencies to take
various actions affecting imports of foreign goods. The plaintiffs
and the Court have no coherent textual or commonsensical
explanation for why a rational Congress would, in such a momentous
and carefully considered statute as IEEPA, grant the President the
power to impose quotas and embargoes, but not tariffs, on foreign
imports during emergencies.<p></p>

															<em>Second</em>, IEEPA was not debated and passed
in a vacuum in 1977=E2=80=94it was enacted around the same time that
Congress significantly <em>constrained</em> executive power in
multiple ways in the wake of Watergate and Vietnam. The list of
major new statutory restrictions on Presidential power enacted in
the 1970s is long and extraordinary, with lasting effects to the
present day.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-=
1287/#F15" name=3D"T15" id=3D"T15">15</a>]<p></p>

															And Congress, during that comprehensive
examination and recalibration of government power, did not overlook
TWEA and the President=E2=80=99s emergency authorities. Led by Senators
Church and Mathias, Congress carefully studied the President=E2=80=99s
emergency authorities, including TWEA. Then, in 1976 and 1977,
Congress enacted a variety of legislation to tighten up the
President=E2=80=99s emergency powers, including by passing a new National
Emergencies Act that cabined the President=E2=80=99s authority to declare
emergencies by setting forth various procedural requirements.<p></p>

															Yet when enacting IEEPA in 1977, Congress
continued to grant the President the power to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D in declared national emergencies=E2=80=
=94a
power that the President had possessed since 1941 under TWEA and
that had recently been invoked by President Nixon to justify his
1971 tariffs. In IEEPA (and TWEA) in 1977, Congress consciously
balanced concerns about expansive exercises of emergency powers
against the necessity of equipping the President with tools to
address exigencies that are difficult if not impossible to foresee.
That broader congressional context=E2=80=94general skepticism and scaling
back of executive power combined with re-enactment of the familiar
=E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D language in IEEPA=E2=
=80=94strongly
indicates that Congress said what it meant and meant what it said
when it enacted IEEPA and continued to authorize the President to
=E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D during national
emergencies.<p></p>

															III<p></p>

															In an ordinary statutory interpretation case,
I am confident that a majority of this Court would flatly reject
the plaintiffs=E2=80=99 exceedingly weak statutory arguments and would hold
that IEEPA=E2=80=99s authorization for the President to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D during national emergencies includes
the power to impose tariffs.<p></p>

															Notably, the Court today does not claim that the
phrase =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D on its own ex=
cludes
tariffs as a matter of ordinary statutory meaning. Only three
Members of the Court, Justice Sotomayor, Justice Kagan, and Justice
Jackson, do so.<p></p>

															The Chief Justice=E2=80=99s opinion in Part II=E2=80=93A=E2=
=80=932,
which is joined only by Justice Gorsuch and Justice Barrett,
instead relies on the major questions doctrine. The major questions
doctrine is an important canon of statutory interpretation that the
Court has applied in a number of significant cases over the last 45
years. See <em>Industrial Union Dept., AFL=E2=80=93CIO</em> v. <em>American
Petroleum Institute</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme=
.justia.com/cases/federal/us/448/607/"></a><a class=3D"related-case" href=
=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/448/607/">448
U.S. 607</a>, 645 (1980) (plurality opinion).<p></p>

															Justice Scalia articulated the canonical
statement of the major questions doctrine: =E2=80=9CWe expect Congress to
speak clearly if it wishes to assign to an agency decisions of vast
=E2=80=98economic and political significance.=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D <em>U=
tility Air
Regulatory Group</em> v. <em>EPA</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"ht=
tps://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/573/302/">573 U.S.
302</a>, 324 (2014) (quoting <em>FDA</em> v. <em>Brown &amp;
Williamson Tobacco Corp.</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://su=
preme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/529/120/">529 U.S.
120</a>, 160 (2000)); see also <em>Alabama Assn. of Realtors</em> v.
<em>Department of Health and Human Servs.</em>, 594 U.S. 758, 764
(2021) (<em>per curiam</em>); <em>National</em> <em>Federation of
Independent Business</em> v. <em>OSHA</em>, 595 U.S. 109, 117 (2022)
(<em>per curiam</em>); <em>Biden</em> v. <em>Nebraska</em>, 600 U.S. 477,
507 (2023); cf. <em>West Virginia</em> v. <em>EPA</em>, 597 U.S. 697,
723 (2022).<p></p>

															Stated otherwise, in cases where the Executive
Branch takes an action of major economic and political
significance, it must =E2=80=9Cpoint to =E2=80=98clear congressional author=
ization=E2=80=99
for the power it claims.=E2=80=9D <em>Ibid</em>. (quoting <em>Utility Air</=
em>,
573 U.&nbsp;S., at 324).<p></p>

															The requirement of =E2=80=9C<em>clear</em> congressional
authorization=E2=80=9D for executive actions of major economic and
political significance is =E2=80=9Cgrounded in two overlapping and
reinforcing presumptions: (i) a separation of powers-based
presumption against the delegation of major lawmaking authority
from Congress to the Executive Branch, and (ii) a presumption that
Congress intends to make major policy decisions itself, not leave
those decisions to agencies.=E2=80=9D <em>United States Telecom Assn.</em> =
v.
<em>FCC</em>, 855 F.3d 381, 419 (CADC 2017) (Kavanaugh, J.,
dissenting from denial of rehearing en banc) (citation omitted). As
this Court later recounted in <em>West Virginia</em>, =E2=80=9Cboth
separation of powers principles and a practical understanding of
legislative intent make us reluctant to read into ambiguous
statutory text the delegation claimed to be lurking there.=E2=80=9D 597
U.&nbsp;S., at 723 (quotation marks omitted).[<a href=3D"https://supreme.ju=
stia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F16" name=3D"T16" id=3D"T16">16</a>]=
 The doctrine guards =E2=80=9Cagainst unintentional,
oblique, or otherwise unlikely delegations of the legislative
power.=E2=80=9D <em>NFIB</em>, 595 U.&nbsp;S., at 125 (Gorsuch, J.,
concurring).[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-=
1287/#F17" name=3D"T17" id=3D"T17">17</a>]<p></p>

															I agree that this case involves an executive
action of major economic and political significance=E2=80=94which is
typically the trigger for requiring =E2=80=9Cclear congressional
authorization.=E2=80=9D But in my respectful view, The Chief Justice=E2=80=
=99s
opinion=E2=80=99s application of the major questions doctrine in this case
is incorrect for two alternative and independent reasons. First,
the statutory text, history, and precedent constitute =E2=80=9Cclear
