authorizes the President to =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports=E2=80=9D of a forei=
gn good
without mentioning =E2=80=9Ctariffs=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Ctaxes.=E2=80=9D An=
d so would tariffs
imposed in wartime under TWEA=E2=80=99s authority to =E2=80=9Cregulate
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation.=E2=80=9D[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com=
/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F21" name=3D"T21" id=3D"T21">21</a>]<p></p>

															The Chief Justice=E2=80=99s opinion=E2=80=99s approach to th=
e
major questions doctrine is a magic-words test under another
name=E2=80=94in contravention of our precedents that make clear that
Congress need not use magic words or =E2=80=9Chighly specific=E2=80=9D term=
s.
<em>Biden</em> v. <em>Nebraska</em>, 600 U.&nbsp;S., at 516 (Barrett,
J., concurring) (quotation marks omitted).[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justi=
a.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287/#F22" name=3D"T22" id=3D"T22">22</a>]<p>=
</p>

															In previous cases, the Court has looked at the
four factors to determine whether there is =E2=80=9Cclear congressional
authorization=E2=80=9D precisely because the major questions canon has no
magic words requirement. If magic words or the equivalent were
necessary, that would be the only factor. And the Court would not
need the four factors that the Court has consistently
applied.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-1287=
/#F23" name=3D"T23" id=3D"T23">23</a>]<p></p>

															In sum, under the major questions doctrine as
the Court has applied it, this should be a straightforward case.
Congress supplied clear authorization for the President to impose
tariffs under IEEPA.<p></p>

															B<p></p>

															1<p></p>

															Second, there is an alternative and
independent reason why the major questions doctrine does not apply
here: This is a foreign affairs case.<p></p>

															A plethora of statutes in the U.&nbsp;S. Code
grant the Executive the power to act in foreign affairs. And most
of the important actions that =E2=80=9Cpresidents take today, including in
foreign affairs, rest at least in part on statutory authorization.=E2=80=9D
C. Bradley &amp; J. Goldsmith, Foreign Affairs, Nondelegation, and
the Major Questions Doctrine, 172 U. Pa. L. Rev. 1743, 1745
(2024).<p></p>

															Yet this Court has <em>never</em> before applied
the major questions doctrine=E2=80=94or anything resembling it=E2=80=94to a=
 foreign
affairs statute. I would not make this case the first.<p></p>

															Rather, in the foreign affairs context, this
Court has interpreted statutes as written, with respect for the
primacy of Congress=E2=80=99s and the President=E2=80=99s roles in foreign =
affairs
and without using the major questions doctrine as a thumb on the
scale against the President. See, <em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., <em>Department
of Navy</em> v. <em>Egan</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://su=
preme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/484/518/">484 U.S.
518</a>, 529=E2=80=93530 (1988). That deeply rooted textualist approach to
interpreting foreign affairs statutes is nothing new. What is new
and rather extraordinary is the approach embodied in The Chief
Justice=E2=80=99s opinion for three Justices, which would extend the major
questions doctrine into the foreign affairs realm for the first
time.<p></p>

															Recall that the major questions doctrine is
based on two overlapping foundations: =E2=80=9Cseparation of powers
principles and a practical understanding of legislative intent.=E2=80=9D
<em>West Virginia</em>, 597 U.&nbsp;S., at 723.<p></p>

															With respect to separation of powers, the major
questions doctrine serves to reinforce the nondelegation doctrine.
But in the foreign affairs realm, the Court has recognized that
Congress often broadly delegates authority to the Executive. From
the Founding, numerous foreign affairs statutes =E2=80=9Cauthorizing action
by the President in respect of subjects affecting foreign
relations=E2=80=9D either =E2=80=9Cleave the exercise of the power to his
unrestricted judgment, or provide a standard far more general than
that which has always been considered requisite with regard to
domestic affairs.=E2=80=9D <em>United States</em> v. <em>Curtiss-Wright Exp=
ort
Corp.</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/ca=
ses/federal/us/299/304/">299 U.S.
304</a>, 324 (1936); <em>Department of Transportation</em> v.
<em>Association of American Railroads</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=
=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/575/43/">575 U.S.
43</a>, 80, n. 5 (2015) (Thomas, J., concurring in judgment). The
reason for those broad delegations is simple and obvious: If
=E2=80=9Csuccess=E2=80=9D for America=E2=80=99s foreign affairs =E2=80=9Cai=
ms=E2=80=9D is to be =E2=80=9Cachieved,
congressional legislation .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. must often accord to the
President a degree of discretion and freedom from statutory
restriction which would not be admissible were domestic affairs
alone involved.=E2=80=9D <em>Curtiss-Wright</em>, 299 U.&nbsp;S., at 320.
Stated otherwise, =E2=80=9CCongress=E2=80=94in giving the Executive authori=
ty over
matters of foreign affairs=E2=80=94must of necessity paint with a brush
broader than that it customarily wields in domestic areas.=E2=80=9D
<em>Zemel</em> v. <em>Rusk</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://=
supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/381/1/">381 U.S.
1</a>, 17 (1965).<p></p>

															As Justice Robert Jackson summarized, the
Court=E2=80=99s nondelegation cases=E2=80=94consistent with the =E2=80=9Cun=
broken
legislative practice which has prevailed almost from the inception
of the national government,=E2=80=9D <em>Curtiss-Wright</em>, 299 U.&nbsp;S=
.,
at 322=E2=80=94have =E2=80=9Crecognized internal and external affairs as be=
ing in
separate categories, and held that the strict limitation upon
congressional delegations of power to the President over internal
affairs does not apply with respect to delegations of power in
external affairs.=E2=80=9D <em>Youngstown Sheet &amp; Tube Co.</em> v.
<em>Sawyer</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.c=
om/cases/federal/us/343/579/">343 U.S.
579</a>, 636, n. 2 (1952) (concurring opinion); see
<em>Curtiss-Wright</em>, 299 U.&nbsp;S., at 319=E2=80=93322; <em>Panama
Refining Co.</em> v. <em>Ryan</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https=
://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/293/388/">293 U.S.
388</a>, 422 (1935).<p></p>

															As Justice Jackson further noted, the Court=E2=80=99s
precedents recognize the =E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98unwisdom of requiring Cong=
ress in
this field of governmental power to lay down narrowly definite
standards by which the President is to be governed.=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D
<em>Youngstown</em>, 343 U. S., at 636, n. 2 (concurring opinion)
(quoting <em>Curtiss-Wright</em>, 299 U.&nbsp;S., at 321=E2=80=93322).<p></=
p>

															If the major questions doctrine is designed in
part to protect nondelegation principles, but the nondelegation
doctrine does not play a substantial role in foreign affairs cases
(as the Court has held), then it follows that courts should not
employ the major questions doctrine to put a thumb on the scale
against the President when interpreting foreign affairs statutes.
Rather, as Justice Robert Jackson stated, courts should interpret
those statutes as written.<p></p>

															Relatedly, to the extent that the major
questions doctrine is designed to reflect a =E2=80=9Cpractical
understanding of legislative intent,=E2=80=9D <em>West Virginia</em>, 597
U.&nbsp;S., at 723, the doctrine appropriately plays no role in
=E2=80=9Cnational security or foreign policy contexts, because the canon
does not reflect ordinary congressional intent in those areas.=E2=80=9D
<em>FCC</em> v. <em>Consumers=E2=80=99 Research</em>, 606 U.S. 656, 706 (20=
25)
(Kavanaugh, J., concurring). In the foreign affairs realm, Congress
=E2=80=9Chas good reason to=E2=80=94and intends to=E2=80=94authorize many e=
xecutive branch
actions related to foreign affairs in broad or general terms.=E2=80=9D
Bradley &amp; Goldsmith, 172 U. Pa. L. Rev., at 1793.<p></p>

															Congress ordinarily seeks =E2=80=9Cto give the President
substantial authority and flexibility to protect America and the
American people.=E2=80=9D <em>Consumers=E2=80=99 Research</em>, 606 U.&nbsp=
;S., at
706=E2=80=93707 (Kavanaugh, J., concurring). After all, the President
exercises the =E2=80=9Cvast share of responsibility for the conduct of our
foreign relations.=E2=80=9D <em>American Ins. Assn.</em> v. <em>Garamendi</=
em>,
<a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/=
us/539/396/">539 U.S.
396</a>, 414 (2003) (quotation marks omitted). So Congress =E2=80=9Coften=
=E2=80=9D
gives the President =E2=80=9Ca degree of discretion.=E2=80=9D
<em>Curtiss-Wright</em>, 299 U.&nbsp;S., at 320. That =E2=80=9Cunbroken
legislative practice=E2=80=9D from the Founding means that courts
interpreting statutes in the foreign affairs field should assume
that Congress meant what it said. <em>Id</em>., at 322.<p></p>

															Stated otherwise, =E2=80=9Cif the major questions
doctrine turns on a contextual inquiry into likely congressional
intent, it is likely for a variety of reasons to have less purchase
in the foreign affairs area.=E2=80=9D Bradley &amp; Goldsmith, 172 U. Pa.
L. Rev., at 1790.<p></p>

															To be clear, Congress of course maintains the
ultimate power over how broadly or narrowly to write statutes in
the foreign policy and national security contexts. For example,
Congress can write foreign affairs statutes narrowly. Indeed, even
for wartime powers, Congress rarely gives the President a =E2=80=9Cblank
check.=E2=80=9D <em>Hamdi</em> v. <em>Rumsfeld</em>, <a class=3D"related-ca=
se" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/542/507/"></a><a cl=
ass=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/54=
2/507/">542
U.S. 507</a>, 536 (2004) (plurality opinion). And when Congress
writes a narrow foreign affairs statute, this Court has enforced
those statutory limits as written. Cf. <em>Hamdan</em> v.
<em>Rumsfeld</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia=
.com/cases/federal/us/548/557/">548 U.S.
557</a>, 593=E2=80=93595 (2006); <em>id</em>., at 638=E2=80=93639 (Kennedy,=
 J.,
concurring in part).<p></p>

															Moreover, when it does legislate more broadly,
Congress sometimes claws back the statutory authorization by
rescinding or amending overbroad statutes, or by restricting
previously granted Presidential power through the leverage it
possesses over appropriations, new legislation, or confirmations.
See, <em>e</em>.<em>g</em>., Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act of
1978, 92Stat. 1783; Military Commissions Act of 2006, 120Stat.
2600, as amended, 10 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A7948a <em>et&nbsp;seq</em>.;
Case-Church Amendment, Pub. L. 93=E2=80=9350, =C2=A7307, 87Stat. 129. Eithe=
r
House of Congress alone, through the appropriations process, can
insist on certain limits as a condition of approving funding. At
the end of the day, given the appropriations power, Congress holds
the cards.<p></p>

															In short, in the foreign affairs context, this
Court has <em>never</em> before super-imposed the major questions
doctrine (or any similar canon or principle) onto ordinary
statutory interpretation to place a thumb on the scale against the
President. Rather, the Court interprets the relevant statutes
according to their text, with respect for Congress=E2=80=99s and the
President=E2=80=99s central roles in the foreign policy and national
security fields.<p></p>

															2<p></p>

															This tariffs case plainly falls into the
foreign affairs category. IEEPA =E2=80=9Cdirectly and expressly relate[s]
to foreign affairs.=E2=80=9D Bradley &amp; Goldsmith, 172 U. Pa. L. Rev.,
at 1796. And like quotas and embargoes, tariffs regulate the goods
that are imported into the country from foreign nations. The
tariffs do not apply to goods produced in America.<p></p>

															Moreover, tariffs on foreign imports are
significant tools of foreign policy and national security, whether
imposed under IEEPA, TWEA, Section 232, Section 122, Section 201,
Section 301, or Section 338. They are often used to =E2=80=9Cadvance
foreign policy goals, or as negotiating leverage in trade
negotiations.=E2=80=9D Congressional Research Service, U.&nbsp;S. Tariff
Policy: Overview 1 (2025). Like other economic tools, tariffs can
=E2=80=9Cserve as a =E2=80=98bargaining chip=E2=80=99 to be used by the Pre=
sident when
dealing with a hostile country,=E2=80=9D <em>Dames &amp; Moore</em> v.
<em>Regan</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.co=
m/cases/federal/us/453/654/"></a><a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://=
supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/453/654/">453
U.S. 654</a>, 673 (1981)=E2=80=94or to incentivize a change in behavior by
allies, partners, or enemies. Cf. <em>Association of American
Railroads</em>, 575 U.&nbsp;S., at 80 (opinion of Thomas, J.)
(embargo statute =E2=80=9Cinvolved the external relations of the United
States=E2=80=9D); <em>Gundy</em> v. <em>United States</em>, 588 U.S. 128,
170=E2=80=93171 (2019) (Gorsuch, J., dissenting).<p></p>

															With respect to foreign trade specifically,
Congress often =E2=80=9Cinvest[s] the President with large discretion in
matters arising out of the execution of statutes relating to trade
and commerce with other nations.=E2=80=9D <em>Marshall Field &amp; Co.</em>
v. <em>Clark</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia=
.com/cases/federal/us/143/649/">143 U.S.
649</a>, 691 (1892). Since the Founding, that longstanding practice
has included tariff statutes: Congress has granted the President
expansive power over tariffs and foreign trade. <em>Ante,</em> at
13=E2=80=9317 (Thomas, J., dissenting). And this Court has uniformly
rejected challenges to tariffs imposed by Presidents under those
statutory authorities. <em>E</em>.<em>g</em>., <em>Federal Energy
Administration</em> v. <em>Algonquin SNG, Inc</em>., <a class=3D"related-ca=
se" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/426/548/">426 U.S.
548</a>, 558=E2=80=93560 (1976); <em>J. W. Hampton, Jr.,</em> <em>&amp;
Co.</em> v. <em>United States</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https=
://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/276/394/">276 U.S.
394</a>, 409 (1928); <em>Marshall Field</em>, 143 U.&nbsp;S., at
690=E2=80=93694; <em>Cargo of Brig Aurora</em> v. <em>United States</em>, 7
Cranch 382, 386=E2=80=93388 (1813).<p></p>

															As Professors Bradley and Goldsmith well
summarized, there is a =E2=80=9Csettled practice of about a century of the
executive branch exercising emergency powers in many important
contexts pursuant to the broadly worded IEEPA and its predecessor,
the Trading with the Enemy Act. And there is an even longer
practice, dating to the Founding, of presidents exercising
trade-related sanctions authority pursuant to broadly worded
statutes. Notably, the Court has already suggested in both of these
contexts that one should expect Congress to, in effect, paint with
a broad brush.=E2=80=9D 172 U. Pa. L. Rev., at 1796=E2=80=931797.<p></p>

															As with tariffs on foreign imports historically,
the IEEPA tariffs on foreign imports at issue in this case
implicate foreign affairs. According to the Government, the
President has leveraged the IEEPA tariffs into trade deals with
major trading partners including China, the United Kingdom, and
Japan, among other countries. The Government says that the tariffs
have helped make certain foreign markets more accessible to
American businesses and have contributed to trade deals with
foreign nations worth trillions of dollars.<p></p>

															Moreover, consistent with history and the
traditional uses of tariffs, the President =E2=80=9Cis exercising his IEEPA
authority in connection with highly sensitive negotiations he is
conducting to end the conflict between the Russian Federation and
Ukraine.=E2=80=9D Decl. of M. Rubio in No. 25=E2=80=931812 (CA Fed., Aug. 2=
9,
2025), p. 3. To that end, on August 6, 2025, the President imposed
tariffs on India for =E2=80=9Cdirectly or indirectly importing Russian
Federation oil.=E2=80=9D Exec. Order No. 14329, 90 Fed. Reg. 38701 (2025).
And on February 6, 2026, the President reduced the tariffs on India
because, according to the Government, India had =E2=80=9Ccommitted to stop
directly or indirectly importing Russian Federation oil.=E2=80=9D Exec.
Order No. 14384, 91 Fed. Reg. 6501 (2026).<p></p>

															To be sure, most foreign affairs and national
security actions=E2=80=94whether war, international agreements, trade
deals, or tariffs=E2=80=94lead to significant domestic ramifications within
the United States. And this case is no exception. Nonetheless, in
the foreign affairs field, courts interpret statutes as written,
with appropriate respect to Congress and the President and without
a major questions doctrine weight on the scale against the
President. See <em>Youngstown</em>, 343 U.&nbsp;S., at 636, n. 2
(Jackson, J., concurring).<p></p>

															Lest there be any remaining doubt that the major
questions doctrine does not apply to tariffs on foreign imports,
recall again this Court=E2=80=99s decision in <em>Algonquin</em>. That case
involved significant tariffs imposed by President Ford on oil
imports. The relevant statute granted the President the authority
to =E2=80=9Cadjust the imports.=E2=80=9D 19 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71862(b=
) (1970 ed.).
The Court upheld the tariffs by interpreting the statute as
written. Neither the major questions doctrine=E2=80=94nor anything
resembling that doctrine=E2=80=94played a role in that case.<p></p>

															In short, =E2=80=9CPresidential actions pursuant to
broad congressional authorizations related to foreign affairs often
have long historical pedigrees that can in various ways inform
congressional intent to approve the actions in question. To the
extent that this is so in particular instances, <em>the major
questions doctrine=E2=80=99s clear authorization requirement does not
apply</em>.=E2=80=9D Bradley &amp; Goldsmith, 172 U. Pa. L. Rev., at 1794
(emphasis added).<p></p>

															So it is here: Presidents =E2=80=9Chave long been
granted substantial discretion over tariffs.=E2=80=9D <em>Id.</em>, at 1759=
,
n. 90. This Court has never before applied the major questions
doctrine to a statute authorizing the President to take action with
respect to foreign affairs in general or tariffs in particular. And
it should not do so today.<p></p>

															The Chief Justice=E2=80=99s opinion=E2=80=99s reliance on th=
e
major questions doctrine in this foreign affairs case is a first=E2=80=94a
novel and unprecedented use of the major questions doctrine to
invalidate Presidential action taken pursuant to congressional
authorization in the foreign affairs area. I firmly disagree with
that use of the major questions doctrine here. In the foreign
affairs context, including tariffs, the longstanding rule is
simple: Interpret the statute as written, not with a thumb on the
scale against the President.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/fe=
deral/us/607/24-1287/#F24" name=3D"T24" id=3D"T24">24</a>]<p></p>

															3<p></p>

															Related precedent further demonstrates that
the major questions doctrine has not traditionally applied in the
national security or foreign policy contexts. Consider two
prominent examples.<p></p>

															<em>First</em>, in <em>Hamdi</em> v.
<em>Rumsfeld</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia=
.com/cases/federal/us/542/507/">542 U.S.
507</a>, this Court considered the 2001 Authorization for Use of
Military Force, which Congress passed and President George W. Bush
signed on September 18, 2001, in the wake of the al Qaeda attacks
on the United States. The law broadly empowered the President to
use =E2=80=9Call necessary and appropriate force against those nations,
organizations, or persons he determines planned, authorized,
committed, or aided the terrorist attacks=E2=80=9D that occurred on
September 11, 2001. Authorization for Use of Military Force,
115Stat. 224 (Sept. 18, 2001).<p></p>

															In <em>Hamdi</em>, the Government militarily
detained in the United States an American citizen who had taken up
arms with the Taliban. 542 U.&nbsp;S., at 510=E2=80=93511. The plaintiff
Hamdi argued, among other things, that the AUMF generally
authorized the use of force but did not specifically authorize
military detention, at least detention of American-citizen enemy
combatants in the United States. See <em>id</em>., at 515=E2=80=93517. He
contended that his military detention was therefore illegal.<p></p>

															In the principal opinion by Justice O=E2=80=99Connor,
the Court rejected Hamdi=E2=80=99s statutory argument, explaining that it
was =E2=80=9Cof no moment that the AUMF does not use specific language of
detention.=E2=80=9D <em>Id</em>., at 519. Rather, because =E2=80=9Cdetentio=
n to
prevent a combatant=E2=80=99s return to the battlefield is a fundamental
incident of waging war, in permitting the use of =E2=80=98necessary and
appropriate force,=E2=80=99 Congress has clearly and unmistakably
authorized detention in the narrow circumstances considered here.=E2=80=9D
<em>Ibid</em>.<p></p>

															Consider the similarities between <em>Hamdi</em>
and this case. Both involve major questions of foreign affairs.
<em>Hamdi</em> involved U.&nbsp;S. military detention of an American
citizen in America, pursuant to a generally worded authorization
for use of military force. This case involves tariffs on foreign
goods imported into America pursuant to a generally worded
authorization to regulate importation. Detention is a traditional
incident of the President=E2=80=99s delegated power to wage war. See
<em>id</em>., at 518. Tariffs are a traditional incident of the
President=E2=80=99s delegated power to regulate imports and foreign
commerce. In <em>Hamdi</em>, the Court said that as a matter of
history, practice, and precedent, the AUMF=E2=80=99s general authorization
for the use of military force clearly encompassed detention of
enemy combatants. <em>Id</em>., at 518=E2=80=93522. Here, as a matter of
history, practice, and precedent, IEEPA=E2=80=99s general authorization for
regulation of importation likewise clearly encompasses tariffs on
foreign imports.<p></p>

															<em>Second</em>, in 1981 in <em>Dames &amp;
Moore</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/ca=
ses/federal/us/453/654/">453 U.S.
654</a>, the Court did not apply the major questions doctrine, even
though the Court had recently applied that principle in a
significant domestic policy case. Cf. <em>Industrial Union Dept.,
AFL=E2=80=93CIO</em> v. <em>American Petroleum Institute</em>, <a class=3D"=
related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/448/607/"=
>448 U.S.
607</a> (1980) (plurality opinion).<p></p>

															The <em>Dames &amp; Moore</em> case arose in the
wake of the Iran hostage crisis where Iran held more than 50
