in this
area=E2=80=9D=E2=80=94combined with Congress=E2=80=99s longstanding acquies=
cence to the
President=E2=80=99s practice of settling claims=E2=80=94supported the Presi=
dent=E2=80=99s
suspension of those claims. <em>Id</em>., at 678. Congress=E2=80=99s =E2=80=
=9Cgeneral
tenor=E2=80=9D and acquiescence are of course far less than the =E2=80=9Ccl=
ear
congressional authorization=E2=80=9D that The Chief Justice=E2=80=99s opini=
on today
newly demands for the President=E2=80=99s tariffs.<p></p>

															Again, consider the similarities between
<em>Dames &amp; Moore</em> and this case. <em>Dames &amp; Moore</em>
involved complicated questions of foreign policy and national
security. The statutes in <em>Dames &amp; Moore</em> were generally
worded and did not specifically authorize suspension of claims. But
Presidents had historically exercised a similar power. See
<em>id</em>., at 677=E2=80=93682. Here, we likewise have a generally worded
statutory authorization to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation.=
=E2=80=9D
And Presidents have historically imposed tariffs.<p></p>

															If IEEPA permitted the President to lawfully
suspend claims in <em>Dames &amp; Moore</em>=E2=80=94despite the Court=E2=
=80=99s
transparent acknowledgment that the actual statutory text did not
clearly authorize the President=E2=80=99s actions=E2=80=94then surely IEEPA=
=E2=80=99s
authorization to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation=E2=80=9D eas=
ily
justifies these tariffs.<p></p>

															The Chief Justice=E2=80=99s opinion would chart a new
course for the major questions doctrine, extending it for the first
time deep into the foreign affairs sphere. If the Court had applied
the major questions doctrine in <em>Hamdi</em> and <em>Dames &amp;
Moore</em>, those two landmark cases almost certainly would have
been decided differently. So today=E2=80=99s opinion marks a significant
change. Will the Court apply the major questions doctrine in the
foreign affairs context again in the future? Or is this a ticket
good for one day and one train only? Time will tell. But in the
meantime, the decision could engender significant uncertainty over
the Executive=E2=80=99s exercise of statutory authority in the foreign
affairs realm.<p></p>

															As the <em>Hamdi</em> and <em>Dames &amp; Moore</em>
examples demonstrate, applying the major questions doctrine in the
foreign policy and national security contexts in the past would
have seriously hindered the President=E2=80=99s ability to exercise power
granted by Congress to achieve important foreign policy and
national security objectives for America. And if applied in the
foreign affairs context in the future, it could impair Presidents=E2=80=99
vital statutory authorities with respect to foreign policy and
national security.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/6=
07/24-1287/#F25" name=3D"T25" id=3D"T25">25</a>]<p></p>

															*=E2=80=83=E2=80=83*=E2=80=83=E2=80=83*<p></p>

															Having said all of that on foreign affairs, I
reiterate that the major questions doctrine=E2=80=94even if it applies in
this foreign affairs context=E2=80=94does not defeat major executive
actions that are <em>clearly authorized</em> by Congress. See Bradley
&amp; Goldsmith, 172 U. Pa. L. Rev., at 1790=E2=80=931791. And as explained
in Part III=E2=80=93A above, in IEEPA Congress clearly authorized the
President to impose tariffs to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.
importation=E2=80=9D in national emergencies. In other words, even if the
major questions doctrine applies in the foreign affairs context
exactly as it does in domestic affairs, the President should still
prevail in this case.<p></p>

															IV<p></p>

															Finally, no Member of the Court today relies
on the nondelegation doctrine. But the plaintiffs briefly raise
such an argument, and I will therefore briefly address it. The
argument is unavailing for many of the reasons already noted in the
major questions analysis above. This Court has repeatedly rejected
constitutional challenges to congressional delegations to the
President in the foreign affairs area, including delegations of
tariff authority.<p></p>

															For matters of foreign affairs and national
security, the Court has traditionally recognized that Congress
=E2=80=9Cmust of necessity paint with a brush broader than that it
customarily wields in domestic areas.=E2=80=9D <em>Zemel</em> v. <em>Rusk</=
em>,
<a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/=
us/381/1/">381 U.S.
1</a>, 17 (1965). And to reiterate, numerous statutes
=E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98authorizing action by the President in respect of s=
ubjects
affecting foreign relations=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=
=98either leave the
exercise of the power to his unrestricted judgment, or provide a
standard far more general than that which has always been
considered requisite with regard to domestic affairs.=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=
=9D
<em>Department of Transportation</em> v. <em>Association of American
Railroads</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.co=
m/cases/federal/us/575/43/">575 U.S.
43</a>, 80, n.&nbsp;5 (2015) (Thomas, J., concurring in judgment)
(quoting <em>United States</em> v. <em>Curtiss-Wright Export
Corp.</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/ca=
ses/federal/us/299/304/">299 U.S.
304</a>, 324 (1936)). Therefore, as Justice Thomas has explained,
the Court=E2=80=99s precedents establish that =E2=80=9Cthe Constitution gra=
nts the
President a greater measure of discretion in the realm of foreign
relations.=E2=80=9D <em>Association of American Railroads</em>, 575
U.&nbsp;S., at 80, n.&nbsp;5; see <em>Curtiss-Wright Export
Corp.</em>, 299 U.&nbsp;S., at 319=E2=80=93322; <em>Panama Refining Co.</em=
>
v. <em>Ryan</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.=
com/cases/federal/us/293/388/">293 U.S.
388</a>, 422 (1935).<p></p>

															Justice Robert Jackson likewise noted the
=E2=80=9C&nbsp;=E2=80=98unwisdom of requiring Congress in this field of
governmental power to lay down narrowly definite standards by which
the President is to be governed.=E2=80=99&nbsp;=E2=80=9D <em>Youngstown She=
et &amp;
Tube Co.</em> v. <em>Sawyer</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https:/=
/supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/343/579/">343 U.S.
579</a>, 636, n.&nbsp;2 (1952) (concurring opinion) (quoting
<em>Curtiss-Wright</em>, 299 U.&nbsp;S., at 321=E2=80=93322). As such, the
=E2=80=9Cstrict limitation upon congressional delegations of power to the
President over internal affairs does not apply with respect to
delegations of power in external affairs.=E2=80=9D <em>Youngstown</em>, 343
U.&nbsp;S., at 636, n.&nbsp;2 (concurring opinion).<p></p>

															Because statutes that =E2=80=9Cinvolv[e] the external
relations of the United States=E2=80=9D do not trigger the same kind of
delegation concerns as purely domestic ones, <em>Association of
American Railroads</em>, 575 U.&nbsp;S., at 80 (opinion of Thomas,
J.), the Court has regularly upheld delegations of power to the
President in the national security and foreign policy realms. See,
<em>e.g.</em>, <em>Curtiss-Wright</em>, 299 U.&nbsp;S., at 319=E2=80=93322;
<em>Loving</em> v. <em>United States</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=
=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/517/748/">517
U.S. 748</a>, 771=E2=80=93774 (1996). Indeed, if a strict nondelegation
doctrine applied in those areas, numerous statutes=E2=80=94including many
authorizations for use of military force in the Nation=E2=80=99s
history=E2=80=94would have been unconstitutional delegations of authority
to the President. See Authorization for Use of Military Force,
115Stat. 224 (Sept. 18, 2001) (=E2=80=9C[T]he President is authorized to
use all necessary and appropriate force against those nations,
organizations, or persons he determines planned, authorized,
committed, or aided the terrorist attacks that occurred on
September 11, 2001=E2=80=9D).<p></p>

															As to tariffs in particular: Broad delegations
of tariff authority to the President have been in the heartland of
permissible delegations upheld by this Court. Congress may, without
running afoul of the Constitution, =E2=80=9Cinvest the President with large
discretion in matters arising out of the execution of statutes
relating to trade and commerce with other nations.=E2=80=9D <em>Marshall
Field &amp; Co.</em> v. <em>Clark</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"h=
ttps://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/143/649/">143 U.S.
649</a>, 691 (1892). Congressional delegations of tariffs and other
foreign trade authorities to the President date back to near the
Founding. And this Court has uniformly rejected nondelegation
challenges to statutes delegating that authority to the President.
<em>E.g.</em>, <em>Federal Energy Administration</em> v. <em>Algonquin
SNG, Inc.</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.co=
m/cases/federal/us/426/548/">426 U.S.
548</a>, 558=E2=80=93560 (1976); <em>J. W. Hampton, Jr., &amp; Co.</em> v.
<em>United States</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.j=
ustia.com/cases/federal/us/276/394/">276 U.S.
394</a>, 409 (1928); <em>Marshall Field</em>, 143 U.&nbsp;S., at
690=E2=80=93694; <em>Cargo of Brig Aurora</em> v. <em>United States</em>, 7
Cranch 382, 386=E2=80=93388 (1813).<p></p>

															This Court=E2=80=99s decision in <em>Algonquin</em> is
again instructive. There, the Court held that Section 232 did not
constitute an unconstitutional delegation. 426 U.&nbsp;S., at
558=E2=80=93560. The Court found it sufficient that the President could act
=E2=80=9Conly=E2=80=9D to the extent =E2=80=9Che deems necessary to adjust =
the imports=E2=80=9D of
an article such that it =E2=80=9Cwill not threaten to impair the national
security.=E2=80=9D <em>Id</em>., at 559 (quotation marks omitted).<p></p>

															To be clear, I am not suggesting that there is
no nondelegation doctrine in the foreign affairs realm. But the
Court has consistently recognized that the doctrine affords more
flexibility to Congress and the President in that area to deal with
the complex foreign relations issues and national security threats
facing America. See <em>Association of American Railroads</em>, 575
U.&nbsp;S., at 80, n.&nbsp;5 (opinion of Thomas, J.);
<em>Youngstown</em>, 343 U.&nbsp;S., at 636, n.&nbsp;2 (Jackson, J.,
concurring); <em>Curtiss-Wright</em>, 299 U.&nbsp;S., at 319=E2=80=93322;
<em>Panama Refining</em>, 293 U.&nbsp;S., at 422.<p></p>

															In all events, for purposes of this Court=E2=80=99s
nondelegation precedents, IEEPA sufficiently constrains the
President=E2=80=99s authority to declare an emergency and impose tariffs.
See <em>J. W. Hampton</em>, 276 U.&nbsp;S., at 409; <em>FCC</em> v.
<em>Consumers=E2=80=99 Research</em>, 606 U.S. 656, 673=E2=80=93675, 681=E2=
=80=93691 (2025).
The President may exercise the authorities in IEEPA =E2=80=9Conly=E2=80=9D =
=E2=80=9Cto deal
with an unusual and extraordinary threat=E2=80=9D that =E2=80=9Chas its sou=
rce in
whole or substantial part outside the United States=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Cw=
ith
respect to which a national emergency has been declared.=E2=80=9D 50
U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A71701. Congress placed numerous limits on IEEPA,
including a default 1-year time limit, an enumerated list of
exceptions, and comprehensive congressional reporting requirements.
See =C2=A7=C2=A71622(d), 1702(b), 1703.<p></p>

															It is also useful to underscore the
extraordinary nature of the plaintiffs=E2=80=99 nondelegation argument
here. The plaintiffs=E2=80=99 submission would mean that these tariffs
would be unlawful <em>even if</em> IEEPA explicitly authorized
tariffs. Unlike their statutory and major questions doctrine
arguments, their nondelegation argument is <em>not</em> based on a
lack of an explicit reference to =E2=80=9Ctariffs=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cduti=
es=E2=80=9D or the like.
Their nondelegation argument instead goes much further and would
require very specific congressional directions to the President on
when and under what circumstances he could impose tariffs and how
high those tariffs could be. The plaintiffs=E2=80=99 theory would have
dramatic consequences and likely wipe out many of the existing
tariff statutes that have long been upheld by this Court, as well
as TWEA. And if the tariff authority here is unlawful, so too are
most if not all IEEPA authorities such as asset freezes, embargoes,
and quotas. And it would not stop there. The plaintiffs=E2=80=99
nondelegation theory would threaten various other national security
and foreign affairs statutes that similarly grant substantial
discretion to the President. The Court today thankfully does not go
down that road.[<a href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/=
24-1287/#F26" name=3D"T26" id=3D"T26">26</a>]<p></p>

															V<p></p>

															The overarching theme of the Court=E2=80=99s opinion
is that tariffs are not a clear means to =E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.
importation=E2=80=9D and that Congress was therefore required to use the
word =E2=80=9Ctariff,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cduty,=E2=80=9D or the like in IEEPA=
 in 1977 if it wanted to
authorize tariffs on foreign imports. But that conclusion
contravenes text, history, and precedent. To summarize:
<em>Algonquin</em> in 1976 unanimously held the opposite. The Nixon
and Ford tariffs were based on statutory provisions that did not
use the word =E2=80=9Ctariff&nbsp;=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cduty.=E2=80=9D Ther=
e is a long tradition of
Presidents imposing tariffs as a means of regulating importation
and commerce. The predecessor Trading with the Enemy Act has long
been understood to authorize tariffs during wartime as a means to
=E2=80=9Cregulate .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. importation,=E2=80=9D even though it does=
 not use
the word =E2=80=9Ctariff&nbsp;=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cduty.=E2=80=9D The hist=
ory of the Polk,
Lincoln, and McKinley tariffs shows that tariffs are a means of
regulating importation. Marshall, Story, and Madison stated that
tariffs are a means of regulating foreign commerce. The dictionary
definitions and ordinary usage establish that tariffs are a means
of regulating importation.<p></p>

															All of that and much more, in my view,
overwhelmingly establish that IEEPA clearly authorizes the
President to impose tariffs.<p></p>

															That said, with respect to tariffs in
particular, the Court=E2=80=99s decision might not prevent Presidents from
imposing most if not all of these same sorts of tariffs under other
statutory authorities. For example, Section 122 of the Trade Act of
1974 permits the President to impose a =E2=80=9Ctemporary import surcharge=
=E2=80=9D
to =E2=80=9Cdeal with large and serious United States balance-of-payments
deficits.=E2=80=9D 19 U.&nbsp;S.&nbsp;C. =C2=A72132(a). Section 201 of the =
Trade
Act of 1974 provides that, if the International Trade Commission
determines an article is being imported in such quantities that it
is =E2=80=9Ca substantial cause of serious injury, or the threat thereof,
to the domestic industry producing an article like or directly
competitive with the imported article,=E2=80=9D the President may take
=E2=80=9Cappropriate and feasible action,=E2=80=9D including imposing a =E2=
=80=9Cduty.=E2=80=9D
=C2=A7=C2=A72251(a), 2253(a)(3)(A). Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974
authorizes the President through a subordinate officer to =E2=80=9Cimpose
duties=E2=80=9D if he determines that =E2=80=9Can act, policy, or practice =
of a
foreign country=E2=80=9D is =E2=80=9Cunjustifiable and burdens or restricts=
 United
States commerce.=E2=80=9D =C2=A7=C2=A72411(a)=E2=80=93(c). Section 338 of t=
he Tariff Act of
1930 permits the President to impose tariffs when he finds that
=E2=80=9Cany foreign country places any burden or disadvantage upon the
commerce of the United States.=E2=80=9D =C2=A71338(d). And Section 232 of t=
he
Trade Expansion Act of 1962 authorizes the President to, after
receiving a report from the Secretary of Commerce, =E2=80=9Cadjust the
imports of [an] article and its derivatives so that such imports
will not threaten to impair the national security.=E2=80=9D
=C2=A71862(c)(1)(a).<p></p>

															So the Court=E2=80=99s decision is not likely to greatly
restrict Presidential tariff authority going forward. But the
Court=E2=80=99s decision is likely to generate other serious practical
consequences in the near term. One issue will be refunds. Refunds
of billions of dollars would have significant consequences for the
U.&nbsp;S. Treasury. The Court says nothing today about whether,
and if so how, the Government should go about returning the
billions of dollars that it has collected from importers. But that
process is likely to be a =E2=80=9Cmess,=E2=80=9D as was acknowledged at or=
al
argument. Tr. of Oral Arg. 153=E2=80=93155. A second issue is the
decision=E2=80=99s effect on the current trade deals. Because IEEPA tariffs
have helped facilitate trade deals worth trillions of
dollars=E2=80=94including with foreign nations from China to the United
Kingdom to Japan, the Court=E2=80=99s decision could generate uncertainty
regarding various trade agreements. That process, too, could be
difficult.<p></p>

															*=E2=80=83=E2=80=83*=E2=80=83=E2=80=83*<p></p>

															The tariffs at issue here may or may not be
wise policy. But as a matter of text, history, and precedent, they
are clearly lawful. I respectfully dissent.<p></p>
